WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.849
Andrew Huberman: [INTRO THEME MUSIC]
Welcome to the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:05.560
where w e discuss science and
science-based tools for everyday life.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:13.189
I'm Andrew Huberman, and I'm a professor
of Neurobiology and Ophthalmology

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:14.799
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:16.800
Today, my guest is Dr.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.700
Maya Shankar.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:18.530
Dr.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:21.680
Maya Shankar is a cognitive scientist
who did her undergraduate training

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:27.380
at Yale University, her PhD thesis as
a Rhodes Scholar, and a postdoctoral

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.459
fellowship also in Cognitive
Science at Stanford University.

00:00:30.799 --> 00:00:31.079
Dr.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.509
Shankar also served as a senior
advisor to the White House, and she

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:38.370
founded and served as the Chair of the
White House Behavioral Science team.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:39.000
Dr.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.980
Shankar is also the host of her
own podcast entitled A Slight

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.240
Change of Plans . And indeed, Dr.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.929
Shankar herself is no stranger to
having to make major changes to one's

00:00:47.929 --> 00:00:50.190
life plans, as you'll learn today.

00:00:50.589 --> 00:00:53.290
Prior to all of those incredible
accomplishments that Dr.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:57.519
Shankar has achieved, she was a
student at the Juilliard Conservatory

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.530
of Music, preparing her life to
become a professional concert

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:06.039
violinist, but as you'll also soon
learn, she then experienced a career

00:01:06.039 --> 00:01:11.209
devastating injury, forcing herself
to have to reframe everything about

00:01:11.209 --> 00:01:13.330
her life plans and her own identity.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.460
And that's really what
we talk about today.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.090
We talk about identity.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:18.180
Not just Dr.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:21.979
Shankar's prior and current identities,
but, of course, your identity.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.899
We pose a number of questions
geared toward getting you

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.800
to ask, who am I really?

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.209
Do my goals align with
who I am and what I want?

00:01:30.529 --> 00:01:30.829
Dr.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:35.260
Shankar shares with us the research
on identity, goals, motivation, and

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:39.540
plans, as well as many practical
tools to answer those key questions

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.450
that guide us down either the correct
or incorrect trajectories in life.

00:01:43.539 --> 00:01:47.709
She shares with us, for instance,
how to assess on paper goals of the

00:01:47.709 --> 00:01:48.750
sort that you would see on a CV.

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:54.050
So, which school, which job, which
salary, which spouse, etc., etc.,

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:59.000
and how to relate those to the deeper
feelings that relate to one's ability

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.640
to continually pursue a given goal,
knowing that it's the right goal for us.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.880
We also talk about the science of
feelings, what they can and cannot

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:13.810
tell us and when they should or should
not serve as a compass for guiding our

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.160
everyday and longer term decisions.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.290
By the end of today's episode,
you will realize that Dr.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:24.450
Shankar is essentially handing you a
science supported roadmap for how to

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.330
determine and assess your identity and
goals and how one influences the other.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.790
That is, how your identity influences
your goals and how your goals

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:36.739
influence your identity in becoming
the person that you want to be.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.459
Before we begin, I'd like to emphasize
that this podcast is separate from my

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:42.109
teaching and research roles at Stanford.

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:45.890
It is, however, part of my desire and
effort to bring zero cost to consumer

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:49.420
information about science and science
related tools to the general public.

