00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:13.849
.
 And now for my discussion with Dr.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:14.719
Maya Shankar.

00:05:15.099 --> 00:05:15.589
Welcome.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:16.659
I'm so happy you're here.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:17.460
Maya Shankar: Thanks, Andrew.

00:05:17.509 --> 00:05:18.340
It's great to be here.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:23.210
Andrew Huberman: I have a lot of
questions about identity, about

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:27.980
goals and motivation, and about
change in general, but I'd like to

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:33.320
start off with identity, and I'd
like to divide it into two segments.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:39.259
The first is how we form an identity,
and we'll get into your story in,

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.010
I hope, a bit or more of detail,
but when we're younger, we tend to

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:48.890
ask questions about ourselves, but
also about the world around us.

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:54.059
We want to learn what our parents
do for a living, what the workers on

00:05:54.059 --> 00:05:56.189
the street are doing that for, etc.

00:05:57.299 --> 00:06:02.789
How much of our early identity do
you think is formed by observation of

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:07.840
what we are doing versus observation
and labels of the people that

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.750
are around us and closest to us?

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:11.280
Maya Shankar: Yeah, it's a great question.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:16.209
I think a lot of it is based on what
we see around us, and what we see is

00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:19.219
deemed successful and society privileges.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:23.750
And there's a concept called identity
foreclosure, where actually, when

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:27.310
you're young, it's not just that you're
observing what your parents are doing

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:31.710
or what your peer group is doing, they
impose their own structures on you.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:36.059
And so what that can do is it can really
limit your mindset in terms of what it

00:06:36.059 --> 00:06:39.830
is that you want to achieve and what
it is that you're capable of achieving.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:44.150
And so oftentimes when people experience
identity foreclosure, they have to

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:48.970
take a lot of active steps to overcome
whatever biases or limitations they

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:54.640
experienced as a young person, given what
they were projected to do or believe.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:57.140
So, identity, it can be about what you do.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:00.420
It can also be about what you believe
in the world, and so a lot of those

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.910
belief systems are also passed down.

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:06.169
You inherit belief systems from the
people that surround you when you're

00:07:06.170 --> 00:07:11.159
young, and if there's one thing that
I've learned, it's that we tend to put

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:14.039
a huge premium on what it is that we do.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.260
We tend to define ourselves by what we do.

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:20.360
And you can see this in the
questions we ask young children.

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:21.680
What do you want to be when you grow up?

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:24.480
We never say, who do you
want to be when you grow up?

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:25.969
What kind of person do you
want to be when you grow up?

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:27.219
We say, what do you want to be?

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:31.450
And the consequence of that kind
of mindset is that we end up

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:34.890
anchoring our identities very
firmly to what it is that we do.

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:38.570
You were alluding to my
personal story, right?

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:42.869
I started playing the violin when
I was a little kid, six years old,

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:48.029
became absolutely obsessed, and for
the large part of my childhood, I

00:07:48.029 --> 00:07:50.669
was first and foremost a violinist.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.540
I mean, if I had met you, I'd be
like, hey, Andrew, I'm a violinist.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:55.130
And then the second up would be, I'm Maya.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:59.580
That's how tethered my identity
was to being a violinist.

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:02.680
And then fast forward
to when I'm a teenager.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:07.790
I have these huge dreams of
going pro and becoming just,

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:10.880
like, hopefully, a professional
violinist for the rest of my life.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:13.290
And then I tear a tendon in my hand.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:21.289
My dreams end overnight, and suddenly
there's this profound loss of identity,

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:26.150
because what I hadn't realized is that
in losing the violin, sure, I was losing

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:29.220
the ability to play the instrument, but
I was actually losing a huge part of

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:34.150
who I was, and that was so destabilizing
and so disorienting for me, because when

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:37.700
you define yourself by the what, then
as soon as the what goes away, you're

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.529
like, oh, my gosh, who the hell am I?

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:40.449
Andrew Huberman: What do I do?

00:08:40.859 --> 00:08:42.460
What value do I bring to the world?

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.240
And what I experienced at the
time is known in cognitive

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.840
science as identity paralysis.

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:55.019
Maybe you felt this way during various
transitions in your life, but basically,

00:08:56.179 --> 00:08:58.590
who you are and what you're about
is suddenly called into question,

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:00.869
and you end up feeling really stuck.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:07.550
You don't have the courage to imagine
what a future could look like, and I

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:10.170
certainly fell prey to identity paralysis.

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:14.359
And it took me a long time to kind of
figure out what my path would look like

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:20.600
moving forward, but I learned a really
valuable lesson from that very formative

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:24.500
experience I had with change about
how it is that I should define myself.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:28.400
And for what it's worth, I don't
think our desire as humans to

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.110
have identities is going anywhere.

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:32.930
We're not going to be able to dispose
of identities, and we shouldn't, because

00:09:33.309 --> 00:09:36.975
our self-identities bring us so much
meaning and purpose in our lives.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:38.160
Maya Shankar: You're a podcaster.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:38.650
I'm a podcaster.

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:39.670
You're a scientist.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:40.310
I'm a scientist.

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:42.220
These things are actually
really helpful and motivating.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:45.890
So we don't want to do away with
identities altogether, but what

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:51.199
we can be more particular about is
what we anchor our identities to.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:56.790
And I have learned in my adult life
to anchor my identity to why I do the

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:58.750
things I do rather than what I do.

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:03.660
And I found this to be a much more
durable, reliable relationship.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.630
So to make this concrete,
let's think about the violin.

00:10:07.969 --> 00:10:09.220
Sure, I loved playing.

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:10.560
I loved how music sounded.

00:10:10.639 --> 00:10:11.989
I loved the way the violin felt.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:16.700
But when I stripped away all the
superficial features of the violin,

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.080
what I really, really loved and
was so drawn to as a young child

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:26.380
was the emotional connection that
I could form through my music.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:29.689
So that might have been with
my orchestra mates, my chamber

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:35.300
musician friends, playing solo and
performing in front of an audience.

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:39.069
And ideally, we all feel something
new that we haven't felt before.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:44.089
It's kind of an intoxicating feeling when
you're little, to have the ability to

00:10:44.090 --> 00:10:46.500
inspire new feelings in people, right?

00:10:46.809 --> 00:10:53.350
And I was so drawn to human connection,
and when I realized that human

00:10:53.350 --> 00:10:56.780
connection was at the heart of what
it is that drives me as a person.

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:59.830
Like, what lights me up every single
day is a desire to connect with

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:03.879
others, to understand other people,
to understand their psychology, to

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:05.189
understand how their minds work.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:10.070
Then when the violin was taken away from
me, even in terms of the narrative I

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:14.950
tell myself about my life, I could still
find that same core underlying feature

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.860
elsewhere, and I have been able to.

00:11:17.099 --> 00:11:20.870
I found it as an academic, as a
cognitive scientist who studies the

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:22.760
science of connection and emotion.

00:11:23.619 --> 00:11:27.840
I've seen that connection play out
in the work that I did in public

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:29.220
policy when I was at the White House.

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:32.490
Obviously, with my podcast, A
Slight Change of Plans . You're

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:34.780
forming these intimate
connections with people every day.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:40.409
And so even though it feels in my life
like I've done such disparate things,

00:11:40.409 --> 00:11:45.599
there actually is a powerful through
line that connects all of them, and that

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:47.510
is my desire to connect emotionally.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:51.590
And so what I would recommend to
people who are listening, especially

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:54.300
if they're in the throes of change,
and they're feeling destabilized

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:56.059
by that threat to identity.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:00.539
That loss of identity is to try to
figure out what their through line is.

00:12:00.939 --> 00:12:03.280
What are the underlying features
of the things that you used to

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:04.740
do that you absolutely loved?

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:07.699
And can you find the
expression of that elsewhere?

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:11.560
Andrew Huberman: I love that,
and I have so many questions.

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:16.349
The first one relates back to
childhood identities and how we

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:20.370
often can project onto children
what they are likely to become.

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:22.620
I see that as mostly benevolent.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:29.950
You observe a child playing with
trucks in the sandbox, and we say, oh,

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:31.850
they're going to become a contractor.

00:12:32.599 --> 00:12:38.560
We tend to project roles that are fairly
high up within the occupation hierarchy.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:44.720
Like any parents, you wish for the
best possible life for your kids.

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:50.360
But I can see the perils of doing
that if then the kid starts to

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:53.979
think, well, that's what I'm bound
to become, because it is restrictive.

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:59.750
I also am fascinated by the fact that
when we are adolescents and teens,

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:03.860
there's a tendency to ask questions
about identity, like, who am I?

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:09.390
I don't know many 40 year
olds that say, who am I?

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:14.450
At one's core, one's essence, and we might
change careers, change relationships,

00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:16.820
change geographies, all sorts of things.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:21.790
But there must be something going
on in the brain in those adolescent

00:13:21.809 --> 00:13:27.009
and teen years that forces this
question of self, of who am I?

00:13:27.259 --> 00:13:31.660
And teenagers are notorious for trying
on different uniforms, different

00:13:31.670 --> 00:13:35.700
friend groups, different behaviors, as
a way to sort that out, sometimes in

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:39.260
ways that support them and sometimes
in ways that act as pitfalls.

00:13:39.639 --> 00:13:45.620
So I'm curious about what's known about
how we develop our own identity from the

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:48.499
inside out as well as from the outside in.

00:13:48.559 --> 00:13:51.410
Maya Shankar: Yeah, no, that's
really interesting, and it's also

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:52.640
something I'm very curious about.

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:56.630
I mean, we know from neuroscience
research that there are significant

00:13:56.630 --> 00:14:00.600
changes that the brain undergoes
during puberty and other periods of

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:05.900
adolescence, and the primary change that
we see is a desire for independence.

00:14:06.289 --> 00:14:11.320
And so one reason why we see teenagers
grappling with this question of who I am

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:15.980
is that they're actually breaking from
these structures that they grew up around.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:20.310
The imposed structures, t he identity
foreclosure that they might have

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.389
experienced and are starting to
figure out for the first time, or

00:14:23.389 --> 00:14:26.970
wanting to ask the question for the
first time, who do I want to be?

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:31.140
What do I want to do outside of
the systems that I've grown up in?

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:35.479
And I think this is one of the
primary reasons why we find that

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:39.469
during teenage years, this sort of
question is asked more commonly.

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:47.090
I think that one challenge that we
can face, because you said this one

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:49.750
word that really caught my attention,
which was, what's my essence?

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:54.520
And one of the things I studied as a
cognitive scientist, is the psychology

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:55.730
of what's called essentialism.

00:14:55.740 --> 00:15:00.770
So our underlying belief is that there
are essential qualities to people

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:05.050
that are immutable, and there's lots
of studies with young children and

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:08.270
adults showing that we really believe
that people do have these essences.

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:13.410
And it's unclear what that even
means in a metaphysical sense, I

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:14.610
don't know what that would even mean.

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:20.370
I think that the challenge in believing
that we have essences is that it

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:24.940
leads us to believe that there are
these truly immutable states about

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:29.949
ourselves that we're incapable of
changing, and I think this can give

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:32.950
rise to feelings of shame, for example.

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:33.919
So what is shame?

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:37.400
Shame is not the feeling,
oh, I did something bad.

00:15:37.470 --> 00:15:39.420
Shame is the feeling, I am bad.

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:42.800
It's not that I lost at something,
I failed at something, it's

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:45.190
that I'm a loser, I'm a failure.

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:50.060
And so the problem when we try to figure
out the essence piece is that it doesn't

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:54.400
give you the kind of malleable way of
thinking that actually there might not

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:57.640
be something that's so defining about
you that you're incapable of changing.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:01.849
As humans, maybe all we are are
collections of behaviors and thoughts.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:04.540
And there's nothing more to it than that.

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:08.720
And I find that way of thinking a bit
more freeing when it comes to who we

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:13.099
are, because I think it allows us to
cultivate more of a growth mindset.

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:16.090
I think it prevents us from
engaging in these very harmful

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:19.360
self-narratives that a lot of people
tend to have about themselves.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:22.780
Probably a lot of people listening
to your podcast are self-critical.

00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:24.650
I'm a very self-critical person.

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:26.840
We listen to this because
we want to improve.

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:29.410
I'm a fan of your show because I want
to be better and I want to improve.

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:32.620
But that also is often accompanied
by a lot of self-berating

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:33.970
and questioning of self.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:38.229
And so, yeah, I think I've just
tried to have a slightly more

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:43.140
capacious understanding of who I
am and also recognizing that there

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:46.890
might not really be these essential
features that are immutable.

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:51.170
I don't know if you resonate
with this notion of the desire

00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:53.000
to feel that we have essences?

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:56.869
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, I used the word
essence without thinking too carefully

00:16:56.870 --> 00:17:02.989
about exactly what I meant, but what I
was trying to say when I said essence

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:11.770
is as a child, I did certain things,
and I enjoyed some of them, didn't enjoy

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:13.250
others, and I really disliked others.

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:17.750
A very famous neuroscientist who's at
Caltech named Marcus Meister, people

00:17:17.759 --> 00:17:21.309
literally refer to him as the great
Marcus Meister, once said, and I

00:17:21.509 --> 00:17:25.539
totally subscribe to the fact, that
neural circuits in the brain basically

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:29.430
divide our sensory experience along
the dimensions of yum yuck and meh.

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:34.990
There's not a lot of in between, because
the circuits ultimately have to drive

00:17:35.139 --> 00:17:42.069
either forward movement toward more
repetitive behaviors, as in nerdspeak, or

00:17:42.069 --> 00:17:44.149
aversive leaning out, I don't want that.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:47.270
Or just kind of a neutral
response, a yum yuck and meh

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:52.500
seems to be the trinary response.

00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:58.639
And there is this component of childhood,
I think, where we are foraging naturally,

00:17:58.639 --> 00:18:04.360
using our senses, experiencing yum,
yucks, and mehs and hearing yum,

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:05.950
yucks and mehs from our parents.

00:18:05.950 --> 00:18:07.950
That's good, that's bad, that's whatever.

00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:08.610
It's neutral.

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:14.420
But at some point, I certainly have had
the experience and I've observed others,

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:19.499
I think having the experience of feeling
something that's on a different dimension

00:18:19.499 --> 00:18:24.640
entirely, which is this notion of delight,
which is that it sort of fills your body

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:31.859
with a sense of so much yum that it gives
you energy to do so much more of it in a

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:34.860
way that is almost on a different plane.

00:18:35.059 --> 00:18:37.870
And I'm not trying to be spiritual
or metaphysical about it, but

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:39.370
it feels distinctly different.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:44.389
And I don't know what it represents, but
I think that's that piece that perhaps,

00:18:44.389 --> 00:18:47.919
even as a scientist, I don't really
need to assign a neural circuit to.

00:18:49.449 --> 00:18:50.830
Maya Shankar: Sure, d o you
think what you're describing

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:52.489
in part is the feeling of awe?

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:56.489
Like, when you talk about delight, do
you think part of it is a feeling of awe?

00:18:56.669 --> 00:18:56.820
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:00.790
Like the first time I went to New York
City as a six year old kid, I remember

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:03.820
thinking, and I still feel every time I'm
there, I can't believe this place exists.

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:06.590
It's like a human tropical reef.

00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:08.349
Like everywhere you look, there's life.

00:19:08.349 --> 00:19:10.400
So that was awe and delight.

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:13.613
Although I saw some things, this was
New York in the 70s, there were some

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:15.530
things like Times Square in the 70s.

00:19:15.530 --> 00:19:19.490
If anyone's seen that show, The Deuces
, it looked like that, especially as a

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:20.920
young kid, it was kind of aversive.

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:21.630
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:26.100
Andrew Huberman: So it wasn't always
awed, but the delight for me was in

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:29.230
learning, and certain animals and
certain things for you as the violin.

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:30.379
And I want to make sure that I--

00:19:30.390 --> 00:19:32.205
Maya Shankar: --And awe, by the
way, I mean, it can be aversive.

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:35.480
So awe isn't necessarily, I think,
in the western world, we think of

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:39.620
awe-inspiring experiences as having a
positive emotional valence, but they can

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:41.080
also have a negative emotional valence.

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:46.450
So, the two criteria for satisfying
an awe-inspiring experience, and a

00:19:46.450 --> 00:19:49.469
lot of this work comes from Dacher
Keltner, professor at Berkeley,

00:19:50.119 --> 00:19:53.870
is one, there should be some
element of perceived vastness.

00:19:55.330 --> 00:19:56.500
This is all reference dependent.

00:19:56.500 --> 00:20:01.699
So it's all based on your own frame of
mind, but there's this sense of mystery

00:20:01.719 --> 00:20:06.599
and wonder at just how vast either the
physical apparatus is, like Times Square.

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:11.350
It's this massive set of buildings,
and it kind of overwhelms your

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:14.919
senses because of all the lights and
sounds that are hitting your visual

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:16.340
system and your auditory system.

00:20:18.349 --> 00:20:20.410
There's also conceptual vastness.

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:24.590
So we can feel awe when we
feel the delight of a new

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:26.140
scientific discovery, right?

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:30.659
Or in my case, like for the first time
reading a book about how the mind works.

00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:33.840
I just remember marveling
at this organ and just being

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:36.110
completely in awe of how it works.

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:39.620
And then the second criteria for an
awe-inspiring experience, which I

00:20:39.639 --> 00:20:43.460
think might have been met as well
when you were in New York, is what's

00:20:43.460 --> 00:20:44.920
called a need for accommodation.

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:49.000
So it's just a fancy way of saying that we
have a certain mental model of the world.

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:52.980
And typically in the presence of
awe, we need to assimilate this new

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:56.570
information with our existing model
because it challenges it in some way.

00:20:57.120 --> 00:21:02.449
And it actually leads us to have
more open minds because we realize,

00:21:02.470 --> 00:21:06.129
wait a second, I have this existing
vision of what the world is like and

00:21:06.130 --> 00:21:09.050
now I'm experiencing this new thing
and I need to kind of make it work.

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:11.760
I need to integrate it with my
existing understanding of the world

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:13.279
and that's the mind blowing part of it.

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:18.750
But I absolutely remember my childhood
experience kind of mirroring your

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:23.500
experience in New York, I was twelve
years old or maybe eleven years old.

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:25.410
I was at a summer music camp.

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:26.760
It was late at night.

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:30.929
I had my Discman, which is how we
listened to things back in the day.

00:21:30.929 --> 00:21:33.710
I recall I had a CD in there.

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:37.970
It was the Beethoven Violin
Concerto by Anne Sophie Mutter.

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:44.090
I was so young, Andrew, so I still
don't know how to use words to describe

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:49.110
how it is that I felt something that
was so powerful and so transcendent.

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:52.730
But I remember listening to the
first movement of this violin

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:55.959
concerto and it consumed me.

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.629
I mean, I felt chills
up and down my spine.

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:01.340
My heart would race along with the melody.

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:03.860
It felt otherworldly, right?

00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:05.894
And I think that was kind of what
you're getting at before where it's

00:22:05.969 --> 00:22:08.820
like it's this altered state of mind.

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:13.389
And the language I've used since
to code that experience is that

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:15.840
it was an awe-inspiring experience
because I think both things happened.

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:20.520
I was impressed by the
vastness of the experience.

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:23.809
It also sent me through time
in this interesting way, back

00:22:23.809 --> 00:22:25.979
to the time of Beethoven.

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:29.849
So the vastness can exist along a
temporal horizon and then the need for

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:33.660
accommodation, which, you know, I didn't
study cognitive science at this point.

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:39.320
So I remember thinking I cannot believe a
collection of musical notes arranged just

00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:44.050
so can make me feel this way and that if
you were to tweak it just slightly, just

00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:49.169
like, take the E flat and move it down the
stream a little bit, emotional resonance

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:50.710
is completely gone from the passage.

00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:55.010
And there was just something so simple
and magical about that realization.

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:58.479
So, anyway, I resonate with
this kind of delight and awe

00:22:58.639 --> 00:22:59.860
experience that you describe.

00:23:00.139 --> 00:23:02.289
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, I'm
so glad you described it.

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:08.700
That, you know, this isn't a discussion
about my experience, but for me, I realize

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:11.140
now that New York was awe-inspiring.

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:17.780
Prior to that, the only thing similar
was discovering animal specialization,

00:23:17.780 --> 00:23:20.580
something I'm still fascinated by,
the sensory systems of animals and

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:23.859
how they experience the world and
how humans experience the world.

00:23:24.039 --> 00:23:27.240
And then ultimately, it was, well,
then I went into skateboarding

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:30.360
and that whole landscape, and
then eventually into neuroscience.

00:23:30.970 --> 00:23:34.920
The difference between the New York
experience of awe, and I do think that

00:23:34.940 --> 00:23:40.840
that's what it was, and biology, animals,
and eventually neuroscience, is that,

00:23:41.279 --> 00:23:45.780
like your experience with music and
realizing that the movement of a note

00:23:45.810 --> 00:23:50.270
could change something fundamentally
when it came to learning about biology

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:55.289
and neuroscience, I felt not just
awe, but a sense of delight in that.

00:23:55.920 --> 00:23:57.639
I felt there was a place for me there.

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:03.480
And what came out of what you just
described really resonated in terms of

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:07.070
this moving of a note, because it took
something from a passive experience, I

00:24:07.070 --> 00:24:11.719
believe, of that's this incredible thing
over there, like New York City, was awe.

00:24:11.719 --> 00:24:17.519
But I didn't see myself having any
kind of verb state within it that would

00:24:17.529 --> 00:24:22.210
change it or alter how it is, or for me.

00:24:22.410 --> 00:24:25.550
Whereas with music, for you or, I think
neuroscience, when I realized that you

00:24:25.550 --> 00:24:29.870
could do experiments, you could actually
do some sort of manipulation, and

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:33.929
through that, hopefully unveil something
fundamental about how the brain works,

00:24:34.279 --> 00:24:35.859
I thought, there's a place for me here.

00:24:36.330 --> 00:24:40.310
And so I think there's something
about the experience of something

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:44.879
just from a raw sensory perspective,
music or animals or neuroscience

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:46.180
in the examples we're using here.

00:24:46.570 --> 00:24:49.470
But then realizing that there's
a verb state of self like that,

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:53.789
I could enact something within it
that could give me more of that.

00:24:54.270 --> 00:24:57.480
Whereas I think when, as a young kid
in New York City, I just didn't feel

00:24:57.480 --> 00:25:00.463
any way that I could plug into it
except in a passive way, because it's

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:03.800
the difference between a kid who, and
this wouldn't have been me who sees a

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:07.369
game of soccer or football or baseball
or watches the Olympics and goes, that

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:12.860
is amazing, and the kid that says, I'm
going to go do that, in fact, I could

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:17.169
do that, and I could maybe do that
even better, or even half as well.

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:21.610
And so the delight, I think,
is in the possibility of

00:25:21.800 --> 00:25:23.899
engagement, and I'm fascinated.

00:25:24.099 --> 00:25:26.750
A friend of mine who's a trauma
therapist, he's not a neuroscientist.

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:30.449
He always says nouns are just
very slow verbs, but verbs are

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:33.540
far more exciting because they
create this anticipatory activity.

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:34.459
Anyway--

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:39.120
Maya Shankar: --Before you move on
from that, I love that you said that,

00:25:39.120 --> 00:25:41.459
because you're helping me realize
something really important about

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:43.909
how I saw my role as a violinist.

00:25:44.139 --> 00:25:49.069
I'm never going to modify the notes on
the page, because obviously, I'm going

00:25:49.069 --> 00:25:51.110
to be faithful to what Beethoven wrote.

00:25:51.170 --> 00:25:52.640
Andrew Huberman: This is what
made you a great musician.

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:54.730
And me, by the way, I
was a failed violinist.

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:58.040
They pulled me out of it because
t he neighbor's dogs howled.

00:25:59.179 --> 00:26:00.230
I was in the Suzuki method.

00:26:00.559 --> 00:26:04.930
I was so terrible at it that they
literally made me stop playing music

00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:06.840
just to protect the neighborhood.

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:07.860
Maya Shankar: That's adorable.

00:26:07.890 --> 00:26:09.669
And we'll talk about
the science of quitting.

00:26:09.670 --> 00:26:11.810
Maybe later, but that was
a great choice for you.

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:20.020
But what I'm realizing is that there
was that element of defining self

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:22.000
through the pursuit of the instrument.

00:26:22.039 --> 00:26:28.020
And I saw a place for myself exactly
like you did, where I thought, I

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:29.820
decide how this phrase unfolds.

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:32.400
I decide how much vibrato I use.

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:36.179
I decide exactly what the angling
of my bow is and the cadence

00:26:36.179 --> 00:26:39.839
and the pacing and the emotion
that I bring to the experience.

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:45.260
And when you see a place for yourself,
that takes an awe-inspiring experience.

00:26:45.290 --> 00:26:50.659
And then, actually, there's a translation
process where you become something bigger

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:52.050
than what you thought you could be.

00:26:52.050 --> 00:26:54.430
And actually, it's so interesting you
mentioned this, Andrew, because I've

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:59.110
been chatting recently with a guy named
Reginald Dwayne Betts, and he spent

00:26:59.110 --> 00:27:03.590
nine years in prison, and he's now
an internationally renowned scholar.

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:09.940
So he committed a carjacking when
he was 15 years old and then went

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:11.790
to an adult prison for nine years.

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:13.420
Andrew Huberman: As a 15 year old?

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:16.200
Maya Shankar: He just turned 16
by the time he got his sentence.

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:17.500
Yeah, it was totally wild.

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:17.900
Andrew Huberman: Brutal.

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:23.280
Maya Shankar: And he actually talks
about the fact that there was this

00:27:23.310 --> 00:27:30.370
underground library in the prison system,
and he didn't know what he could be in

00:27:30.370 --> 00:27:35.579
the prison, what identity he could take
on when everyone seemed to be defined

00:27:35.590 --> 00:27:37.170
by what crime they had committed.

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:42.570
It felt like his imagination
was so limited to the talk

00:27:42.570 --> 00:27:43.720
about identity paralysis.

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:47.540
I mean, you're denied all your basic
freedoms in this environment, right?

00:27:47.540 --> 00:27:50.180
So you really don't even have the ability
to imagine what more you could be.

00:27:50.180 --> 00:27:55.640
So one day he gets a book called The
Black Poets . And in the book, he

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:59.320
read a poem by Etheridge Knight, who
had also spent time in prison and had

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:02.890
written this incredibly stirring poem
about the criminal justice system.

00:28:03.559 --> 00:28:11.069
And he goes by Dwayne, but what Dwayne
shared with me is he said, I was

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:13.230
awe inspired by what I was reading.

00:28:14.010 --> 00:28:16.590
But the most important thing that
happened in reading that book and

00:28:16.690 --> 00:28:20.939
understanding the author's history
is that it gave me something to be.

00:28:21.559 --> 00:28:24.379
I saw a place for myself in this world.

00:28:24.929 --> 00:28:28.509
And he was so prolific.

00:28:28.529 --> 00:28:32.210
He wrote like a thousand poems
in the year after he stumbled

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:36.010
upon this book, and he ended up
winning the MacArthur Genius Award.

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:37.070
He went to Yale Law School.

00:28:37.070 --> 00:28:39.040
I mean, he's just crushed it ever since.

00:28:39.750 --> 00:28:42.030
But I think you've stumbled upon
a really important point, which is

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:46.199
there's a fascinating science of awe
and all the benefits it can confer

00:28:46.199 --> 00:28:50.480
to our well being, but it can also
serve as an entry point to helping to

00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:52.449
define our identities in new places.

00:28:52.450 --> 00:28:53.370
And I just love that.

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:55.210
I think that's a wonderful
way to think about it.

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:56.380
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

00:28:56.380 --> 00:29:03.410
When we see ourselves entering
the sphere of experience that is

00:29:03.410 --> 00:29:09.090
evoking awe, I do think something
about it converts to this delight.

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:12.330
Although I have to acknowledge that
language is insufficient to describe

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:14.570
a lot of what we're referring to.

00:29:14.570 --> 00:29:14.840
Right.

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:22.769
Even the most reductionist language
of biology can't grab the higher

00:29:22.770 --> 00:29:25.450
order emotions and complexity.

00:29:25.460 --> 00:29:26.360
Not yet, anyway.

00:29:26.360 --> 00:29:28.090
We just don't have a language for it.

00:29:29.080 --> 00:29:33.230
I'd like to talk more about the violin,
not just because I failed miserably at the

00:29:33.230 --> 00:29:36.860
violin, but actually, I figured out pretty
early on I wasn't going to be a musician.

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:40.110
I still have absolutely
no ability to read music.

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:44.070
I can memorize lyrics very easily,
and I love music, and I love classical

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:49.040
music as well as other forms of
music, but zero musical talent.

00:29:49.420 --> 00:29:53.969
You, on the other hand,
got quite good at violin.

00:29:54.259 --> 00:29:58.450
It was interesting for me to learn that
the violin was a bit of a rebellious

00:29:58.450 --> 00:30:01.280
choice for you, given your family history.

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:06.879
And you and I do both share this
fairly unusual fact that both of our

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:09.140
fathers are theoretical physicists.

00:30:09.309 --> 00:30:12.330
So did you feel pressured to be
a scientist or something else?

00:30:12.349 --> 00:30:18.310
And being a musician, was that initially
looked at as a route to poverty or a

00:30:18.310 --> 00:30:22.780
bad choice, or were your parents a bit
more cautious, like, oh, okay, that's

00:30:22.780 --> 00:30:26.280
great, but maybe make that a supplement
to your other studies and pursuits.

00:30:26.530 --> 00:30:26.770
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

00:30:26.770 --> 00:30:30.220
So I'm the youngest of four kids,
and kind of stereotypically, my

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:33.050
three older siblings were total
math wizzes, they were, you know,

00:30:33.389 --> 00:30:37.340
taking the SAT when they were very
young because they were so talented.

00:30:38.490 --> 00:30:42.170
But I think one antagonist to some of
those cultural forces is that my mom,

00:30:42.170 --> 00:30:46.449
when she had grown up in India, had
felt very stifled by her environment.

00:30:46.460 --> 00:30:49.840
Like, as a young woman who was
very capable and very smart, I

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:51.209
mean, she majored in physics.

00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:57.180
She was mostly kept to the
spaces of domestic chores,

00:30:57.480 --> 00:30:59.080
occasional singing lessons.

00:30:59.090 --> 00:31:03.080
But mostly her job was like, do your
homework and then help with cooking.

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:04.390
Right, and cleaning and whatnot.

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:10.289
And so when she moved to this
country with my dad in the 1970s,

00:31:10.299 --> 00:31:12.839
she was actually very excited.

00:31:12.850 --> 00:31:14.290
She was 21 years old, by the way.

00:31:14.290 --> 00:31:18.610
So, long story short, she had met my dad
20 days prior to their getting married.

00:31:18.610 --> 00:31:19.690
So it was an arranged meeting.

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:26.280
And my dad is doing his postdoc at Harvard
in physics at the Society of Fellows.

00:31:26.320 --> 00:31:30.439
And my mom just joins him after
a winter break in the dorm.

00:31:30.440 --> 00:31:32.250
And everyone's like, hey,
man, how was your break?

00:31:32.250 --> 00:31:35.480
And there's like, I went snowboarding
and I went, whatever, to Tahoe.

00:31:35.480 --> 00:31:36.810
And my dad's like, I got married.

00:31:37.649 --> 00:31:43.010
And so this new couple arrives, and
my mom was so lonely in this country.

00:31:43.010 --> 00:31:46.029
I mean, this was before you
could text your parents overseas

00:31:46.029 --> 00:31:47.009
or use a WhatsApp group.

00:31:47.359 --> 00:31:50.010
So she could only handwrite
letters to her family back home.

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:52.480
And her goal was, you know what?

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:55.180
I'm going to create a little army
around me in the form of children.

00:31:55.200 --> 00:32:00.580
So she had four kids, and she
was absolutely intent on exposing

00:32:00.580 --> 00:32:03.589
us to as many extracurricular
activities as she could.

00:32:03.589 --> 00:32:06.810
So I have two older brothers and I have
an older sister, especially her girls.

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:09.770
She said, you can do whatever you want.

00:32:09.820 --> 00:32:13.540
I'm going to give you lay the land
when you're young, but when you find

00:32:13.540 --> 00:32:15.750
something that you're passionate
about, I really want to give you

00:32:15.750 --> 00:32:17.110
the opportunity to explore it.

00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:21.339
So I think I really benefited from the
fact that she had been denied that kind

00:32:21.339 --> 00:32:25.950
of exposure and the ability to pursue
her dreams, artistic or otherwise.

00:32:25.970 --> 00:32:29.389
And so she was really hell bent on
making sure that we kids were able to.

00:32:30.350 --> 00:32:33.020
My older three siblings played
musical instruments, so, like

00:32:33.020 --> 00:32:34.169
clarinet, trumpet, flute.

00:32:34.520 --> 00:32:39.070
I think they were surprised by
my affinity for it because when

00:32:39.070 --> 00:32:42.080
I was six, my mom brought down my
grandmother's violin from the attic.

00:32:42.109 --> 00:32:44.790
So my grandmother had played
Indian classical music.

00:32:44.790 --> 00:32:46.830
So that's where you're sitting
cross legged on the floor and

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:48.110
your violins facing the ground.

00:32:48.260 --> 00:32:50.320
It's a very different style of music.

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:53.560
But as, like, a parting gift, my
grandmother had given it to my mom and

00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:55.010
said, hey, bring this with you to the US.

00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:59.670
So she opened the instrument that day,
and I just instantly fell in love with it.

00:33:00.049 --> 00:33:04.990
And I asked very quickly for a
quarter-size violin of my own.

00:33:05.370 --> 00:33:10.450
And while my parents had to nudge me
to do all sorts of things, they really

00:33:10.450 --> 00:33:13.830
never had to push me to practice,
which felt extraordinary at the time.

00:33:13.840 --> 00:33:17.300
Like, okay, clearly the violin is
something that Maya has intrinsic

00:33:17.300 --> 00:33:20.880
motivation for, because how is
it that we're not asking her to

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:22.220
have to practice all the time?

00:33:23.530 --> 00:33:26.740
Similar to you, Andrew, I
never to this day, I have a

00:33:26.740 --> 00:33:28.230
really hard time reading music.

00:33:29.070 --> 00:33:31.090
I never, I was a terrible sight reader.

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:34.889
I couldn't, if you put a piece of music in
front of me, I would not be able to tell

00:33:34.889 --> 00:33:36.320
you probably what it would sound like.

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:38.690
Today, I learn entirely by ear.

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:41.979
So I started with the Suzuki method,
which, as you know, is entirely by ear.

00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:49.179
And then I had an extremely kind,
awesome, but very inexperienced teacher.

00:33:49.179 --> 00:33:50.140
I was his first student.

00:33:50.670 --> 00:33:53.969
My mom went backstage at a symphony
concert in New Haven, which is where I

00:33:53.969 --> 00:33:57.870
grew up, and just asked the concertmaster,
like, hey, will you teach my daughter?

00:33:57.870 --> 00:33:58.940
And he's like, sure.

00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:01.270
Never taught anyone before,
but I'll give this a go.

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:03.430
And so we just made
things up along the way.

00:34:03.469 --> 00:34:07.149
I mean, he would play stuff and I would
mimic it, and I would let my emotions

00:34:07.150 --> 00:34:10.060
and whatever innate musicality guide me.

00:34:10.840 --> 00:34:14.970
And eventually, I think what that did
actually is really interesting, from a

00:34:14.970 --> 00:34:20.299
skill building perspective my technique
absolutely suffered in the long term from

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:24.610
not having a more structured approach,
but I was able to fall in love with

00:34:24.610 --> 00:34:28.820
this endeavor much more quickly than
other kids who had drill sergeants that

00:34:28.820 --> 00:34:32.630
were forcing them to practice their
scales every day and practice etudes.

00:34:32.630 --> 00:34:34.960
I mean, that stuff is so boring, right?

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:37.140
And when you're a little kid, you
just want to bang your head against

00:34:37.140 --> 00:34:41.020
the wall when you're put up against
that, when there's so many barriers

00:34:41.040 --> 00:34:44.639
to actually enjoying the fun parts,
which are actually playing the pieces.

00:34:44.650 --> 00:34:47.940
So the one kind of fun aside about
my musical journey is I got to jump

00:34:47.940 --> 00:34:51.210
straight to the fun stuff, and I
think that helped me cultivate a much

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:53.420
more natural love of the instrument.

