00:35:58.770 --> 00:36:00.810
.
 The intrinsic motivation part is so key.

00:36:00.810 --> 00:36:03.690
I've talked a few times before
on the podcast about this.

00:36:03.690 --> 00:36:07.390
I think of a now famous study that
was done at Bing Nursery School at

00:36:07.390 --> 00:36:10.799
Stanford, where they observed what kids
did during free time, and then they

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:14.029
rewarded them or didn't reward them,
and then they later removed the rewards.

00:36:14.029 --> 00:36:17.849
And the essential takeaway is that
receiving rewards for something that

00:36:18.250 --> 00:36:23.200
a child was initially intrinsically
motivated to do undermines some

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:24.480
of that intrinsic motivation.

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:28.690
So I have to wonder whether or not the
fact that your parents neither encouraged

00:36:28.700 --> 00:36:32.620
nor discouraged your violin playing
might have allowed you to fully express

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:38.040
and lean into your intrinsic motivation,
as opposed to, for instance in my case,

00:36:39.880 --> 00:36:43.620
we are distantly related, not closely
related, but there is a great violinist

00:36:43.620 --> 00:36:47.489
by the name of Bronislav Huberman, who
has a street named after him in Israel.

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:50.039
There's a famous picture of him and
Einstein playing violin together.

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:52.429
And I was told about that early on.

00:36:52.459 --> 00:36:57.160
And when I failed to play well
after a couple of practices, I was

00:36:57.160 --> 00:37:00.279
convinced that there was no way I was
going to live up to it, and I quit.

00:37:00.340 --> 00:37:01.910
Maya Shankar: That's a high bar.

00:37:02.160 --> 00:37:02.950
Andrew Huberman: It's a high bar.

00:37:02.950 --> 00:37:04.309
Maya Shankar: I didn't have
any such role models that I was

00:37:04.309 --> 00:37:04.690
trying to be like in my family.

00:37:04.690 --> 00:37:04.745
[LAUGHS]

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:07.610
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, it
turns out, but, exactly!

00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:13.950
And so I think that there's actually more
opportunity in kids leaning into, and

00:37:13.950 --> 00:37:18.730
adults probably leaning into the sensory
experience of what they're doing and not

00:37:18.890 --> 00:37:20.629
putting that up against some benchmark.

00:37:21.080 --> 00:37:24.570
I worry about that today so much with
social media and with video games,

00:37:24.920 --> 00:37:28.319
where in a video game we're on social
media, you can see something being

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:33.400
done at the very highest level, often
by someone quite young or early in

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:35.840
their career, to the point where it
can be a little bit overwhelming.

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:40.420
And I think then we start measuring
ourselves against metrics that

00:37:40.420 --> 00:37:42.560
are not about the experience.

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:48.199
That said, your parents, whatever
they did worked out well enough that

00:37:48.199 --> 00:37:49.930
you became very proficient, right?

00:37:49.930 --> 00:37:52.990
You succeeded in getting into
Juilliard, which, at least from my

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:58.890
understanding, is the most competitive
music preparatory, is that how you refer

00:37:58.890 --> 00:38:01.990
to it, that one can possibly go to?

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:08.400
And so at that point, had your identity
merged with the behavior, and were

00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:12.220
you still enjoying yourself up until
the point where you had this injury?

00:38:12.220 --> 00:38:13.300
That we'll also talk about.

00:38:13.610 --> 00:38:18.170
Maya Shankar: Yeah, I was still enjoying
myself around the time when I auditioned

00:38:18.179 --> 00:38:21.090
for Juilliard, in particular, because
of exactly what you said, which was

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:24.700
that everything was kind of beating
my expectations and my parents'

00:38:24.700 --> 00:38:26.220
expectations up until this point.

00:38:26.520 --> 00:38:29.410
Which is that we didn't really
have any, and so it all just

00:38:29.410 --> 00:38:31.370
felt like icing on the cake.

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:31.900
Wow!

00:38:31.900 --> 00:38:33.710
Our kids found something
that they really love.

00:38:34.809 --> 00:38:35.719
This is great, right?

00:38:35.719 --> 00:38:38.853
It can sometimes take you years,
decades, to figure out what it is that

00:38:38.929 --> 00:38:40.180
you love, what you're passionate about.

00:38:40.180 --> 00:38:42.803
And I think we go through this
renewal process often in our lives.

00:38:42.803 --> 00:38:45.340
I've had to have moments in life
where I'm like, what do I like again?

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:46.250
What do I love again?

00:38:46.250 --> 00:38:51.650
And so it's not also a one time
experience, but there was a thrilling

00:38:51.650 --> 00:38:54.060
aspect to my musical life when I was
young, which was, again, everything

00:38:54.060 --> 00:38:55.250
kind of felt like, it's like, a bonus.

00:38:56.220 --> 00:38:59.210
So one story I love sharing
is about how I even got into

00:38:59.210 --> 00:39:00.329
Juilliard in the first place.

00:39:00.340 --> 00:39:06.210
So my dad's a theoretical
physicist, as you mentioned.

00:39:06.430 --> 00:39:09.370
My mom helps immigrants get green
cards to study in this country.

00:39:09.790 --> 00:39:14.229
Neither of them had exposure
to the classical music sphere.

00:39:14.559 --> 00:39:16.250
So they're, like, the
opposite of tiger parents.

00:39:16.279 --> 00:39:18.570
Like, even if they wanted to be tiger
parents, they wouldn't know how to

00:39:18.580 --> 00:39:21.890
be tiger parents in this domain,
because they lack the connections

00:39:22.520 --> 00:39:26.549
and the wherewithal to figure out
what it would mean to go pro and to

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:27.920
access the best teachers or whatever.

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:32.740
So my mom, who is a very fearless
person by nature, she knew that

00:39:32.740 --> 00:39:37.720
at some point, my passion for the
violin was surpassing her ability to

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:39.730
connect me with the right resources.

00:39:39.820 --> 00:39:45.250
And so one weekend, we were in New
York, awe-inspiring New York, and I

00:39:45.260 --> 00:39:49.639
had my violin with me because I had
another audition, and we were just

00:39:49.639 --> 00:39:52.040
walking by Juilliard, the building.

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:55.569
And my mom was just eager for me to
see it from the outside because it's

00:39:55.570 --> 00:39:57.110
just really cool as a kid, right?

00:39:57.110 --> 00:39:59.210
It's like, all your musical
idols went to this place.

00:39:59.210 --> 00:40:02.673
I just wanted to see it and imagine what
it would have been like for Perlman to

00:40:02.840 --> 00:40:06.819
go in and out and Midori to go in and
out, Yo Yo Ma, li ke, it's so exciting.

00:40:07.440 --> 00:40:11.880
And as we're passing the entrance,
my mom looks at me and says,

00:40:12.920 --> 00:40:14.120
hey, why don't we just go in?

00:40:14.810 --> 00:40:16.290
And I was like, what
are you talking about?

00:40:16.920 --> 00:40:17.995
She's like, let's just go in.

00:40:18.260 --> 00:40:19.660
What's the worst thing that can happen?

00:40:19.930 --> 00:40:23.780
And I'm like, security guards and a lot
of other terrible things, mom, right?

00:40:24.370 --> 00:40:28.330
But I had a useful enthusiasm that
propelled me into the building that day.

00:40:29.299 --> 00:40:32.960
She strikes up a conversation with
a fellow student, and her mom finds

00:40:32.960 --> 00:40:37.160
out that she's studying with a top
teacher at Juilliard, asks if we can

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:39.060
get an introduction within an hour.

00:40:39.080 --> 00:40:41.259
I'm auditioning for this
teacher on the spot.

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:41.780
Right.

00:40:42.130 --> 00:40:43.680
No idea that this was going to happen.

00:40:43.690 --> 00:40:44.260
Andrew Huberman: Wild!

00:40:44.750 --> 00:40:50.340
Maya Shankar: Yeah, he tells me
he has what I refer to as a muted

00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:51.790
enthusiasm about my playing.

00:40:51.800 --> 00:40:54.690
Doesn't think I'm great, but, sees
something, he told me later he

00:40:54.690 --> 00:40:56.520
liked my personality, my enthusiasm.

00:40:56.520 --> 00:40:59.319
So I got the personality card
coming out of that music audition.

00:40:59.490 --> 00:40:59.899
Great.

00:41:00.860 --> 00:41:04.490
And what he did is he
said, look, I'm with you.

00:41:04.520 --> 00:41:05.609
I don't think that you're ready.

00:41:05.859 --> 00:41:08.080
You would not get into Juilliard
if you auditioned today.

00:41:08.860 --> 00:41:13.880
However, I take residence at a
summer music program in Colorado.

00:41:13.930 --> 00:41:17.680
If you come there for five weeks, we
can do an intense boot camp where I

00:41:17.680 --> 00:41:22.479
try to skill you up and get you to
learn your first scale and your first

00:41:22.479 --> 00:41:25.760
etude, which you will need to pass
the Juilliard audition and also maybe,

00:41:25.770 --> 00:41:28.850
hopefully get you to read music a little
bit better than you can right now.

00:41:29.490 --> 00:41:33.340
And I went to that summer
camp, and I worked my butt off.

00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:37.430
I mean, you're also in this incredibly
intensive environment where everyone your

00:41:37.450 --> 00:41:40.810
age is there and they're all practicing
like their age equivalent, right?

00:41:40.810 --> 00:41:42.870
And so I felt very inspired by that.

00:41:42.900 --> 00:41:45.880
And I ended up getting
into Juilliard in the fall.

00:41:45.960 --> 00:41:51.780
And it was such a wonderful reminder that
when opportunities are not served on a

00:41:51.780 --> 00:41:56.940
silver platter for you, you just have to
have this kind of imaginative courage.

00:41:56.940 --> 00:41:58.259
And what my mom had that day.

00:41:58.520 --> 00:42:02.730
To figure out a path from point A to
point B, she really just created a

00:42:02.730 --> 00:42:06.650
plate for me and said, like, okay,
you're prepared for this thing.

00:42:06.680 --> 00:42:08.080
We're going to get you
in front of this teacher.

00:42:08.400 --> 00:42:11.309
And that's a lesson I've
used time and time again.

00:42:11.590 --> 00:42:14.430
When I felt like there was
something cool I could be doing,

00:42:14.799 --> 00:42:16.300
the opportunity did not exist.

00:42:16.309 --> 00:42:20.330
So, for example, when I was in the
White House, the job that I wanted,

00:42:20.330 --> 00:42:23.830
which was to be a practitioner of
behavioral science, did not exist.

00:42:24.310 --> 00:42:28.460
And so I sent cold emails and I pitched
them on the idea of creating a new

00:42:28.469 --> 00:42:30.390
position for a behavioral science advisor.

00:42:30.679 --> 00:42:33.830
And then I said, hey, by the way, if
you create this position, could you also

00:42:33.830 --> 00:42:38.040
consider hiring me to play that job,
even though I've had no public policy

00:42:38.300 --> 00:42:42.440
experience and I've been an academic
for the entirety of my adult life?

00:42:42.760 --> 00:42:44.390
And they said yes.

00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:49.520
And so it was such an energizing
lesson to learn as a young kid, which

00:42:49.520 --> 00:42:51.530
is like, you can do the cold call.

00:42:52.240 --> 00:42:54.160
Oftentimes there's few consequences.

00:42:54.160 --> 00:42:55.120
You'll just get rejected.

00:42:55.130 --> 00:42:57.000
I mean, that's truly the worst
thing that's going to happen.

00:42:57.000 --> 00:42:59.500
But it's one thing to be told
that, it's another thing to have

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:04.670
lived the experience out and to see
how amazing the aftermath can be.

00:43:04.670 --> 00:43:06.950
And that's what I got to
experience as a young kid.

00:43:07.589 --> 00:43:08.259
Andrew Huberman: Amazing.

00:43:09.170 --> 00:43:13.070
So let's all express some thanks to
your mom for barging in the door,

00:43:13.790 --> 00:43:18.310
and to you, because you also had the
agency to do the audition on the spot.

00:43:18.310 --> 00:43:21.910
I think a lot of kids and adults
would have thought, I'm not ready, I'm

00:43:21.910 --> 00:43:27.560
not going to do this, but it takes a
certain gumption to just do it, right,

00:43:28.379 --> 00:43:30.679
and also to integrate the feedback.

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:32.419
And then I'm curious about this camp.

00:43:33.130 --> 00:43:37.400
I went to a few camps of different
types, crashed a few camps.

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:38.420
That's a different story.

00:43:38.470 --> 00:43:40.940
Turns out if you show up, you can
get by for a few days before they

00:43:40.940 --> 00:43:43.690
realize that you're not one of
the main, oh, yeah, no, there's a

00:43:43.690 --> 00:43:44.940
whole other set of stories there.

00:43:44.940 --> 00:43:45.340
Maya Shankar: I love it.

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:47.959
Andrew Huberman: But I'm curious.

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:53.950
You're among very driven,
maybe even obsessive kids.

00:43:54.890 --> 00:43:56.430
Were they nice to one another?

00:43:56.820 --> 00:43:59.029
Do you recall the kid that was the best?

00:43:59.399 --> 00:43:59.849
Maya Shankar: Oh, yeah.

00:43:59.990 --> 00:44:00.490
Rachel Lee.

00:44:00.559 --> 00:44:01.220
Andrew Huberman: There you go.

00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:02.129
Isn't this incredible?

00:44:02.160 --> 00:44:02.649
Maya Shankar: Oh, my God.

00:44:02.650 --> 00:44:03.600
Andrew Huberman: We remember these names.

00:44:03.660 --> 00:44:03.930
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:04.880
Total prodigy.

00:44:05.279 --> 00:44:10.380
I bristle when people say, Maya
was a young violin prodigy.

00:44:10.420 --> 00:44:11.879
I'm like, no, I wasn't.

00:44:11.960 --> 00:44:14.340
And there's no false
humility in my saying that.

00:44:14.639 --> 00:44:18.920
I just actually saw what prodigies
were like and I was not one of them.

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:22.609
I mean, truly, just talk
about awe-inspiring.

00:44:22.670 --> 00:44:26.450
I'm like, how is it that music
comes so effortlessly to Rachel?

00:44:26.920 --> 00:44:29.906
I feel like she was born
with a violin in her hands.

00:44:30.040 --> 00:44:32.150
I mean, that's how it felt
whenever I watched her play.

00:44:32.559 --> 00:44:35.939
And it's a double-edged sword.

00:44:36.179 --> 00:44:39.550
On the one hand, you're deriving
inspiration from the incredible

00:44:39.550 --> 00:44:40.680
talent you see around you.

00:44:41.109 --> 00:44:45.720
On the other hand, you feel demoralized
so often because you're running

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:49.170
up against whatever limitations
exist when it comes to your

00:44:49.170 --> 00:44:52.320
natural talent and your work ethic.

00:44:52.760 --> 00:44:56.040
At the end of the day, I was never
the hardest working violinist.

00:44:56.270 --> 00:44:59.319
My mom insisted that we
were well-rounded kids.

00:44:59.359 --> 00:45:01.430
I played soccer all
through elementary school.

00:45:01.670 --> 00:45:06.360
I auditioned for the school play,
Really Rosie , I did art classes.

00:45:06.810 --> 00:45:09.290
It was just really important
to both my parents.

00:45:09.290 --> 00:45:12.020
I think that we had just,
like, relatively normal lives.

00:45:12.530 --> 00:45:17.080
And I was studying alongside kids
who had literally left half their

00:45:17.080 --> 00:45:23.790
families behind in their home country,
had moved with one parent to a studio

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:28.310
apartment in Manhattan or in Colorado
for this camp, and were devoting

00:45:28.310 --> 00:45:30.549
their entire lives to this pursuit.

00:45:31.040 --> 00:45:35.599
And so I felt like, I
was a super envious kid.

00:45:35.680 --> 00:45:39.450
Like, I was always looking around, being
like, I suck, and they're great, right?

00:45:39.450 --> 00:45:42.149
We talked about having a
self-critical personality--

00:45:42.350 --> 00:45:44.759
Andrew Huberman: --I think
a lot of kids feel that way.

00:45:45.429 --> 00:45:48.980
I think at that age, and this
sometimes extends into adulthood,

00:45:50.190 --> 00:45:54.150
we have this tendency to try and
find benchmarks of where we are.

00:45:55.110 --> 00:45:59.339
And sometimes that turns
into a hierarchical thing,

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:00.590
sometimes very lateralized.

00:46:00.620 --> 00:46:03.405
But trying to figure out where you
are in the landscape of things, it

00:46:03.550 --> 00:46:07.020
just seems like it's fundamental
to the teenage experience.

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:09.419
Maya Shankar: Yeah, your
universe shrinks, too, right?

00:46:09.429 --> 00:46:13.470
So you're no longer getting access to what
the average kid violinist sounds like.

00:46:13.880 --> 00:46:15.399
I mean, you're in the elite of the elite.

00:46:15.410 --> 00:46:17.029
And so it's so intimidating.

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:22.360
And I felt like what happened
is, especially when I became a

00:46:22.360 --> 00:46:24.610
teenager, so two things happened
when I became a teenager.

00:46:24.630 --> 00:46:28.390
The first is that my violin life
just started to speed forward.

00:46:28.410 --> 00:46:33.669
So Itzhak Perlman invited me to be
his private violin student, considered

00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:35.070
the best violinist in the world.

00:46:35.080 --> 00:46:38.520
It was an incredible experience.

00:46:38.540 --> 00:46:40.879
I felt so overwhelmed,
even by the opportunity.

00:46:41.259 --> 00:46:44.540
I'd also stumbled upon MTV and was like,
do I even want to do classical music?

00:46:44.540 --> 00:46:46.490
Like, Britney Spears is
doing much cooler things?

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:49.259
So that was my version of teenage
rebellion, was coming home from

00:46:49.260 --> 00:46:51.839
school, and what I should have
been practicing, watching MTV.

00:46:52.740 --> 00:46:58.090
But the other thing that happened is I
went through the natural teenage process,

00:46:58.099 --> 00:47:00.520
which is I became very self-conscious.

00:47:01.759 --> 00:47:03.209
I became more insecure.

00:47:03.230 --> 00:47:05.549
I was trying to figure
out who I was, who I am.

00:47:06.029 --> 00:47:10.480
And I think that was the period of
my life, my high school years, when

00:47:10.480 --> 00:47:12.210
I was the least happy as a violinist.

00:47:12.240 --> 00:47:17.929
So I described to you earlier that
incredibly awe-inspiring experience of

00:47:17.929 --> 00:47:22.310
listening to the Beethoven Violin concerto
and it feeling otherworldly and feeling

00:47:22.320 --> 00:47:26.950
like I could see a world beyond my own
personal wants and needs and desires.

00:47:27.090 --> 00:47:30.670
It really made me feel small against
the backdrop of this magnificent world.

00:47:30.850 --> 00:47:32.540
And I liked that feeling of smallness.

00:47:33.130 --> 00:47:38.009
And when I was in my teenage
years, we're all in this highly

00:47:38.009 --> 00:47:39.459
narcissistic state of mind.

00:47:39.809 --> 00:47:43.040
We're consumed with
ourselves and how we feel.

00:47:43.430 --> 00:47:47.830
And I just felt like I gave some of my
worst performances when I was a teenager.

00:47:47.840 --> 00:47:52.779
And I often found, to your point, about
these pressure cooker environments.

00:47:53.170 --> 00:47:55.740
My best performances were
actually just to the public.

00:47:56.080 --> 00:47:59.219
My worst performances were
when I was in my little studio,

00:47:59.240 --> 00:48:00.490
having to play for my peers.

00:48:01.240 --> 00:48:04.720
That just sapped all the joy
out for me because I was just,

00:48:04.720 --> 00:48:05.839
like, really tough on myself.

00:48:06.769 --> 00:48:10.400
And that was a period of time
where I lost touch with what

00:48:10.400 --> 00:48:12.070
it is that I loved about music.

00:48:12.520 --> 00:48:15.130
And of course, there's an ebb and flow.

00:48:15.179 --> 00:48:18.390
I had magical experiences playing the
violin when I was a high schooler.

00:48:18.400 --> 00:48:23.230
But I just think if you were to do the
average of joy, like pre twelve and

00:48:23.230 --> 00:48:27.450
then post twelve, the average joy was
much higher before I became a teenager.

00:48:27.920 --> 00:48:30.940
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, there's so
many things to extrapolate from that.

00:48:30.940 --> 00:48:36.450
I really feel that when we get into
a mode of trying to hit milestones

00:48:36.450 --> 00:48:41.310
that are extrinsic, that it really can
undermine our love of what we're doing.

00:48:41.620 --> 00:48:47.089
But if we keep going and we can
reframe what those external rewards

00:48:47.090 --> 00:48:50.780
are, in part by just realizing that
they're so transient compared to

00:48:50.780 --> 00:48:53.199
the delight that we can experience.

00:48:53.209 --> 00:48:56.600
What I mean is that I don't think
of delight as something that wells

00:48:56.610 --> 00:48:58.490
up in us and then dissipates.

00:48:58.490 --> 00:49:01.870
I think of it as something that
changes our nervous system in a way

00:49:01.870 --> 00:49:03.649
that gives us access to new abilities.

00:49:03.650 --> 00:49:04.199
I really do.

00:49:04.210 --> 00:49:07.030
I mean, being a faculty member at
Stanford, you look to your left, you

00:49:07.030 --> 00:49:09.479
look to your right, and it's like
literally in the building, I mean,

00:49:09.480 --> 00:49:11.330
I've got a Nobel prize winner below me.

00:49:11.330 --> 00:49:13.990
Like the people by me I've got
MacArthur award winners all over the

00:49:13.990 --> 00:49:16.640
place, like everywhere you turn, and
these people do other things, too.

00:49:16.970 --> 00:49:20.890
So also D1 athletes, and they've
got five kids, and all their

00:49:20.890 --> 00:49:21.930
kids seem to be doing great.

00:49:22.380 --> 00:49:23.259
Who are these people?

00:49:23.509 --> 00:49:29.130
And it becomes very important in that
environment to just shrink your spirits,

00:49:29.190 --> 00:49:33.129
like, what's one foot in front of you and
just keep going and not pay attention.

00:49:33.130 --> 00:49:36.690
But it's hard to do, not by way of
comparison, because I actually get

00:49:36.810 --> 00:49:40.899
excited about being immersed in a
group where everyone's doing well.

00:49:40.900 --> 00:49:44.759
I do think being among all these
other incredibly talented and driven,

00:49:45.670 --> 00:49:49.460
although you carefully said, and
importantly said rather, that you did

00:49:49.460 --> 00:49:52.350
not see yourself as talented, it's
very clear that you have a ton of grit

00:49:53.240 --> 00:49:54.920
and hard work clearly went into it.

00:49:54.920 --> 00:49:58.650
I think that word talent can be
a little bit misleading, so we

00:49:58.650 --> 00:50:01.379
want to underscore the fact that
you've worked incredibly hard.

00:50:01.950 --> 00:50:04.239
But I think that it's a tough thing.

00:50:05.109 --> 00:50:08.089
It's hard for us to develop much in
isolation, and it's also hard for

00:50:08.090 --> 00:50:10.830
us to stay connected to the source.

00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:12.420
Maya Shankar: Yes, exactly.

00:50:12.480 --> 00:50:15.299
Andrew Huberman: And that's a word
that I stole from a former guest on

00:50:15.300 --> 00:50:18.630
this podcast and a good friend of mine
who's the great Rick Rubin, one of

00:50:18.639 --> 00:50:21.650
the most successful music producers,
rock and roll music producers of all.

00:50:21.650 --> 00:50:21.987
Maya Shankar: I loved that interview.

00:50:21.987 --> 00:50:26.330
Andrew Huberman: He talks about
the source, so there are so many

00:50:26.330 --> 00:50:28.040
different trails we could go down here.

00:50:28.040 --> 00:50:33.960
Just one thing, briefly, is I,
again, am completely miserable at

00:50:33.960 --> 00:50:39.000
music, but I once saw Itzhak Perlman
in the airport with his family.

00:50:39.130 --> 00:50:42.370
I was with my father, who's a
huge classical music fan, and we

00:50:42.370 --> 00:50:43.810
watched him, and he said, watch.

00:50:43.810 --> 00:50:45.799
And it turns out he was
getting onto our plane.

00:50:45.810 --> 00:50:49.580
He sat in first class next to his,
I presume, Stradivarius violin.

00:50:50.040 --> 00:50:51.929
His violin got a first class seat.

00:50:52.270 --> 00:50:55.419
He got a first class seat, and
his family sat across from him.

00:50:55.419 --> 00:50:59.879
And my dad said his violin is so important
that it gets its own first class seat.

00:50:59.900 --> 00:51:01.409
I couldn't believe it.

00:51:01.430 --> 00:51:01.879
So great.

00:51:02.630 --> 00:51:03.250
In any event--

00:51:04.120 --> 00:51:07.520
Maya Shankar: --I think, just one t
hing to your point, one reflection

00:51:07.520 --> 00:51:11.030
I've had, and this kind of goes back
to this question of identity, which is

00:51:12.160 --> 00:51:14.879
when you are in these very competitive
environments, and again, I'm sure a

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:18.920
lot of people listening are in very
competitive environments, you feel that

00:51:18.960 --> 00:51:22.850
so much can be taken away from you,
just in terms of mental well being,

00:51:23.120 --> 00:51:26.450
because you're always looking at the
world through a comparative lens.

00:51:26.500 --> 00:51:28.850
You're benchmarking yourself,
as you said, like there's a

00:51:28.920 --> 00:51:33.989
benchmark, and where do I fall on
the continuum of mediocre to great?

00:51:33.990 --> 00:51:37.560
I don't know, and yesterday I had
a terrible performance, so that's

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:38.859
going to set me back, etc., etc.

00:51:40.260 --> 00:51:47.350
I have found that when I re-anchor
myself to what Rick Rubin referred

00:51:47.350 --> 00:51:52.490
to as the source and identify the
characteristics of music or other

00:51:52.490 --> 00:51:55.500
pursuits, that really energizes me.

00:51:56.530 --> 00:52:00.850
It feels like I'm actually insulated
from a lot of the external noise,

00:52:01.320 --> 00:52:05.189
and I bring a lot more clarity and
focus to the work that I do every day.

00:52:05.190 --> 00:52:09.969
So there's two things that I think
define me as a person, at least right

00:52:09.969 --> 00:52:11.220
now, I allow for that malleability.

00:52:12.549 --> 00:52:14.570
One is that I'm a deeply curious person.

00:52:15.220 --> 00:52:18.930
And the second is that I really
relish getting better at things.

00:52:19.230 --> 00:52:21.380
I love seeing progress internally.

00:52:22.099 --> 00:52:29.029
And in my violin life, no one could
take those two things away from me.

00:52:29.430 --> 00:52:33.840
In my current life as a cognitive
scientist, as a podcaster, you

00:52:33.840 --> 00:52:35.370
just can't take those from me.

00:52:35.740 --> 00:52:38.120
No one can take that joy from me.

00:52:38.660 --> 00:52:41.680
And it feels protective in a
really important way, which

00:52:41.680 --> 00:52:45.220
is, for example, just like you.

00:52:45.220 --> 00:52:48.613
I mean, I see the labor of love that
you put into the Huberman Lab podcast.

00:52:48.680 --> 00:52:49.430
It's extraordinary.

00:52:50.010 --> 00:52:53.810
I put so much time and energy and
thoughtfulness and love into making A

00:52:53.810 --> 00:52:56.949
Slight Change of Plans , but at the end
of the day, when you put the episode

00:52:56.950 --> 00:53:00.260
out into the world, you just don't get
to control what the reaction is, right?

00:53:00.550 --> 00:53:03.310
Your favorite episode might
not be everyone else's favorite

00:53:03.310 --> 00:53:05.210
episode, and that's just
something you have to deal with.

00:53:05.940 --> 00:53:09.570
But what I found is that if I really
relished the process of making the

00:53:09.570 --> 00:53:11.630
episode, it fed that curiosity.

00:53:11.800 --> 00:53:15.400
And I got better as an interviewer,
I got better as a thinker.

00:53:15.650 --> 00:53:19.080
I got more clarity on a topic
that I was curious about.

00:53:19.080 --> 00:53:23.929
I mean, it gives me a foundation
that feels really sturdy.

00:53:23.940 --> 00:53:24.800
Do you know what I mean?

00:53:25.260 --> 00:53:28.380
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, w ell, those
things are intrinsic to you, and

00:53:28.670 --> 00:53:33.970
they are, I guess, now we're using
nomenclature, but they're not what we

00:53:33.970 --> 00:53:37.930
would call domain-specific, like the
curiosity, the desire for progress

00:53:37.930 --> 00:53:40.629
through effort and through focus.

00:53:41.160 --> 00:53:42.580
Those are music.

00:53:43.180 --> 00:53:45.800
They're not music irrelevant,
but they're music independent.

00:53:45.820 --> 00:53:51.209
And that actually brings me to a
very important component of your

00:53:51.210 --> 00:53:58.040
work and your life arc, which
is this notion of recreating and

00:53:59.010 --> 00:54:01.660
refinding identity in new endeavors.

00:54:02.040 --> 00:54:06.880
So if I understand correctly, and
hopefully you'll embellish on this, you

00:54:06.890 --> 00:54:13.749
had the unfortunate, perhaps unfortunate
experience of playing the violin and then

00:54:13.750 --> 00:54:18.359
injuring your finger very badly to the
point where it was, at least for your

00:54:18.420 --> 00:54:20.390
music career, career ending, absolutely.

00:54:20.740 --> 00:54:22.990
And that happened when you were how old?

00:54:23.069 --> 00:54:23.669
Maya Shankar: I was 15.

00:54:24.420 --> 00:54:28.630
Andrew Huberman: So given how much of
your identity and energy was put into

00:54:28.630 --> 00:54:31.590
violin, that must have been devastating.

00:54:32.630 --> 00:54:36.930
And yet you've obviously, I don't want
to say re-created yourself, because I

00:54:36.930 --> 00:54:41.730
like the idea that this essence within
you has many opportunities and forms.

00:54:41.730 --> 00:54:46.830
And I like it as an example for everybody
having some essence of many things that

00:54:46.830 --> 00:54:51.830
could give them delight, and that it's
something about the feelings associated

00:54:52.209 --> 00:54:57.970
with a given choice of occupation or hobby
or behavior, or perhaps relationship.

00:54:58.800 --> 00:55:01.529
Relationships end sometimes by
decision, death or otherwise,

00:55:01.770 --> 00:55:03.030
and people are devastated.

00:55:03.389 --> 00:55:07.670
Their identities are completely, at
least in their minds, obliterated.

00:55:07.730 --> 00:55:10.819
And then people have this
amazing ability to recreate

00:55:11.320 --> 00:55:13.209
themselves and new circumstances.

00:55:13.210 --> 00:55:17.049
So if you could take us back to the time
when you were 15, you have this injury.

00:55:17.180 --> 00:55:21.340
What was your initial mindset in the
days and weeks after that a nd then,

00:55:21.760 --> 00:55:26.220
if you would, could you link that up
to some of what I see as incredibly

00:55:26.220 --> 00:55:30.350
important work that you've done,
helping people understand not just

00:55:30.350 --> 00:55:35.720
who they are, but how to identify the
components of who they are that are truly

00:55:35.720 --> 00:55:38.670
indomitable, that just cannot go away.

00:55:39.260 --> 00:55:42.290
Like, your drive for
curiosity and hard work--.

00:55:42.320 --> 00:55:43.230
Maya Shankar: --And human connection.

00:55:43.250 --> 00:55:43.560
Yeah.

00:55:45.110 --> 00:55:51.830
In the days and weeks and months
and year after, I felt terrible.

00:55:52.370 --> 00:55:58.500
It was awful because I think, in my case,
also, when you're a kid who's really

00:55:58.850 --> 00:56:01.809
bubbly and energetic, you just kind
of move forward, and you don't always

00:56:02.280 --> 00:56:06.399
think about how identity defining the
thing you're doing is you just do it.

00:56:06.800 --> 00:56:10.799
And so it was really interesting, I
think, in losing the violin, that's

00:56:10.800 --> 00:56:15.060
actually when it became so salient
to me how much the instrument had

00:56:15.060 --> 00:56:17.449
meant to me and had defined who I was.

00:56:17.969 --> 00:56:24.500
And so I felt a dampening of
some of my more organic traits.

00:56:24.550 --> 00:56:26.760
Like, I was less curious for a long time.

00:56:27.760 --> 00:56:29.080
Andrew Huberman: I'm going
to interrupt you on purpose.

00:56:29.110 --> 00:56:29.910
I apologize.

00:56:29.940 --> 00:56:33.029
But at the same time, I'm not apologizing,
because there was something that you

00:56:33.040 --> 00:56:36.410
said in a prior discussion that just
keeps ringing in my mind, which is

00:56:36.410 --> 00:56:41.230
that your body and your nervous system
actually grew up around the violin.

00:56:42.070 --> 00:56:43.700
That, to me, was just.

00:56:43.980 --> 00:56:45.520
I will never forget that statement.

00:56:45.560 --> 00:56:49.940
I want to also thank you for it,
because that, to me, is perhaps the most

00:56:49.950 --> 00:56:56.829
profound way to describe an experience
of identity, is that your nervous system

00:56:56.829 --> 00:57:00.820
in your body isn't growing up with
something or alongside it, but that

00:57:01.000 --> 00:57:07.509
much like a relationship of a human
kind, humankind, that your body is

00:57:07.510 --> 00:57:10.430
actually developing around this object.

00:57:10.630 --> 00:57:13.170
Maya Shankar: It absolutely
developed around the ergonomics

00:57:13.170 --> 00:57:14.290
of playing the violin.

00:57:14.330 --> 00:57:17.339
So, to this day, my right
shoulder is slightly elevated

00:57:17.340 --> 00:57:21.080
relative to my left because of
all the hours I spent doing this.

00:57:21.490 --> 00:57:25.330
It makes strength training really
annoying because I always have this slight

00:57:25.330 --> 00:57:30.320
imbalance, and I have a light scoliosis in
my spine as well, also from this posture.

00:57:30.799 --> 00:57:34.250
And, yeah, it feels intimate in a way.

00:57:34.260 --> 00:57:36.520
It's like, wow, the shape of my body.

00:57:36.770 --> 00:57:41.590
Like, my architecture was
defined by this instrument.

00:57:42.070 --> 00:57:51.889
And so it's left this indelible
imprint on me that will never go away.

00:57:51.969 --> 00:57:58.299
And I think that a lot of us
feel this disorientation, right?

00:57:58.300 --> 00:58:02.609
So it might not be that you lost the
ability to do something you love.

00:58:02.610 --> 00:58:04.410
It could be that you lost
someone that you love.

00:58:04.410 --> 00:58:10.090
It could be that you lost
your mojo or whatever.

00:58:10.090 --> 00:58:14.140
I mean, there's so many types of
loss and so many kinds of grief

00:58:14.140 --> 00:58:16.060
we all experience as human beings.

00:58:16.500 --> 00:58:20.620
And I think in all those cases, again,
it really feels like the rug has been

00:58:20.620 --> 00:58:24.850
pulled out from under you because
this thing that gave you so much

00:58:24.880 --> 00:58:28.009
meaning and so much purpose and so
much energy in life no longer exists.

00:58:28.719 --> 00:58:33.120
And so I think, for a while, yeah, I
felt kind of like, lost at sea, and

00:58:33.680 --> 00:58:36.649
I assumed I'll never find anything
that I'm as passionate about.

00:58:37.040 --> 00:58:42.285
And I think, what my dad did for me at
that, you know, theoretical physicist.

00:58:42.540 --> 00:58:46.700
So he's an academic, and he said,
I think you should just read a lot.

00:58:47.520 --> 00:58:48.959
Just, like, read a bunch of stuff.

00:58:49.630 --> 00:58:54.000
And I was like, okay, I mean,
I'm supposed to be in China this

00:58:54.000 --> 00:58:55.610
summer touring with my classmates.

00:58:55.660 --> 00:59:00.419
I am at home in Connecticut with my
parents, perusing their bookshelf.

00:59:00.429 --> 00:59:02.320
So, like, slightly less
cool summer situation.

00:59:02.320 --> 00:59:05.829
But I had a lot of time on my
hands because I wasn't in Shanghai.

00:59:06.160 --> 00:59:09.520
So I started perusing the bookshelf,
and then I came across this pop

00:59:09.520 --> 00:59:12.660
science book called the Language
Instinct by Stephen Pinker.

00:59:13.860 --> 00:59:18.440
And that was a turning point for me.

00:59:18.500 --> 00:59:22.640
I mean, I was headed to
college maybe later that year.

00:59:23.119 --> 00:59:30.440
I opened up this book, and it
detailed our marvelous ability to

00:59:30.510 --> 00:59:32.240
comprehend and produce language.

00:59:33.110 --> 00:59:36.780
And up until this point in my life,
I had completely taken language

00:59:36.820 --> 00:59:40.150
abilities for granted, just like
something that I did, and I just

00:59:40.309 --> 00:59:41.810
kind of learned it along the way.

00:59:41.840 --> 00:59:48.470
And when Pinker pulled the curtain
back and revealed how sophisticated and

00:59:48.470 --> 00:59:53.100
complex the cognitive machinery is that's
operating behind the scenes that gives

00:59:53.100 --> 00:59:56.640
rise to language, my mind was truly blown.

00:59:56.690 --> 00:59:57.509
I was like, wow.

00:59:57.530 --> 01:00:00.090
I never thought about it, it's not
like we are with three year olds,

01:00:00.090 --> 01:00:02.025
not like we sit down with them
and we're like, this is a gerund.

01:00:02.320 --> 01:00:03.600
This is a past participle, whatever.

01:00:04.580 --> 01:00:08.049
They just learn because they have these
kind of light switches in their brain

01:00:08.050 --> 01:00:11.899
that are activated on and off depending
on what language they're learning.

01:00:12.230 --> 01:00:16.239
And it was so fascinating to
learn about language development,

01:00:16.299 --> 01:00:20.580
about neurolinguistics,
about syntax and semantics.

01:00:21.500 --> 01:00:24.490
I just remember thinking,
language is fascinating.

01:00:24.520 --> 01:00:25.920
Cognition is fascinating.

01:00:26.179 --> 01:00:32.200
And I'm also now wondering about all
these other systems that are in place.

01:00:32.450 --> 01:00:33.999
So this is what's involved in language.

01:00:34.000 --> 01:00:39.089
What's involved in the complex
math equations our dads do, right?

01:00:39.110 --> 01:00:44.420
Like, what's involved in, what's the
mental processing behind a new discovery

01:00:44.420 --> 01:00:47.479
or an insight or an aha moment or
falling in love or falling out of love.

01:00:47.679 --> 01:00:49.770
I mean, it just lit up my imagination.

01:00:50.139 --> 01:00:53.880
And very similar to you, Andrew, I
love that we have this connection.

01:00:54.209 --> 01:00:56.600
You said when you learned about
neurobiology and neuroscience,

01:00:56.620 --> 01:00:58.790
you saw that there was a
place for yourself in there.

01:00:59.170 --> 01:01:01.930
And I remember reading this book, and
because it was a pop science book,

01:01:01.930 --> 01:01:05.439
and I love pop science books because
sometimes, even if they don't fully do

01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:09.699
justice to the science, they can take
someone who's never had any exposure to

01:01:09.700 --> 01:01:14.690
the subject matter, and it's thrilling
to learn about the thing, right?

01:01:14.710 --> 01:01:18.490
I would never have gotten the same
experience had I opened up an introduction

01:01:18.490 --> 01:01:19.850
to cognitive science textbook.

01:01:19.880 --> 01:01:21.549
It would not have had
the same impact on me.

01:01:21.550 --> 01:01:24.550
So, like, shout out to pop
science folks everywhere.

01:01:24.590 --> 01:01:28.599
Andrew Huberman: Thank you for saying,
just thank you, because I think that

01:01:28.609 --> 01:01:32.550
many of my colleagues in academic
science at Stanford and elsewhere

01:01:32.550 --> 01:01:34.180
feel that way, but I think many don't.

01:01:34.180 --> 01:01:36.380
They think of it as
"dumbing down" of things.

01:01:36.750 --> 01:01:42.100
But I'll tell you, rarely, if ever, does
somebody just wander into a university

01:01:42.150 --> 01:01:44.730
classroom and hear a lecture by accident.

01:01:44.730 --> 01:01:44.820
Maya Shankar: [LAUGHS]

01:01:44.839 --> 01:01:47.820
Andrew Huberman: I mean, maybe if your
mom was at the helm, they all would.

01:01:47.859 --> 01:01:49.890
So mom's everywhere, barge right in.

01:01:51.790 --> 01:01:57.210
But I think it's actually, I'll
go a step further, and I'll do

01:01:57.210 --> 01:01:58.239
this so that you don't have to.

01:01:58.239 --> 01:01:59.670
And these are not your words.

01:01:59.670 --> 01:02:00.140
These are mine.

01:02:00.170 --> 01:02:06.109
I think that there's actually a pretty
intense arrogance to the idea within the

01:02:06.750 --> 01:02:10.600
established scientific community that
pop science books, while they might not

01:02:10.620 --> 01:02:15.689
be exhaustive, provided they're accurate
and they're making an attempt to educate

01:02:15.690 --> 01:02:17.660
and draw people in from all sectors.

01:02:18.349 --> 01:02:19.360
Amen to that.

01:02:19.520 --> 01:02:23.589
I just can't hear a counterargument in
my head or elsewhere where that's not one

01:02:23.590 --> 01:02:25.620
of the best things that people can do.

01:02:25.620 --> 01:02:32.950
So regardless of people's motivations
for picking them up in the first

01:02:32.950 --> 01:02:36.319
place, I mean, they brought a lot
of people into the curiosity and

01:02:36.320 --> 01:02:40.120
delight that is science or music or...

01:02:40.709 --> 01:02:46.810
I think that the more positive,
benevolent, safe sensory experiences

01:02:46.810 --> 01:02:50.480
that we can expose young people to, the
greater probability that we're going

01:02:50.480 --> 01:02:55.140
to flesh out those professions with
the greatest number of diverse minds

01:02:55.140 --> 01:02:56.689
who are going to have the best ideas.

01:02:57.110 --> 01:03:02.000
I think that there's a ton of foresight
in what you're describing that,

01:03:02.270 --> 01:03:08.250
picking up a book is now, you're also
now a PhD in cognitive science, and

01:03:08.260 --> 01:03:09.459
you did your postdoc at Stanford.

01:03:09.459 --> 01:03:13.159
I mean, you're a scientist,
presumably because you went into the

01:03:13.160 --> 01:03:15.079
bookshelf and picked up that book.

01:03:15.139 --> 01:03:15.649
Maya Shankar: 100%.

01:03:15.649 --> 01:03:20.840
And I think it was also role modeled for
me because my dad, despite being in a

01:03:20.850 --> 01:03:25.799
very, very technical field, spent a large
part of his career actually working on the

01:03:25.800 --> 01:03:31.390
translation of complex subjects and trying
to convey them to general audiences.

01:03:31.950 --> 01:03:35.710
And I loved witnessing this because
it's like, if you can figure out a

01:03:35.710 --> 01:03:40.360
way to communicate about theoretical
physics to a general audience, I mean,

01:03:40.460 --> 01:03:42.580
wow, that's a masterful pursuit, right?

01:03:42.930 --> 01:03:43.089
Andrew Huberman: Feynman.

01:03:43.100 --> 01:03:43.509
Richard Feynman.

01:03:43.580 --> 01:03:43.890
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

01:03:43.890 --> 01:03:44.170
Richard Feynman.

01:03:44.520 --> 01:03:45.110
Exactly.

01:03:45.120 --> 01:03:47.160
Andrew Huberman: No one really
knows what Feynman did for his

01:03:47.219 --> 01:03:49.049
Nobel Prize work, except physicists.

01:03:49.170 --> 01:03:52.099
You know that most people, you ask
them, what was Feynman's Nobel for?

01:03:52.099 --> 01:03:53.550
And they're like, I don't know.

01:03:53.799 --> 01:03:54.330
Maya Shankar: I don't know.

01:03:54.620 --> 01:03:56.469
Andrew Huberman: He said something
about birds and taxonomy and

01:03:56.490 --> 01:03:59.120
how it's less interesting than,
you know, quantum mechanics.

01:03:59.130 --> 01:04:02.719
Maya Shankar: And one of the reasons
that I love Huberman Lab, and I just

01:04:02.719 --> 01:04:06.940
love the work you do, is that you are
taking concepts that might have been

01:04:06.950 --> 01:04:10.660
inaccessible to the average person,
and you're making science accessible.

01:04:10.940 --> 01:04:17.189
And I feel so much gratitude to every
scientist out there, every researcher out

01:04:17.190 --> 01:04:22.150
there who thinks that it's worth their
time to be a practitioner of their work.

01:04:22.690 --> 01:04:25.660
Because ultimately, think about how
many lives you're changing through

01:04:25.660 --> 01:04:29.059
the show by trying to break down some
of these more complicated things into

01:04:30.190 --> 01:04:33.720
concepts that people can understand
and relate to and actually act on.

01:04:34.100 --> 01:04:39.960
And it also reminds me, part of
my job when I was in the Obama

01:04:39.960 --> 01:04:43.950
administration was translating
insights from behavioral science, from

01:04:43.950 --> 01:04:48.270
cognitive science into interventions
that my government agency colleagues

01:04:48.270 --> 01:04:51.830
could implement in the Department of
Veterans affairs, in the Department

01:04:51.830 --> 01:04:53.410
of Defense, Department of Education.

01:04:53.610 --> 01:04:56.790
And that same translation process
was part of that effort, too.

01:04:56.790 --> 01:04:59.430
And I think it's really,
really hard to do well.

01:04:59.630 --> 01:05:01.200
I respect it so much.

01:05:01.410 --> 01:05:04.880
I respect pop science writers
who do a good job so much.

01:05:05.550 --> 01:05:07.629
And yeah, I think it's
a wonderful service.

01:05:07.670 --> 01:05:10.149
They don't have to spend their
time writing these books.

01:05:10.480 --> 01:05:14.069
They could just publish more research
papers, which is the currency that

01:05:14.090 --> 01:05:15.729
academic institutions care about.

01:05:15.969 --> 01:05:18.689
And so I see it as just like a
public good, what they're doing.

01:05:18.870 --> 01:05:19.759
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, I do, too.

01:05:19.759 --> 01:05:22.029
And right back at you, because
you're doing it as well.

01:05:22.029 --> 01:05:23.930
And so we're all better off for it.

01:05:23.940 --> 01:05:25.089
So thank you.

