01:06:32.009 --> 01:06:38.180
So I want to go back to this
injury, to summer at home, to

01:06:38.200 --> 01:06:39.720
discovery of something new.

01:06:40.470 --> 01:06:45.030
Was it at that point that you
realized the feeling of excitement

01:06:45.030 --> 01:06:49.000
that I'm getting from learning about
neurolinguistics and related topics

01:06:49.320 --> 01:06:55.540
is somehow similar to the excitement
that I was feeling about the violin, or

01:06:55.540 --> 01:06:57.180
maybe even superseded that excitement?

01:06:57.210 --> 01:07:02.640
I mean, at what point were you able
to make the pivot with confidence

01:07:03.020 --> 01:07:05.160
that this is the new trajectory?

01:07:05.170 --> 01:07:09.890
And an important component of that
that I'd like to understand is you

01:07:09.890 --> 01:07:14.260
also had to cut ties with the past,
something that's very hard to do.

01:07:14.320 --> 01:07:17.150
I mean, I grew up with a
number of kids who became very

01:07:17.490 --> 01:07:20.540
successful teen athletes, really.

01:07:20.870 --> 01:07:25.500
And some of them, once they ceased
to keep up or they had an injury

01:07:25.500 --> 01:07:29.920
or something, their identity stayed
attached to the past in a way that

01:07:29.920 --> 01:07:31.360
did not allow them to move forward.

01:07:31.370 --> 01:07:35.130
Fortunately, many of them
did find new identities in

01:07:35.130 --> 01:07:36.950
business or in other endeavors.

01:07:37.750 --> 01:07:38.960
Some became quite successful.

01:07:38.960 --> 01:07:45.840
But I've seen very often that when people
achieve early success and then they

01:07:45.840 --> 01:07:50.089
hit a cliff, that it's very hard for
them to part with that former identity.

01:07:50.680 --> 01:07:52.520
There's one of the
perils of early success.

01:07:52.580 --> 01:07:58.520
Maya Shankar: Yeah, I wouldn't say
that it superseded the excitement

01:07:58.520 --> 01:07:59.650
that I had with the violin.

01:08:00.059 --> 01:08:02.669
I would say the quality of the
excitement felt very different.

01:08:03.139 --> 01:08:05.940
And that's actually important to
convey because I think when someone

01:08:05.940 --> 01:08:10.890
loses the ability to have a passion,
they're seeking exactly the same sensory

01:08:10.990 --> 01:08:14.720
experience, exactly the same high that
they experienced the first time around.

01:08:15.020 --> 01:08:16.549
And I think that's a really high bar.

01:08:16.559 --> 01:08:19.819
And sometimes it's more of an
apples and oranges type situation.

01:08:19.830 --> 01:08:25.729
So with the violin, there was a really
deep sensory aspect to the experience.

01:08:25.810 --> 01:08:29.559
I mean, I felt things, right,
you're playing and then you're

01:08:29.569 --> 01:08:31.010
feeling things emotionally.

01:08:31.350 --> 01:08:36.339
And it all felt super visceral, and
that was where the passion emerged from.

01:08:36.349 --> 01:08:39.230
It was just this very visceral
feeling of, like, this is so

01:08:39.230 --> 01:08:40.660
beautiful and awesome, and I love it.

01:08:41.449 --> 01:08:45.630
With the cognitive science
stuff, my intellectual brain was

01:08:45.630 --> 01:08:49.000
delighted, and it's just like a
different expression of passion.

01:08:50.189 --> 01:08:54.759
I think the big pressure test was not
if I had held myself to the bar of do I

01:08:54.759 --> 01:08:58.880
love this as much as the violin, there's
no way that I would have been confident

01:08:58.880 --> 01:09:00.519
enough to pursue anything at that point.

01:09:00.979 --> 01:09:05.620
So instead, I really think the question
I asked myself at that time, which was

01:09:05.620 --> 01:09:11.529
a service to me and my more compromised
psychology, was, am I curious enough about

01:09:11.529 --> 01:09:13.750
this thing to ask more questions about it?

01:09:13.939 --> 01:09:15.419
Do I want to learn more?

01:09:15.990 --> 01:09:23.180
And I found, naturally, three days
later, I went to the library, and I got

01:09:23.180 --> 01:09:27.600
another book on the cognitive science
of language, and then I got a book

01:09:27.600 --> 01:09:29.479
on the science of decision making.

01:09:29.729 --> 01:09:34.400
So there was curiosity, and
honestly, that was all I needed.

01:09:34.690 --> 01:09:39.130
That was the little seedling that I needed
to see if it could go somewhere more.

01:09:39.680 --> 01:09:41.220
I took that as a very strong signal.

01:09:41.229 --> 01:09:44.049
Like, I care to learn more
about this, and I don't care to

01:09:44.050 --> 01:09:45.719
learn about everything, right?

01:09:46.179 --> 01:09:51.209
And I remember perusing the course
book of my undergrad institution,

01:09:51.210 --> 01:09:54.319
and they had a cognitive science
major, which was awesome because

01:09:54.770 --> 01:09:56.580
not all schools had one at the time.

01:09:56.580 --> 01:09:57.610
It was a very new major.

01:09:57.610 --> 01:09:58.920
It's interdisciplinary.

01:09:59.270 --> 01:10:02.360
You approach questions of the
mind from multiple perspectives.

01:10:02.360 --> 01:10:07.609
So from the perspective of neuroscience,
linguistics, philosophy, psychology,

01:10:08.580 --> 01:10:11.880
computer science, and anthropology.

01:10:12.070 --> 01:10:14.110
So you're just like a bunch
of different disciplines.

01:10:15.320 --> 01:10:19.290
But that was when I thought, ooh, I can
at least see if I can get into this major.

01:10:19.300 --> 01:10:20.660
I remember it was,
like, a selective major.

01:10:20.660 --> 01:10:21.519
It was selective.

01:10:21.520 --> 01:10:23.930
And so I freaked out, of course,
and had super impostor syndrome.

01:10:23.930 --> 01:10:25.940
It was like, I'm not going
to get into the program.

01:10:26.509 --> 01:10:30.820
But thankfully, I got in, and I think
that's where I was able to connect,

01:10:30.820 --> 01:10:37.980
like, this little seedling of curiosity
to the actual pursuit of the thing.

01:10:38.460 --> 01:10:41.970
And that's a really important translation,
because there can often be a mismatch.

01:10:42.320 --> 01:10:45.419
You're really passionate about something,
but you actually hate the process.

01:10:46.260 --> 01:10:49.169
Like, you hate the actual work that's
involved in getting better at it.

01:10:49.600 --> 01:10:54.799
And I was lucky in my undergrad because
I fought my way, my mom style, barging

01:10:54.810 --> 01:10:59.730
into classes that really would only
accept seniors or juniors, and I was

01:10:59.730 --> 01:11:02.030
like, I'm a lowly freshman, but accept me.

01:11:02.679 --> 01:11:06.250
And I was able to run experiments
on adults, and I was actually able

01:11:06.250 --> 01:11:10.429
to see what it would be like to be
a researcher, to ask novel questions

01:11:10.790 --> 01:11:14.970
and to get the delight that you feel
right when you're in a lab and you're

01:11:14.970 --> 01:11:16.539
actually testing out new hypotheses.

01:11:16.620 --> 01:11:21.389
And so it was really important that
I saw that I not only was excited,

01:11:22.139 --> 01:11:25.320
but that I could actually enjoy parts
of the process of getting better.

01:11:25.980 --> 01:11:29.280
Andrew Huberman: I love your description
of curiosity because it makes me think

01:11:29.870 --> 01:11:34.549
that in some way, it has something
to do with a deep motivation and

01:11:34.550 --> 01:11:39.300
desire to figure out what's next or
what's around the corner without an

01:11:39.300 --> 01:11:41.010
emotional attachment to the outcome.

01:11:41.559 --> 01:11:44.210
Curiosity is really just trying to
figure out what's there as opposed to

01:11:44.220 --> 01:11:45.870
hoping that something specific is there.

01:11:45.870 --> 01:11:49.530
And sometimes even the surprises are
more exciting than our predictions.

01:11:50.120 --> 01:11:54.540
I think the quote was
initially from Dorothy Parker.

01:11:54.650 --> 01:11:56.340
I think this is debated,
but I think it was.

01:11:58.379 --> 01:12:00.439
"The cure for boredom is curiosity.

01:12:00.950 --> 01:12:02.630
There is no cure for curiosity."

01:12:03.320 --> 01:12:04.040
Maya Shankar: Oh, that's awesome.

01:12:04.690 --> 01:12:05.480
I hadn't heard that.

01:12:05.490 --> 01:12:08.610
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, I believe
it was Dorothy Parker, sometimes

01:12:08.630 --> 01:12:11.490
misattributed to Agatha Christie,
but I think it was Dorothy Parker.

01:12:11.490 --> 01:12:15.789
And what I love about it is that there's
something about curiosity, that when

01:12:15.790 --> 01:12:18.300
it's genuine, it's self-amplifying.

01:12:18.560 --> 01:12:21.610
It's an upward spiral,
because there is no endpoint.

01:12:21.800 --> 01:12:22.040
Right?

01:12:22.040 --> 01:12:27.100
I mean, that's one of the things that
you learn early in science, is you

01:12:27.990 --> 01:12:31.570
learn, you test hypotheses, you get
answers, and you get more questions,

01:12:31.570 --> 01:12:34.490
and you form hypotheses, and you
do that until you die, basically.

01:12:34.779 --> 01:12:36.130
And they can be a little bit dark.

01:12:36.630 --> 01:12:41.210
But when you think about it as a journey,
that it's just so much fun along the way.

01:12:41.210 --> 01:12:44.650
If you're just really interested in
knowing what the answers are without

01:12:44.650 --> 01:12:48.400
getting too attached to the answers, it
just feels like, even as I'm describing

01:12:48.400 --> 01:12:52.689
it now, they just can just fill you
up, and it provides more energy for

01:12:52.690 --> 01:12:54.289
the next round and the next round.

01:12:54.289 --> 01:12:57.830
And that really came through in your
description of cognitive science.

01:12:57.830 --> 01:13:00.660
I also find it interesting
that you couldn't read sheet

01:13:00.660 --> 01:13:02.070
music, at least not very well.

01:13:02.460 --> 01:13:07.810
You were so deeply immersed in
an endeavor, violin playing,

01:13:08.049 --> 01:13:10.460
that is not of verbal language.

01:13:10.869 --> 01:13:15.894
And then you went into a field that's
about, or initially, you were sparked

01:13:16.230 --> 01:13:19.570
an interest in a field through an
understanding of verbal language.

01:13:20.020 --> 01:13:25.120
And earlier you said that the thing
that bridges the violin and what came

01:13:25.120 --> 01:13:29.360
next as a passion and pursuit was
this desire for human connection.

01:13:29.830 --> 01:13:31.320
At what point did you realize that?

01:13:31.800 --> 01:13:35.410
And here I do want to emphasize that
while we're talking about your story,

01:13:35.410 --> 01:13:39.559
I hope, I can only imagine that people
are starting to think about what are

01:13:40.830 --> 01:13:44.139
the intrinsic points of motivation for
what they're doing and what they've

01:13:44.150 --> 01:13:49.110
done, asking the sorts of questions
that I hope everyone is asking.

01:13:50.080 --> 01:13:54.510
What is it really that motivates
me to love this and to see

01:13:54.510 --> 01:13:56.179
a place for myself in that?

01:13:57.459 --> 01:13:59.889
Because those are ultimately,
I think, the questions that

01:13:59.900 --> 01:14:02.789
everyone should and can ask.

01:14:02.860 --> 01:14:06.270
Maya Shankar: Yeah, it
took me a really long time.

01:14:06.270 --> 01:14:08.850
It's actually only been in the last
few years that I've discovered this.

01:14:09.670 --> 01:14:13.309
I discovered this as a result of
creating A Slight Change of Plans . So

01:14:15.889 --> 01:14:20.429
my desire to create the show came
from a very personal place, which

01:14:20.429 --> 01:14:22.660
is that I'm terrified of change.

01:14:22.670 --> 01:14:24.740
So even though I've had these
formative experiences with

01:14:24.740 --> 01:14:26.700
change, I'm a creature of habit.

01:14:27.180 --> 01:14:28.750
I'm willing to change my habits.

01:14:28.760 --> 01:14:31.840
For example, I now take caffeine
90 minutes after I get up.

01:14:31.840 --> 01:14:32.849
Andrew Huberman: How's
that working for you?

01:14:33.000 --> 01:14:34.219
Maya Shankar: Very well, even today.

01:14:34.260 --> 01:14:34.550
Okay.

01:14:35.020 --> 01:14:35.880
I'm a good disciple.

01:14:36.500 --> 01:14:36.540
[LAUGHS]

01:14:36.750 --> 01:14:39.609
Andrew Huberman: Well, I like
to think that people afford

01:14:39.610 --> 01:14:42.300
themselves some flexibility if
you got to run to the airport--

01:14:42.520 --> 01:14:42.790
Maya Shankar: --60 to 90 minutes.

01:14:43.360 --> 01:14:46.060
Andrew Huberman: [LAUGHS] 60 and
90, or the occasional within 30

01:14:46.060 --> 01:14:50.100
minutes if you have to, but nobody's
perfect, nor should we strive.

01:14:50.290 --> 01:14:50.570
Maya Shankar: I'm a student.

01:14:50.670 --> 01:14:52.440
I'm willing to update my habits,
but I'm a creature of habit.

01:14:53.040 --> 01:14:59.920
And there's a couple of reasons why
we, as humans, are scared of change.

01:14:59.920 --> 01:15:02.780
And I think one of them, which
is incredibly relatable, is that

01:15:03.570 --> 01:15:07.540
change is filled with a lot of
uncertainty, and we hate uncertainty.

01:15:08.040 --> 01:15:12.049
We will go to irrational
lengths to avoid uncertainty.

01:15:12.050 --> 01:15:15.460
So one of my favorite studies
coming out of cognitive sciences

01:15:15.799 --> 01:15:17.439
is one involving electric shocks.

01:15:17.459 --> 01:15:22.830
And what they found is that people are
far more stressed when they're told they

01:15:22.830 --> 01:15:26.549
have a 50% chance of getting an electric
shock than when they're told they have a

01:15:26.980 --> 01:15:29.440
100% chance of getting an electric shock.

01:15:29.990 --> 01:15:33.769
So we would rather be sure, certain
that a bad thing is going to happen

01:15:34.119 --> 01:15:37.600
than to have to deal with any
feelings of uncertainty and ambiguity.

01:15:37.600 --> 01:15:37.935
Andrew Huberman: Right.

01:15:38.270 --> 01:15:41.299
That result, I love that
you brought up that result.

01:15:41.330 --> 01:15:44.679
It still is bewildering to me,
because if you think about it,

01:15:45.770 --> 01:15:53.139
100% trial to trial shock means you
have to take on the okay, bring it,

01:15:53.420 --> 01:15:55.290
just bring it on kind of mentality.

01:15:55.670 --> 01:15:58.630
But if you did that for every
trial and then half of the

01:15:58.630 --> 01:15:59.830
trials, you don't get shocked.

01:15:59.830 --> 01:16:07.690
We know there's a dopamine release from
the lack of punishment, so the ideal

01:16:07.690 --> 01:16:11.139
strategy is the same, and yet somehow,
people are averse to the uncertainty.

01:16:11.139 --> 01:16:14.660
Maya Shankar: Yeah, we don't
like uncertainty, even though,

01:16:14.660 --> 01:16:17.429
again, the uncertainty is what
drives that dopamine first.

01:16:18.270 --> 01:16:22.790
And yet we bristle, certainly,
at that uncertainty.

01:16:22.790 --> 01:16:27.230
And so I definitely am like,
please, status quo, everyone,

01:16:27.240 --> 01:16:28.330
I would love the status quo.

01:16:28.359 --> 01:16:31.269
Even when the status quo has
been suboptimal, Andrew, I've

01:16:31.270 --> 01:16:32.429
been fine with the status quo.

01:16:32.430 --> 01:16:35.810
So part of it came from my desire
to figure out, okay, how is it...

01:16:36.050 --> 01:16:40.100
Like, A Slight Change of Plans , marries
science and storytelling to help us figure

01:16:40.100 --> 01:16:42.170
out strategies for better managing change.

01:16:42.179 --> 01:16:45.969
So I wanted to figure out, how are
people coming to terms with uncertainty?

01:16:46.930 --> 01:16:51.540
One of the things that I realized I
learned from the guests on my show

01:16:51.540 --> 01:16:54.870
and also the scientists, is there's
this concept called cognitive closure,

01:16:55.560 --> 01:17:00.350
and it is the need to arrive at
clear, definitive answers to things.

01:17:00.500 --> 01:17:04.860
It's basically the opposite of this
open ended curiosity that you just

01:17:04.860 --> 01:17:09.610
described, which is with cognitive
closure, you have a need to, you aren't

01:17:09.610 --> 01:17:11.510
indifferent towards what the answers are.

01:17:11.670 --> 01:17:13.710
You aren't indifferent towards
what the questions are.

01:17:13.840 --> 01:17:14.920
You care about everything.

01:17:14.920 --> 01:17:17.360
You care about micromanaging
every part of the curious

01:17:17.370 --> 01:17:19.269
process from point A to point B.

01:17:19.759 --> 01:17:24.269
And there's a lot of research showing
that when we reduce our need for

01:17:24.270 --> 01:17:29.400
cognitive closure, when we become a
little bit more open to the unbidden,

01:17:29.860 --> 01:17:36.180
to mystery, more open to awe-inspiring
experiences, we can experience huge boosts

01:17:36.190 --> 01:17:40.790
in well being, and we can become a lot
more resilient in the face of change.

01:17:40.799 --> 01:17:43.560
So that's something that I'm working
on, which is like, okay, maybe I can

01:17:43.560 --> 01:17:46.140
reduce my need for cognitive closure.

01:17:46.550 --> 01:17:56.130
And the other thing that I am starting
to appreciate is one reason that we

01:17:56.130 --> 01:18:01.019
get change wrong and we maybe fear
it more than we should, is that when

01:18:01.020 --> 01:18:05.680
we anticipate what a change will be
like in the future, we tend to imagine

01:18:05.870 --> 01:18:10.009
how our present day selves will
respond to that future change, right?

01:18:10.010 --> 01:18:11.960
So it's almost like a magic mirror.

01:18:11.960 --> 01:18:16.240
It's Maya in present day, going
through this mirror, comes out

01:18:16.240 --> 01:18:18.180
the other side two years from now.

01:18:18.180 --> 01:18:21.270
She's the one who's overcoming
the challenges of a diagnosis

01:18:21.270 --> 01:18:22.450
or some other life change.

01:18:23.060 --> 01:18:27.190
And what we forget is that the
big changes in our lives can

01:18:27.190 --> 01:18:30.530
change us in pretty profound ways.

01:18:31.500 --> 01:18:34.870
And when we recognize and we
all fall prey to this illusion.

01:18:34.990 --> 01:18:36.370
It's called the end of history illusion.

01:18:36.379 --> 01:18:37.610
So, this is work by Dan Gilbert.

01:18:37.620 --> 01:18:41.200
And basically what it says is
we fully acknowledge that we've

01:18:41.210 --> 01:18:43.360
changed considerably in the past.

01:18:43.380 --> 01:18:45.510
So you think back to your
skateboard days, right?

01:18:45.510 --> 01:18:48.180
I think back to my high school
days, and I think, oh, my

01:18:48.180 --> 01:18:49.250
gosh, of course I've changed.

01:18:49.660 --> 01:18:51.890
I would be embarrassed to
listen to any interview I gave

01:18:52.420 --> 01:18:53.530
when I was younger, right?

01:18:53.539 --> 01:18:55.210
Like, what were the thoughts
I was even thinking?

01:18:55.870 --> 01:18:57.450
So we will see it, absolutely.

01:18:57.710 --> 01:19:00.480
We were totally different
ten years ago, 20 years ago.

01:19:01.060 --> 01:19:03.830
But when it comes to thinking about
the future and projecting into the

01:19:03.830 --> 01:19:08.100
future, we are absolutely convinced that
who we are right now, in this moment

01:19:08.250 --> 01:19:09.570
is the person that's here to stay.

01:19:10.480 --> 01:19:15.419
And that can lead us astray when
it comes to thinking about how we

01:19:15.420 --> 01:19:18.030
will respond to change, because we
forget that there's actually a lot

01:19:18.030 --> 01:19:20.250
of wiggle room around who we become.

01:19:20.599 --> 01:19:23.760
And to your point, I mean, I love
the point you made about curiosity.

01:19:23.969 --> 01:19:28.640
What that means is we want to be
curious, not just about the things we do.

01:19:28.640 --> 01:19:30.399
We want to be curious about ourselves.

01:19:31.680 --> 01:19:35.840
One huge lesson that I've learned
from the interviews that I've had

01:19:35.840 --> 01:19:39.569
on A Slight Change of Plans is that
I need to constantly be auditing

01:19:39.570 --> 01:19:44.070
myself through my change experience
to figure out how I have changed.

01:19:44.530 --> 01:19:48.349
Because when we experience change,
it doesn't happen in a vacuum.

01:19:48.620 --> 01:19:52.360
So let's say I get a promotion or I
enter into a relationship, or I leave

01:19:52.390 --> 01:19:57.290
a relationship or some other, again,
narrow slice of my life is altered.

01:19:57.950 --> 01:20:00.820
We can think of that change as
happening in a vacuum, right?

01:20:01.150 --> 01:20:05.709
As being confined to just the unique
area of our life that change exists in.

01:20:06.109 --> 01:20:09.469
But, of course, we are
incredibly complex creatures.

01:20:09.720 --> 01:20:11.700
Our psychology is incredibly complex.

01:20:11.730 --> 01:20:14.150
We live in these remarkably
complex ecosystems.

01:20:15.260 --> 01:20:18.600
Change in one area of our life will
inevitably have spillover effects into

01:20:18.750 --> 01:20:22.459
all other parts of our lives in ways
that are extremely hard to predict.

01:20:24.610 --> 01:20:27.090
I think a lot of your listeners are
familiar with the research showing we're

01:20:27.280 --> 01:20:28.990
really bad cognitive forecasters, right?

01:20:28.990 --> 01:20:32.139
We're bad at predicting what's going
to make us happy, what's going to make

01:20:32.139 --> 01:20:35.050
us sad, how long we're going to be
sad, how long we're going to be happy.

01:20:35.240 --> 01:20:40.140
Well, one of the reasons for that is
that we forget that we are a dynamic

01:20:40.140 --> 01:20:41.799
entity that might change as well.

01:20:42.380 --> 01:20:45.720
That our preferences might change,
our choice, that might change,

01:20:45.990 --> 01:20:48.980
we might change in these really
profound ways that we don't realize.

01:20:50.770 --> 01:20:54.780
I think there's an inspiring message
coming out of this, which is, one, what

01:20:54.780 --> 01:20:58.310
we're capable of right now really might
not be what we're capable of later,

01:20:59.230 --> 01:21:05.849
and what I found in my own experience
is that it's interesting when it

01:21:05.850 --> 01:21:10.080
comes to our self-perception, because
we have a first person perspective

01:21:10.080 --> 01:21:13.889
on who we are, we tend to think
that we have a very comprehensive,

01:21:14.090 --> 01:21:15.620
veridical understanding of who we are.

01:21:15.799 --> 01:21:19.430
Like, I have a pretty good grasp of who
I, Maya, am and what I'm capable of and

01:21:19.560 --> 01:21:22.049
what I value and what my identity is.

01:21:22.960 --> 01:21:29.240
But the reality is that that understanding
is based on the random set of data

01:21:29.240 --> 01:21:33.390
points that I've happened to collect
over the course of my lifetime, based

01:21:33.390 --> 01:21:38.130
on the random set of experiences
and opportunities and failures and

01:21:38.130 --> 01:21:39.585
successes that I've happened to have.

01:21:39.890 --> 01:21:40.200
Right?

01:21:40.670 --> 01:21:45.230
Andrew Huberman: And if I'm not
mistaken, there's a salience to the

01:21:45.230 --> 01:21:49.570
negative experiences, often for reasons
that make sense according to nervous

01:21:49.570 --> 01:21:51.419
systems that want to keep us safe, etc.

01:21:51.730 --> 01:21:54.499
But, for instance, you remember
the name of this child prodigy--

01:21:54.820 --> 01:21:55.470
Maya Shankar: --Rachel Lee.

01:21:55.490 --> 01:21:55.530
[LAUGHS]

01:21:55.679 --> 01:21:56.210
Andrew Huberman: Rachel Lee.

01:21:56.210 --> 01:21:59.630
[LAUGHS] My sister still talks about,
I won't say their names because we know

01:21:59.630 --> 01:22:04.640
that these people are still around,
fortunately, the names of some of the

01:22:04.640 --> 01:22:10.200
girls in junior high school that were
particularly popular and perhaps not--

01:22:10.200 --> 01:22:11.287
Maya Shankar: --You mean Kellen Lindsay?

01:22:11.287 --> 01:22:11.304
[LAUGHS]

01:22:11.304 --> 01:22:13.810
Andrew Huberman: [LAUGHS] Yeah, perhaps
not kind to her, right, exactly.

01:22:14.280 --> 01:22:15.530
Maya Shankar: Were they nice to me?

01:22:15.530 --> 01:22:16.850
Not super nice, but it's okay.

01:22:17.160 --> 01:22:17.330
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

01:22:17.330 --> 01:22:19.620
There's a lot of web searching
nowadays for what these

01:22:19.620 --> 01:22:21.159
people are up to now anyway.

01:22:22.120 --> 01:22:23.960
Not by me, anyway.

01:22:24.020 --> 01:22:24.650
I have a sister.

01:22:24.650 --> 01:22:26.320
We occasionally touch into this.

01:22:26.490 --> 01:22:27.160
She's doing great.

01:22:27.170 --> 01:22:32.640
Fortunately, there's a salience
to the negative experiences.

01:22:32.640 --> 01:22:37.650
But I think what I'm hearing, and
I totally agree with, is that we'd

01:22:37.650 --> 01:22:42.190
like to think that we have complete
or at least adequate self-knowledge,

01:22:42.630 --> 01:22:44.089
but that we likely don't.

01:22:44.710 --> 01:22:49.150
What are some of the ways that we
can get better data on ourselves

01:22:49.150 --> 01:22:50.450
in ways that can help us?

01:22:50.590 --> 01:22:54.830
Is that through the
application of mentorship?

01:22:55.180 --> 01:23:00.849
Is it asking people for an honest
assessment of us with, of course, the

01:23:00.849 --> 01:23:02.429
willingness to hear what they have to say?

01:23:04.109 --> 01:23:08.940
What are some of the, I love zero cost
behavioral, but what are some of the zero

01:23:08.940 --> 01:23:14.659
cost behavioral sources that people have
around them in order to ask these, what

01:23:14.660 --> 01:23:16.969
I think are really fundamental questions.

01:23:17.380 --> 01:23:17.540
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

01:23:17.540 --> 01:23:21.829
So there's two information asymmetries,
let's say, that we're trying to

01:23:21.830 --> 01:23:25.120
solve for, s o two areas where we
might not have full knowledge of

01:23:25.120 --> 01:23:26.420
who we are for one of two reasons.

01:23:26.420 --> 01:23:32.129
So, one is that we have an incomplete
understanding of who we are just based

01:23:32.130 --> 01:23:33.589
on the random set of experiences.

01:23:33.610 --> 01:23:38.420
And the second is that going through this
big change actually alters us in some way.

01:23:38.540 --> 01:23:41.510
Okay, so if we're trying to solve for
the, I think the second problem is

01:23:41.510 --> 01:23:46.370
actually easier to solve for in that
we often just don't even know to look

01:23:46.910 --> 01:23:49.880
inwards during a big change to see
how we've changed because we think,

01:23:50.120 --> 01:23:52.900
oh, I'll just pay attention to how I'm
performing at work because that was the

01:23:52.900 --> 01:23:54.660
new variable that was thrown into my life.

01:23:54.880 --> 01:23:57.354
And we forget to evaluate
other parts of our lives.

01:23:57.509 --> 01:23:59.890
Like, what impact has this
had on my relationship?

01:23:59.949 --> 01:24:02.540
What impact has this had
on my overall well being?

01:24:02.810 --> 01:24:03.450
Am I different?

01:24:03.450 --> 01:24:04.720
Do I have a different set of preferences?

01:24:04.720 --> 01:24:05.780
Do I care about different things?

01:24:05.790 --> 01:24:11.160
So, in the second category, become very
inquisitive about who you are over a

01:24:11.160 --> 01:24:15.699
longer time frame and assume that it's not
a static state when it comes to the first

01:24:16.169 --> 01:24:21.230
bucket, which is how do we develop a more
complete and richer understanding of self?

01:24:22.370 --> 01:24:24.916
I think it's actually about
surrounding yourself with a diverse

01:24:24.916 --> 01:24:28.490
set of people, people that you
wouldn't naturally gravitate towards.

01:24:28.520 --> 01:24:31.199
I think this solves for a bunch of
social ills, which is that, again, we

01:24:31.200 --> 01:24:34.710
tend to live in our silos, and we're
really averse to talking to people

01:24:34.710 --> 01:24:35.940
who have different points of view.

01:24:36.219 --> 01:24:40.190
But I will tell you, at times I've
learned the most about myself, I've

01:24:40.190 --> 01:24:44.910
learned the most about my weaknesses and
sometimes my strengths from talking with

01:24:44.910 --> 01:24:46.469
someone that I vehemently disagree with.

01:24:47.190 --> 01:24:48.829
And it's a really hard thing to do.

01:24:49.059 --> 01:24:50.240
It's very painful.

01:24:50.509 --> 01:24:54.039
But in terms of edifying experiences go.

01:24:54.449 --> 01:24:56.800
It's through those conversations
that I almost see this

01:24:56.830 --> 01:24:58.100
mirror reflected back on me.

01:24:58.330 --> 01:25:01.890
Like, wow, I'm much more aware of
how I'm coming across to that person

01:25:01.900 --> 01:25:04.340
because they disagree with me about
something or they're not someone

01:25:04.340 --> 01:25:06.090
I would normally fraternize with.

01:25:06.500 --> 01:25:09.930
And it's just bred more
self-awareness in me.

01:25:09.969 --> 01:25:12.940
And so I would encourage people
to actually seek out connections

01:25:13.400 --> 01:25:17.580
in uncomfortable spaces because
that will allow you to fill

01:25:17.580 --> 01:25:18.500
in at least some of the gaps.

01:25:18.530 --> 01:25:21.469
Now, some of the gaps will
truly only be revealed to you

01:25:21.670 --> 01:25:23.080
because of life experiences.

01:25:23.099 --> 01:25:26.889
So, I'm thinking in my own
life, I thought I grieved in

01:25:27.030 --> 01:25:28.489
a very particular kind of way.

01:25:29.199 --> 01:25:33.600
And then during COVID my husband
and I experienced multiple pregnancy

01:25:33.600 --> 01:25:37.630
losses with our surrogate, and
I found myself grieving in a way

01:25:37.630 --> 01:25:39.400
that was completely foreign to me.

01:25:39.590 --> 01:25:42.020
I don't think talking to anyone
would have revealed to me that I was

01:25:42.020 --> 01:25:45.300
going to grieve in this way where
usually I would reach out to people

01:25:45.300 --> 01:25:46.340
and I would want to stay connected.

01:25:46.340 --> 01:25:49.370
And I became so shut off and closed
off, and I didn't want to talk to

01:25:49.370 --> 01:25:51.480
anyone for days after the losses.

01:25:51.480 --> 01:25:53.760
I was so disoriented there.

01:25:53.760 --> 01:25:58.920
I learned, oh, actually, you can respond
in a diverse set of ways to grief.

01:25:59.360 --> 01:26:01.650
You don't have a singular experience
with grief, but I might have

01:26:01.650 --> 01:26:04.479
only learned that from the actual
experience of confronting it.

01:26:05.350 --> 01:26:08.880
That said, I do think there's a
lot of value in trying to fill in

01:26:08.880 --> 01:26:13.210
gaps in knowledge or self-awareness
through these more quotidian

01:26:13.210 --> 01:26:14.570
conversations you have with people.

01:26:15.590 --> 01:26:21.370
Andrew Huberman: I love, love, love
what you said about deliberately placing

01:26:21.400 --> 01:26:26.450
oneself into environments where we receive
critical feedback from people that we

01:26:26.450 --> 01:26:31.140
view as quite disparate from us, at
least in terms of our experience of them.

01:26:32.590 --> 01:26:36.239
It was the great Karl Deisseroth, another
incredibly accomplished neuroscientist,

01:26:37.940 --> 01:26:42.660
happens to be a colleague of mine at
Stanford who, he's a psychiatrist, and he

01:26:42.660 --> 01:26:46.740
said, you know, we think we know how other
people feel, but we really have no idea

01:26:46.740 --> 01:26:48.750
how other people feel unless we ask them.

01:26:48.930 --> 01:26:51.080
In fact, most of the time we don't
even really know how we feel.

01:26:51.540 --> 01:26:53.540
We're not very good at
gauging our own emotions.

01:26:53.550 --> 01:26:55.750
So credit to Karl for
making that statement.

01:26:55.750 --> 01:27:01.210
But with that said, I think getting
a sense of how other people see

01:27:01.210 --> 01:27:06.690
us, and disagreement in particular,
can be incredibly informative.

01:27:06.700 --> 01:27:10.130
Maya Shankar: I just want to say one other
point on this, which is I think getting

01:27:10.130 --> 01:27:13.249
feedback from others almost gets a bad
rap these days in society, because it's

01:27:13.250 --> 01:27:17.099
like you should only care about who you
are inside, who you know yourself to be.

01:27:17.449 --> 01:27:19.290
And I'm like, dude, we
are social creatures.

01:27:19.309 --> 01:27:22.360
It absolutely matters
how I come off to others.

01:27:22.570 --> 01:27:26.120
I think that should be a huge
part of my self-identity,

01:27:26.349 --> 01:27:27.679
should be how I impact others.

01:27:28.300 --> 01:27:31.530
And I think we should be
shameless about integrating that

01:27:31.730 --> 01:27:32.969
into our understanding of self.

01:27:32.980 --> 01:27:37.450
If I feel like I'm an excellent person
inside and I'm regularly wounding

01:27:37.460 --> 01:27:41.809
the people around me, that matters,
that's relevant to how I see myself.

01:27:41.809 --> 01:27:45.360
And so I do worry sometimes with the
current cultural climate that we're

01:27:45.360 --> 01:27:48.460
pushing ourselves so much towards
the space of, like, all that matters

01:27:48.460 --> 01:27:50.530
is authenticity and being yourself.

01:27:51.000 --> 01:27:52.750
First of all, sometimes
yourself isn't awesome.

01:27:52.750 --> 01:27:55.190
You might want to actually
optimize or change some things

01:27:55.570 --> 01:27:56.750
about yourself to be better.

01:27:56.750 --> 01:27:57.690
I think that's a good thing.

01:27:57.860 --> 01:28:01.480
And then, second, it's okay to
care what other people think.

01:28:01.670 --> 01:28:04.670
Usually they're great barometers of
things that you might not be aware of

01:28:04.730 --> 01:28:05.969
in terms of the impact you're having.

01:28:05.969 --> 01:28:09.450
So I just want to be a lobbyist
for caring what other people

01:28:09.460 --> 01:28:11.139
think, just for a moment.

01:28:11.190 --> 01:28:12.050
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, I agree.

01:28:12.299 --> 01:28:14.990
This is one of the reasons why I say
at the end of every episode that I

01:28:14.990 --> 01:28:16.330
do read all the comments on YouTube.

01:28:16.400 --> 01:28:16.650
Maya Shankar: Me too!

01:28:17.690 --> 01:28:20.630
Andrew Huberman: I think I was
raised in a culture, an academic

01:28:20.630 --> 01:28:25.510
culture, where feedback on lectures,
student feedback, was critical.

01:28:25.510 --> 01:28:31.480
I mean, it is important, I believe,
to be a selective filter, because in

01:28:31.480 --> 01:28:34.910
the old days, we'll say, there was
an opportunity to map the statements

01:28:34.910 --> 01:28:37.570
to the grade that the student
received, you can no longer do this.

01:28:37.900 --> 01:28:43.370
So you would often see that some of the
worst feedback was, "hated, unclear"...

01:28:43.809 --> 01:28:44.329
Maya Shankar: Exactly.

01:28:44.469 --> 01:28:47.710
Andrew Huberman: And then you'd look at
their grade, and you'd say, well, okay,

01:28:47.710 --> 01:28:52.700
this helps explain, and yet it was also
important to understand where that could

01:28:52.700 --> 01:28:54.850
have represented some failings on my part.

01:28:54.860 --> 01:28:58.270
And a classroom is but one environment.

01:28:58.270 --> 01:29:01.950
I think the online environment is
where this gets tricky because of

01:29:01.950 --> 01:29:07.619
the way that we all differ in our
capacity to receive critical feedback.

01:29:08.240 --> 01:29:14.220
And sometimes the harshness of one
form of feedback sends people feeling

01:29:14.590 --> 01:29:19.009
back on their heels or feeling even
ego or emotionally injured in ways

01:29:19.009 --> 01:29:20.769
that they actually feel traumatic.

01:29:21.130 --> 01:29:28.420
And I think that's part of the problem, is
that we don't really have a way to gauge,

01:29:28.740 --> 01:29:32.860
I mean, we know inappropriate when we see
it, we know appropriate when we see it.

01:29:32.860 --> 01:29:35.289
But all the stuff in between,
because it's on a continuum,

01:29:35.290 --> 01:29:38.250
really is where it gets tricky.

01:29:39.080 --> 01:29:43.490
I certainly think integrating the
possibility that somebody might be right,

01:29:43.730 --> 01:29:47.710
what is it that they say in certain
forms of personal developments, like, if

01:29:47.710 --> 01:29:50.960
somebody's coming at you with an argument
about you, the best state of mind you

01:29:50.960 --> 01:29:54.260
could have is you might be right, because
that lets you hold your ground a bit.

01:29:54.670 --> 01:29:57.839
It still maintains a boundary, but
you're not saying you're right,

01:29:57.839 --> 01:29:58.920
and you're not saying you're wrong.

01:29:59.359 --> 01:30:04.230
You're in a kind of a flat footed
stance where you could move either way.

01:30:04.450 --> 01:30:04.950
And I like that.

01:30:04.950 --> 01:30:08.730
This idea of, well, they might be
right, and then you could say no or yes.

01:30:08.730 --> 01:30:13.160
But in any case, I just want to
throw up both hands and as many

01:30:13.790 --> 01:30:17.919
votes as I can, as one individual
to say yes, I totally agree.

01:30:20.520 --> 01:30:24.830
More direct feedback and
disagreement is great.

01:30:24.930 --> 01:30:25.280
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

01:30:25.290 --> 01:30:25.920
Andrew Huberman: It's wonderful.

01:30:26.040 --> 01:30:29.519
And I think in science, you're used
to people saying harsh things about

01:30:29.520 --> 01:30:31.960
your work until they eventually say,
okay, you c an publish the paper.

01:30:31.970 --> 01:30:32.390
Maya Shankar: [LAUGHS] That is true.

01:30:32.549 --> 01:30:34.230
Andrew Huberman: I grew up in the
culture of skateboarding, where

01:30:34.389 --> 01:30:37.250
nothing's good enough, and then
occasionally something's good.

01:30:38.370 --> 01:30:43.240
And in the landscape of podcasting, I
think the comment section is a great

01:30:43.240 --> 01:30:47.170
way to get feedback, and that's why
I continue to encourage feedback.

01:30:47.170 --> 01:30:48.070
It sounds like you do as well.

01:30:48.070 --> 01:30:52.289
Maya Shankar: Yeah, I think, every
endeavor that I pursue, I try to

01:30:52.289 --> 01:30:53.559
approach with a lot of humility.

01:30:53.990 --> 01:30:58.740
And I think if I were to describe, at
work, right, I lead this team, and I

01:30:58.750 --> 01:31:03.250
think if you were to ask people what
my defining trait is as a leader, it's

01:31:03.250 --> 01:31:04.920
actually not, like, strong convictions.

01:31:04.940 --> 01:31:09.000
It's actually a willingness to
update her opinions on things,

01:31:09.150 --> 01:31:12.920
her belief systems, her strategy
based on incoming information.

01:31:12.920 --> 01:31:17.209
I really, really pride myself on
having a flexible mindset about

01:31:17.210 --> 01:31:18.580
stuff and not being stubborn.

01:31:18.840 --> 01:31:20.280
This is true in my marriage.

01:31:20.400 --> 01:31:23.469
Like, my husband Jimmy and I
really pride ourselves, you

01:31:23.469 --> 01:31:26.050
know, saying, you know what?

01:31:26.549 --> 01:31:28.769
Based on what you just
shared, I'm changing my mind.

01:31:29.209 --> 01:31:30.730
Like, you're right and I'm wrong.

01:31:31.389 --> 01:31:33.880
And if you can actually start to
value that, if you could start to

01:31:33.890 --> 01:31:35.980
see that as a virtuous quality.

01:31:36.730 --> 01:31:40.139
I think historically, when we think
about leadership, we've thought about

01:31:40.139 --> 01:31:44.419
people who are incredibly resolute in
their convictions, but that doesn't allow

01:31:44.420 --> 01:31:48.250
the space to, again, beige an update.

01:31:49.340 --> 01:31:53.050
Update your mindset when you get new
information or you realize that you

01:31:53.060 --> 01:31:55.790
erred in some way in terms of the
logic that you used or what have you.

01:31:56.210 --> 01:32:00.260
And I've been extremely intentional
in every sphere that I've worked in

01:32:00.280 --> 01:32:04.549
to have this very open mind and to
be very open to critical feedback.

01:32:04.559 --> 01:32:08.050
It does not mean that I take
every piece of feedback.

01:32:08.240 --> 01:32:12.980
Okay, obviously I have some criteria I
use to decide whether it's meaningful

01:32:12.990 --> 01:32:14.660
feedback or it's not meaningful feedback.

01:32:15.679 --> 01:32:23.089
But the locus of my pride is not in being
right or having the strong conviction.

01:32:23.219 --> 01:32:28.230
It is actually in my willingness
to have a more dynamic state of

01:32:28.230 --> 01:32:29.780
mind regarding lots of issues.

01:32:30.640 --> 01:32:32.860
Maybe that's just what it
means to be a scientist, right?

01:32:32.860 --> 01:32:36.140
Like, you have to be willing to
update in the face of new information.

01:32:37.190 --> 01:32:39.410
Andrew Huberman: I am nodding
for those that are listening.

01:32:39.410 --> 01:32:42.800
I'm just nodding and
thinking yes and more yes.

01:32:42.840 --> 01:32:46.320
Because I think that we all need
more of that as individuals.

01:32:46.320 --> 01:32:49.470
And if we can't get it from our work
setting or group setting, sometimes

01:32:49.530 --> 01:32:52.530
asking a friend can be extremely useful.

01:32:52.530 --> 01:32:53.250
I have a friend.

01:32:53.250 --> 01:32:56.459
He happens to be a professor
at a university back east.

01:32:56.460 --> 01:33:01.790
I won't embarrass him by disclosing
where he's at, but I recall as a junior

01:33:01.790 --> 01:33:03.880
faculty member because he knows me well.

01:33:04.210 --> 01:33:06.139
He's a few years behind me
in our career trajectories.

01:33:06.180 --> 01:33:09.150
But asking him for an honest
assessment, I asked for the most

01:33:09.160 --> 01:33:13.020
brutally honest assessment of me that
he could give, and some of it stung.

01:33:13.389 --> 01:33:14.389
Some of it stung.

01:33:14.490 --> 01:33:18.469
He was relating some ways in which I show
up as a friend and I'm super present.

01:33:18.510 --> 01:33:20.830
Then I have this tendency, I'm
pretty introverted, I'll disappear

01:33:20.830 --> 01:33:21.610
for long periods of time.

01:33:22.290 --> 01:33:24.470
In college, they called me Dart
because I'd show up at parties.

01:33:24.470 --> 01:33:26.630
I'd be there, and then I would disappear
for like two weeks and just be in my

01:33:26.630 --> 01:33:30.630
books, say hi to people and just keep
going sort of in and out of connection.

01:33:31.230 --> 01:33:33.559
I've worked hard to change
that over the years.

01:33:34.660 --> 01:33:35.729
I think I have.

01:33:35.760 --> 01:33:36.329
But who knows?

01:33:37.290 --> 01:33:40.610
In any event, a friend who knows
us well that you insist on.

01:33:40.610 --> 01:33:42.220
All right, don't give me any compliments.

01:33:42.690 --> 01:33:46.009
Just give me the harsh stuff
that can be very useful.

01:33:46.360 --> 01:33:49.779
Maya Shankar: And that reminds me
of some research by Ethan Cross.

01:33:49.790 --> 01:33:52.420
So he looks at how we can
tame our mental chatter.

01:33:52.900 --> 01:33:59.190
And if you don't have the friend available
to you, there is a really easy distancing

01:33:59.190 --> 01:34:02.240
technique that you can use when you're
in the throes of a problem where you are

01:34:02.240 --> 01:34:06.410
trying to actively reframe something or
maybe see where your blind spots are.

01:34:06.770 --> 01:34:10.599
And that's by thinking about your
problem from a third person perspective

01:34:10.609 --> 01:34:12.230
versus a first person perspective.

01:34:12.240 --> 01:34:16.210
So you play the role of someone who's
giving advice to a friend in your head,

01:34:16.340 --> 01:34:20.540
but that friend is actually you, and
it actually promotes some degree of

01:34:20.559 --> 01:34:28.049
objectivity and emotional distance from,
again, that fuzzy, hazy set of feelings

01:34:28.059 --> 01:34:29.069
that you have around the emotion.

01:34:29.240 --> 01:34:33.150
You're trying to get rid of that piece
so that you can bring a slightly more

01:34:33.150 --> 01:34:35.410
sober recommendation to the situation.

01:34:35.410 --> 01:34:36.530
So that can be really helpful.

01:34:36.850 --> 01:34:42.170
And then the other thing to do is, I
think, when we're facing challenges, when

01:34:42.170 --> 01:34:46.400
we're going through a hard time, we do
have an instinct to want to vent, right?

01:34:46.590 --> 01:34:49.130
Again, in this era of vulnerability
and whatnot, we're told, like, yes,

01:34:49.260 --> 01:34:50.700
share everything that's on your mind.

01:34:51.370 --> 01:34:53.110
It can actually be
counterproductive to vent.

01:34:53.260 --> 01:34:57.259
And the reason for that is that
when you're venting about a hard

01:34:57.260 --> 01:34:59.770
situation that you're going through, or
something that you're frustrated about

01:34:59.770 --> 01:35:04.740
with yourself, typically the person
you've invited into the conversation,

01:35:04.770 --> 01:35:06.389
they're a nice, empathetic person.

01:35:06.600 --> 01:35:07.800
They want to make you feel better.

01:35:08.190 --> 01:35:09.170
And so what do they do?

01:35:09.170 --> 01:35:11.190
They offer emotional
balm in the situation.

01:35:11.190 --> 01:35:15.170
They're like, oh my God, that does
sound terrible, you were so wronged.

01:35:15.200 --> 01:35:19.780
I'm so sorry you went through that,
instead of playing the role of what

01:35:19.790 --> 01:35:24.069
Ethan calls like, a cognitive advisor,
which is actively trying to challenge

01:35:24.090 --> 01:35:27.350
the narrative you're telling about
your situation, actively trying to

01:35:27.350 --> 01:35:31.730
get you to question whether the way
you're portraying the situation is

01:35:31.730 --> 01:35:35.710
accurate, and actively trying to get
you to reframe aspects of the situation.

01:35:36.030 --> 01:35:39.840
And so when we think about venting, when
it comes to, again, filling in those blind

01:35:39.840 --> 01:35:43.800
spots about ourselves, you might want
to tell your friend at the outset, like,

01:35:43.800 --> 01:35:47.110
you even said, lay off the nice stuff.

01:35:47.110 --> 01:35:49.950
I just want to hear the hard stuff you
want to tell your friend at the beginning.

01:35:50.250 --> 01:35:53.589
Look, I'm having this challenge with my
colleague at work, where this guy at the

01:35:53.590 --> 01:35:54.910
gym is giving me a really tough time.

01:35:54.910 --> 01:35:55.820
I don't know what's going on.

01:35:56.770 --> 01:35:58.520
Here's the situation.

01:35:58.779 --> 01:36:02.950
Rather than trying to make me feel
better about the situation, I want

01:36:02.950 --> 01:36:07.389
you to actively find holes, poke holes
in the way that I'm thinking about

01:36:07.390 --> 01:36:11.600
this thing so that I can try and find
some reframing strategies to see the

01:36:11.600 --> 01:36:13.219
situation from a different vantage point.

01:36:13.400 --> 01:36:14.910
So these are all called
distancing techniques.

01:36:15.170 --> 01:36:16.649
Third person versus first person.

01:36:17.049 --> 01:36:19.940
And actually, there's some really
interesting neuroscience research

01:36:19.940 --> 01:36:23.679
showing that when we view our
problems in ourselves from a third

01:36:23.680 --> 01:36:28.670
person perspective, neural activity
in areas associated with hostility

01:36:28.670 --> 01:36:30.089
and aggression actually decrease.

01:36:31.099 --> 01:36:33.839
And so that can be really helpful
when it comes to resolving

01:36:33.849 --> 01:36:36.550
interpersonal conflict or trying to
see where you might have been wrong.

01:36:37.300 --> 01:36:39.490
Andrew Huberman: I love these
examples because especially the

01:36:39.490 --> 01:36:43.130
one where one does it on their own,
it truly doesn't require anything.

01:36:43.140 --> 01:36:45.210
Maya Shankar: You can be the
introverted Andrew and still do this.

01:36:45.230 --> 01:36:47.629
You don't even have to go to the
party and then ghost everyone.

01:36:47.639 --> 01:36:50.500
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, well,
back then, there were no cell

01:36:50.500 --> 01:36:52.340
phones or smartphones, rather.

01:36:52.340 --> 01:36:55.670
But, yeah, it was a bit of ghosting.

01:36:56.060 --> 01:37:02.400
I can reset with small numbers of people
that I'm close to, but I found at that

01:37:02.400 --> 01:37:07.490
time a need to go into an isolated space
to do what I need to do to reset myself.

01:37:08.280 --> 01:37:11.150
But I realized there are certain forms
of communication that are still required.

01:37:11.469 --> 01:37:12.559
Like, I'm alive.

01:37:13.180 --> 01:37:15.910
I still get this from my
mother every once in a while.

01:37:15.910 --> 01:37:20.070
She's like, if you don't reach out, not
only do I not know what's happening with

01:37:20.080 --> 01:37:21.700
you, but I also don't know if you're okay.

01:37:21.700 --> 01:37:22.639
And I'm thinking, I'm a grown man.

01:37:22.639 --> 01:37:23.520
Of course I'm fine.

01:37:24.070 --> 01:37:27.480
And then I, of course, use the worst
possible response that any son or

01:37:27.480 --> 01:37:30.510
child could give, which is, listen,
if something happened to me, someone

01:37:30.580 --> 01:37:31.839
like the police would contact you--

01:37:31.839 --> 01:37:32.342
Maya Shankar: They'll let you know.

01:37:32.342 --> 01:37:32.514
[LAUGHS]

01:37:32.514 --> 01:37:34.980
Andrew Huberman: --or the hospital would
contact you, which is not reassuring.

01:37:35.380 --> 01:37:37.130
So, kids everywhere, call your parents.

01:37:38.150 --> 01:37:38.620
Maya Shankar: I know.

01:37:38.620 --> 01:37:39.179
J ust call your poor mother Andrew.

01:37:39.179 --> 01:37:40.540
Just call her a bit more, come on.

01:37:40.550 --> 01:37:40.590
[LAUGHS]

01:37:40.590 --> 01:37:40.606
Andrew Huberman: I know, I know.

01:37:40.610 --> 01:37:42.320
Well, still working on it.

01:37:42.320 --> 01:37:43.429
It is a work in progress.

01:37:43.429 --> 01:37:43.614
Venting.

01:37:43.799 --> 01:37:49.350
I'm so glad that you brought this up.

01:37:50.160 --> 01:37:56.639
I think that there are these buzzwords
now, authenticity, I do think that there

01:37:56.639 --> 01:38:03.910
are certain forms of communication that
can be injurious to people, and yet I

01:38:03.920 --> 01:38:09.509
think having some internal buffers to
all that incoming stuff is important.

01:38:09.540 --> 01:38:12.139
I mean, you can't be online, and
I think everyone is pretty much

01:38:12.140 --> 01:38:16.769
online these days, without having
some policies for oneself and how

01:38:16.769 --> 01:38:18.120
you're going to deal with this stuff.

01:38:18.130 --> 01:38:19.759
How am I going to be a selective filter?

01:38:19.790 --> 01:38:22.202
I think knowing the ends of the continuum
like, this is clearly benevolent,

01:38:22.202 --> 01:38:23.530
kind, discourse, this is clearly bad.

01:38:24.219 --> 01:38:32.270
I'm going to block this or get rid of
it, but then within that middle range,

01:38:32.330 --> 01:38:36.320
having some rules and policies for how
to filter it, either by time of day

01:38:36.320 --> 01:38:38.150
that you look at it or getting input.

01:38:38.790 --> 01:38:44.620
But considering it might be true, it
might not be true what people are saying.

01:38:44.850 --> 01:38:47.010
Maya Shankar: And like you said, you
were talking about memory and how we

01:38:47.010 --> 01:38:50.360
tend to over weight negative experiences.

01:38:50.780 --> 01:38:55.809
And I did find myself like, so I gave
this speech and it was posted, and

01:38:55.809 --> 01:39:00.640
I was looking at the comments, and
anytime I brain coded a comment as

01:39:00.650 --> 01:39:02.260
positive, I just skipped right past it.

01:39:02.690 --> 01:39:05.250
I was literally just searching
for the n egative stuff.

01:39:05.600 --> 01:39:08.190
Andrew Huberman: As if the
positive is generic and the

01:39:08.200 --> 01:39:10.170
negative is somehow genuine.

01:39:10.179 --> 01:39:10.499
Maya Shankar: Yes.

01:39:10.610 --> 01:39:12.840
And I had to make it mental,
I had to make a mental note.

01:39:12.880 --> 01:39:17.950
Hey, it's okay to marinate in the
messages that are saying that this

01:39:17.959 --> 01:39:21.690
really helped them in some way
and they really enjoyed the thing.

01:39:22.820 --> 01:39:25.830
Again, for self-critical people, I
think it takes an extra step to remind

01:39:25.830 --> 01:39:30.310
yourself to also read the good stuff
and to allow that stuff to count, too.

01:39:30.780 --> 01:39:32.880
Andrew Huberman: Well, we did an
episode on gratitude, and one of

01:39:33.700 --> 01:39:37.530
the big surprises that came to me in
researching for that episode was that

01:39:38.800 --> 01:39:42.890
the best evidence for gratitude having
positive effects on neural circuitry,

01:39:43.480 --> 01:39:47.180
neurochemistry comes from when we receive
gratitude as opposed to giving gratitude.

01:39:47.190 --> 01:39:49.599
This is what's often lost in
the discussion about gratitude.

01:39:49.599 --> 01:39:54.240
So all the more incentive to give
gratitude and to be aware of when it's

01:39:54.240 --> 01:39:55.950
coming your way and internalize it.

01:39:56.610 --> 01:39:59.880
There is a small category of people
out there, I think, hopefully small,

01:40:00.120 --> 01:40:05.359
that so bask in positive feedback
that it amplifies their narcissism.

01:40:06.050 --> 01:40:08.989
But it's clear that you are
not one of those people.

01:40:08.990 --> 01:40:11.999
So zero minus one risk of that happenIng.

01:40:12.010 --> 01:40:19.390
I want to talk a little bit about goals
as it relates to motivation, because

01:40:20.020 --> 01:40:24.330
you've done a lot of important work.

01:40:24.790 --> 01:40:28.650
And what I consider is organization
of this, what would otherwise

01:40:28.660 --> 01:40:29.959
be a pretty complex space.

01:40:30.910 --> 01:40:34.500
What is more important to most people
than being motivated and focused and

01:40:34.500 --> 01:40:39.510
excited, hopefully on endeavors that
they enjoy and that inspire delight.

01:40:39.980 --> 01:40:45.000
But tell us about what can not
just initiate, but what can sustain

01:40:45.000 --> 01:40:48.820
motivation, because we've talked about
the dopamine system on this podcast

01:40:49.050 --> 01:40:52.410
many times before, but that's a
pretty reductionist way to look at it.

01:40:52.530 --> 01:40:55.810
And you have a different perspective
that I've really benefited

01:40:55.810 --> 01:40:57.160
from learning a bit about.

01:40:57.400 --> 01:40:57.769
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

01:40:57.790 --> 01:41:00.970
So when it comes to goals, I mean,
it's first important to recognize

01:41:01.000 --> 01:41:03.030
that there's two parts of a goal.

01:41:03.429 --> 01:41:06.420
So there's the way that we define
the goal, and then there's the

01:41:06.420 --> 01:41:07.660
way that we pursue the goal.

01:41:08.410 --> 01:41:12.160
And I think we tend to overlook the
first category, how we define the goal,

01:41:12.410 --> 01:41:17.080
because oftentimes our goals seem like
they should be so obvious to us, right?

01:41:17.570 --> 01:41:18.440
I want to lose weight.

01:41:18.840 --> 01:41:21.840
I want to avoid sleeping late so
that I get a good night's sleep.

01:41:22.059 --> 01:41:23.250
I want to build muscle mass.

01:41:23.250 --> 01:41:23.420
Right?

01:41:23.420 --> 01:41:26.350
Like these are things that just seem like
they should just be intuitive, right?

01:41:27.020 --> 01:41:31.259
But what research and behavioral
science shows is that not all

01:41:31.270 --> 01:41:32.879
goal frames are made equal.

01:41:33.010 --> 01:41:37.290
In fact, really small tweaks to the
way that we frame our goals can have

01:41:37.290 --> 01:41:41.719
an outsized impact on whether or not
we're successful at reaching that goal.

01:41:42.470 --> 01:41:47.410
So one such framing is whether you
frame your goals in terms of an approach

01:41:47.470 --> 01:41:50.550
orientation or an avoidance orientation.

01:41:50.750 --> 01:41:51.700
Let me talk about what this means.

01:41:51.700 --> 01:41:56.459
So an approach orientation would
be I want to eat healthier foods.

01:41:57.199 --> 01:42:00.149
Avoidance would be I want
to avoid unhealthy foods.

01:42:00.580 --> 01:42:04.219
So in the context of, say, your
social life approach would be,

01:42:04.530 --> 01:42:07.460
I want to be in a relationship,
I want to enter a relationship.

01:42:07.880 --> 01:42:10.660
Avoidance would be, I want
to avoid feeling loneliness.

01:42:11.259 --> 01:42:13.540
I want to avoid feeling isolated.

01:42:14.830 --> 01:42:19.570
Now the reason why these two frames
are important to consider is that

01:42:19.570 --> 01:42:23.630
they can have a different impact on
our motivational states, and they can

01:42:23.660 --> 01:42:29.840
also have a different impact on the
emotional response that we have to

01:42:29.840 --> 01:42:31.820
success and failure in these domains.

01:42:32.230 --> 01:42:37.140
So what we tend to find is that when you
frame something in an approach orientation

01:42:49.190 --> 01:42:54.504
way, when you succeed, that success is met
with feelings of pride and accomplishment.

01:42:54.504 --> 01:42:54.522
We find that it leads to a
boost in motivation, it boosts

01:42:54.522 --> 01:42:54.527
endurance, it boosts perseverance.

01:42:54.527 --> 01:42:54.544
When you frame something in terms
of avoidance, success is met

01:42:54.544 --> 01:42:55.920
with feelings of calm and relief.

01:42:56.300 --> 01:42:58.540
So kind of like a wipe the forehead.

01:42:58.540 --> 01:43:01.190
Like, thank goodness I avoided
that calamitous outcome.

01:43:01.190 --> 01:43:03.590
Or thank goodness I avoided
doing that really bad thing.

01:43:03.650 --> 01:43:04.490
Andrew Huberman: Back to neutral?

01:43:04.540 --> 01:43:05.540
Maya Shankar: Yeah, exactly.

01:43:06.040 --> 01:43:10.609
And so it is fine to frame
goals in terms of avoidance.

01:43:10.650 --> 01:43:12.729
And actually, sometimes it's
just personality dependent.

01:43:12.740 --> 01:43:15.840
Like some people are more
driven by fear or they need a

01:43:15.840 --> 01:43:17.380
lot more urgency to drive them.

01:43:17.700 --> 01:43:22.800
But it is important to know that
the approach orientation is,

01:43:22.820 --> 01:43:24.279
on average, more motivating.

01:43:24.389 --> 01:43:27.140
And so you might want to think
of reframing your goal in terms

01:43:27.140 --> 01:43:28.429
of approach versus avoidant.

01:43:28.789 --> 01:43:32.730
The other advantage to approach is that
when you frame something as avoidant.

01:43:33.100 --> 01:43:35.720
I want to avoid doing X,
I want to avoid doing Y.

01:43:36.030 --> 01:43:37.700
It's really hard to measure success.

01:43:38.420 --> 01:43:43.160
It's like, are you really tracking every
time you're tempted by the chocolate chip

01:43:43.160 --> 01:43:45.040
cookie and you don't actually eat it?

01:43:45.429 --> 01:43:46.639
That's really hard to measure.

01:43:46.650 --> 01:43:49.880
And we do better when we can
measure success and failure.

01:43:50.440 --> 01:43:54.680
It's much easier to track the number
of times you approach a salad.

01:43:54.990 --> 01:43:56.370
You approach something that's healthy.

01:43:56.770 --> 01:43:59.210
And so, anyway, so it's really
interesting to see how, again, this

01:43:59.340 --> 01:44:05.170
really subtle shift, and we see this
across the board in behavioral science,

01:44:05.190 --> 01:44:07.389
can have such a big impact on behavior.

01:44:07.400 --> 01:44:10.950
And on this framing thing, I'll
just share one little anecdote

01:44:10.950 --> 01:44:12.440
from my time working in government.

01:44:12.450 --> 01:44:18.290
So we were trying to motivate veterans
to sign up for an employment and

01:44:18.300 --> 01:44:21.400
educational assistance program, so
this is after their years of service.

01:44:21.799 --> 01:44:24.269
And this is a really important
benefit that the government offers

01:44:24.270 --> 01:44:28.429
for free, because the transition
from military to civilian life

01:44:28.440 --> 01:44:32.259
can be very fraught with a lot of
psychological and physical obstacles.

01:44:33.460 --> 01:44:38.009
And so I remember the Department of
Veterans Affairs, they had almost no money

01:44:38.009 --> 01:44:39.930
to fund a marketing program around this.

01:44:39.930 --> 01:44:43.580
They said, Maya and team, we've
got one email that we're going

01:44:43.580 --> 01:44:45.330
to send to vets and have at it.

01:44:45.630 --> 01:44:46.870
But that's all we're working with.

01:44:47.570 --> 01:44:52.859
And my teammates and I ended up changing
just one word in this email message.

01:44:53.290 --> 01:44:57.040
Instead of telling vets that they were
eligible for the program, we simply

01:44:57.040 --> 01:45:00.130
reminded them that they had earned
it through their years of service.

01:45:00.970 --> 01:45:05.299
And that one word change led to a 9%
increase in access to the benefit.

01:45:05.310 --> 01:45:09.650
And it's based on a psychological
principle called the endowment

01:45:09.860 --> 01:45:13.070
effect, which says that we value
things more when we own them or

01:45:13.070 --> 01:45:14.610
in this case, have earned them.

01:45:14.610 --> 01:45:18.620
And so I shared this example only to say,
like, that is such a small change, right?

01:45:19.490 --> 01:45:22.870
But we just know that, again, these
small little tweaks in the way

01:45:22.870 --> 01:45:25.540
that we talk to ourselves, the way
that we frame our goals, can have a

01:45:25.540 --> 01:45:27.170
really big impact on our behavior.

01:45:27.980 --> 01:45:29.669
Andrew Huberman: I'm
fascinated by that result.

01:45:30.090 --> 01:45:33.830
Some people hearing it might think,
okay, 9%, is that really that great?

01:45:33.849 --> 01:45:35.800
But we're talking about a one word change.

01:45:38.020 --> 01:45:39.440
Maya Shankar: And the scale
of the federal government.

01:45:40.450 --> 01:45:40.560
[LAUGHS]

01:45:40.580 --> 01:45:40.780
Andrew Huberman: Right.

01:45:41.710 --> 01:45:42.620
Big organizations.

01:45:42.680 --> 01:45:48.620
Hard to argue that things change
quickly in big organizations.

01:45:48.790 --> 01:45:49.929
Discussion for another time.

01:45:51.170 --> 01:45:54.070
But eligible versus earned.

01:45:54.070 --> 01:45:57.380
I mean, again, I come back to this
possibility that there's something about

01:45:57.630 --> 01:46:04.099
words like "earned" that invoke a verb
state within us that makes us more action

01:46:04.099 --> 01:46:11.170
oriented, similar to being able to see
ourselves in some landscape that can evoke

01:46:11.270 --> 01:46:18.590
delight or awe, as opposed to just seeing
the landscape that evokes delight or awe.

01:46:19.160 --> 01:46:23.940
Yeah, I'm really hung up on this because
I think one of the major challenges, it

01:46:23.940 --> 01:46:30.809
seems, for behavioral change is that most
people do wait for the stick, as opposed

01:46:30.840 --> 01:46:33.459
to feeling into the carrot, so to speak.

01:46:34.429 --> 01:46:38.290
I mean, all you have to do is look at
the enormous number of people who are

01:46:38.290 --> 01:46:43.929
struggling with health related issues for
which there's now a lot of active debate.

01:46:43.929 --> 01:46:45.410
Is it genetically determined?

01:46:45.410 --> 01:46:49.370
And setting all that aside, it's just
very clear that there are a number of

01:46:49.380 --> 01:46:54.200
behavioral things, sunlight, sleep,
exercise, social connection, nutrition

01:46:55.450 --> 01:47:01.099
among them, that there's no pill
for, there's no injection for, there

01:47:01.099 --> 01:47:02.960
is absolutely no replacement for.

01:47:03.170 --> 01:47:06.200
So getting people to change
their behavior is hard.

01:47:06.720 --> 01:47:09.700
Telling people that they're
capable sometimes helps,

01:47:10.270 --> 01:47:12.090
but doesn't seem sufficient.

01:47:12.540 --> 01:47:18.290
So what are some more of these verb
states that people you think can

01:47:18.599 --> 01:47:23.589
internalize that give them access
to the real sense of possibility and

01:47:23.590 --> 01:47:25.360
get them changing their behavior?

01:47:25.500 --> 01:47:27.840
Maya Shankar: Yeah, and
behavior change is very hard.

01:47:28.070 --> 01:47:31.995
I sometimes bristle at some of the hacks
that I see online because I'm like, I

01:47:31.995 --> 01:47:35.469
don't think there's a lot of evidence
that supports that this works, so, you

01:47:35.469 --> 01:47:39.360
know, what I'm sharing today is actually
backed by really high quality research.

01:47:39.730 --> 01:47:44.880
One of my friends and mentors, Ayelet
Fishbach, has done a lot of this

01:47:44.880 --> 01:47:47.580
work at the University of Chicago
on goal setting and motivation.

01:47:48.520 --> 01:47:51.182
A couple other things for people
to consider, and by the way, I love

01:47:51.182 --> 01:47:54.089
this space because I'm obsessed with
goals, so I love getting better at

01:47:54.090 --> 01:47:57.959
things, and I'm using all of these
insights in my own life, so it is

01:47:57.960 --> 01:47:59.900
truly a delight to get to share them.

01:47:59.969 --> 01:48:00.740
Okay, sidebar.

01:48:01.420 --> 01:48:03.070
Andrew Huberman: Important
sidebar, I would argue, because

01:48:03.070 --> 01:48:04.270
you live this stuff, right?

01:48:04.640 --> 01:48:06.040
You don't just research it, you live it.

01:48:06.090 --> 01:48:08.650
Maya Shankar: Yeah, it's totally
me-search or whatever they call it.

01:48:09.400 --> 01:48:11.910
So who sets the goal matters.

01:48:12.380 --> 01:48:18.839
So a lot of us work with coaches,
trainers, mentors, bosses.

01:48:19.210 --> 01:48:19.849
That's great.

01:48:20.220 --> 01:48:24.380
It's really, really helpful for people
in our lives to bring structure to our

01:48:24.380 --> 01:48:26.580
goals, to push us along, to motivate us.

01:48:27.320 --> 01:48:30.710
But when other people are setting
our goals, setting our targets

01:48:30.719 --> 01:48:34.769
for us, it undermines a really
valuable source of motivation,

01:48:34.809 --> 01:48:37.000
which is being in the driver's seat.

01:48:37.400 --> 01:48:40.820
We love steering our lives.

01:48:40.860 --> 01:48:42.410
We love feeling agency.

01:48:42.410 --> 01:48:45.780
We love recruiting our own agency
when it comes to achieving our goals.

01:48:47.820 --> 01:48:50.260
We talked about how people
will go to irrational lengths

01:48:50.260 --> 01:48:52.530
to avoid feeling uncertainty.

01:48:52.530 --> 01:48:55.950
People will also go to irrational
lengths to preserve their agency

01:48:55.950 --> 01:48:57.420
and control over a situation.

01:48:57.420 --> 01:49:00.070
So there's some really interesting
research that's come out just in the

01:49:00.070 --> 01:49:06.429
last few years showing that humans
prefer to use their judgment over

01:49:06.429 --> 01:49:10.829
an algorithm that they know performs
better than their judgment, but did

01:49:10.830 --> 01:49:14.630
not involve them, and they're much
more satisfied with the outcomes when

01:49:14.940 --> 01:49:16.200
it's them that's in the driver's seat.

01:49:16.200 --> 01:49:21.809
And so what this means, I think, in
everyday context, is not to do away with,

01:49:21.809 --> 01:49:23.180
like, trainers and coaches and whatnot.

01:49:23.200 --> 01:49:25.330
Every trainer and coach who's
listening, don't hate me.

01:49:25.360 --> 01:49:25.630
Okay?

01:49:25.630 --> 01:49:27.040
You're sticking around.

01:49:27.460 --> 01:49:32.130
But what they can do is they can
build something of a choice set

01:49:32.760 --> 01:49:34.929
into your day to day programming.

01:49:35.310 --> 01:49:38.950
So let's say that at work you have a
certain skill that you're trying to build.

01:49:39.150 --> 01:49:40.800
Ask for a set of options to choose from.

01:49:41.080 --> 01:49:44.010
Own the targets more, you will
see a boost in motivation.

01:49:44.429 --> 01:49:45.749
Let's say you're working
out with a trainer.

01:49:45.770 --> 01:49:49.260
They're like, it's leg day, okay, I'm
going to own some of my targets, right?

01:49:49.260 --> 01:49:50.416
Are we going to go
heavy hard on deadlifts?

01:49:50.416 --> 01:49:52.090
Are we going to go hard on squats?

01:49:52.090 --> 01:49:52.725
Whatever it is.

01:49:52.980 --> 01:49:58.169
And so build some agency into the
experience, because nothing supplants that

01:49:58.170 --> 01:50:03.740
kind of intrinsic drive and the feeling
that you own the success or the failure.

01:50:05.180 --> 01:50:08.200
Again, I think to your earlier point, what
we're really trying to do with some of

01:50:08.200 --> 01:50:14.050
these behavioral insights is capitalize
on our natural state as humans, right?

01:50:14.150 --> 01:50:15.170
Like, what drives us.

01:50:15.170 --> 01:50:17.460
And it turns out we really
love being in control.

01:50:17.620 --> 01:50:22.619
Well, why don't we monopolize on that
when it comes to our goal pursuit, right?

01:50:22.619 --> 01:50:25.590
So we're trying to figure out those
areas of psychology that we can leverage.

01:50:25.970 --> 01:50:26.720
Andrew Huberman: That's fantastic.

01:50:26.810 --> 01:50:29.240
The word agency is so key here, I think.

01:50:29.240 --> 01:50:33.140
And it explains that earlier
result, the shock experiment.

01:50:33.490 --> 01:50:39.400
People having agency over their
response to 100% of the time, at

01:50:39.400 --> 01:50:42.460
least it's giving them some sense
of control and mitigating it.

01:50:42.750 --> 01:50:48.370
Whereas when it's random, 50/50,
rather, when it's random, 50% of the

01:50:48.370 --> 01:50:54.989
trials, then even though the outcome
is better on the whole, it's perceived

01:50:55.009 --> 01:50:56.880
somehow as a reduction in agency.

01:50:57.750 --> 01:50:59.449
There's something
fundamental there, for sure.

01:50:59.609 --> 01:51:04.260
When I started my laboratory and there
was an additional pressure to publish

01:51:04.260 --> 01:51:10.080
papers, this was before getting tenure,
I used to ask students in postdocs when

01:51:10.380 --> 01:51:14.669
the paper would be ready, and then finally
I stopped asking and just said, why

01:51:14.670 --> 01:51:17.420
don't you tell me when the deadline is?

01:51:18.059 --> 01:51:20.469
And not a single one failed.

01:51:20.470 --> 01:51:24.570
Or rather, I should put it in the positive
light every single time they succeeded

01:51:24.570 --> 01:51:29.210
in beating their estimate because
they were in control of that endpoint.

01:51:29.210 --> 01:51:29.590
Maya Shankar: Love that.

01:51:29.610 --> 01:51:36.070
Andrew Huberman: So it was at times
challenging for me, but they set a date.

01:51:36.150 --> 01:51:40.020
And also, by the way, if they need
to extend that date outward, we did.

01:51:40.030 --> 01:51:40.980
That was their choice.

01:51:41.310 --> 01:51:42.400
They said they needed more time.

01:51:42.840 --> 01:51:46.090
The rule in science that I think applies
a lot of places is, I always like the

01:51:46.090 --> 01:51:48.690
phrase as fast as I carefully can.

01:51:48.750 --> 01:51:49.919
Because you don't want to rush.

01:51:50.009 --> 01:51:50.759
Maya Shankar: Absolutely.

01:51:51.719 --> 01:51:53.439
Andrew Huberman: But that sense
of agency, I like to think,

01:51:53.469 --> 01:51:56.950
translated to more joy for them.

01:51:57.040 --> 01:52:00.070
Certainly there was a lot of
productivity from them, and

01:52:00.580 --> 01:52:01.910
they might be listening to this.

01:52:01.910 --> 01:52:04.570
And so they can put in the comments
whether or not I'm telling the truth here.

01:52:05.650 --> 01:52:05.780
Maya Shankar: [LAUGHS]

01:52:05.800 --> 01:52:07.100
Andrew Huberman: Most of
them are professors now.

01:52:07.380 --> 01:52:08.610
Maya Shankar: Well, that
probably means they succeeded.

01:52:09.009 --> 01:52:09.779
Andrew Huberman: They
definitely succeeded.

01:52:09.849 --> 01:52:11.739
The question is whether or not
I had anything to do with it.

01:52:11.750 --> 01:52:14.540
My advisors always said the best
thing you could do is support

01:52:14.540 --> 01:52:16.790
your students in postdocs and
then just get out of their way.

01:52:17.210 --> 01:52:19.940
Because the really good
ones, you can't control them.

01:52:20.260 --> 01:52:22.879
You're just trying to not
screw things up for them.

01:52:23.139 --> 01:52:23.889
Maya Shankar: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:52:23.889 --> 01:52:25.189
There's a lot of intrinsic
motivation there.

01:52:25.950 --> 01:52:30.710
Andrew Huberman: I'm curious about
the difference between lone pursuits

01:52:30.730 --> 01:52:34.660
and group pursuits, because I know you
understand a lot about groups, and I

01:52:34.660 --> 01:52:38.259
want to make sure that we talk about
groupthink, although that has such a

01:52:38.280 --> 01:52:44.970
negative connotation, but the way that
we tend to kind of revert to the mean

01:52:45.009 --> 01:52:49.759
when it comes to our thinking and our
opinions and certainly our explanations

01:52:49.770 --> 01:52:53.220
of who's right and who's wrong when we
are in a collection of like-minded people.

01:52:53.650 --> 01:52:56.990
This could also be phrased
as, what are the dangers of

01:52:57.000 --> 01:52:58.229
being among like-minded people?

01:52:59.910 --> 01:53:01.790
And then we'll relate
that back to motivation.

01:53:01.790 --> 01:53:04.859
But what are the dangers of
being among like-minded people?

01:53:05.150 --> 01:53:10.330
Maya Shankar: Yeah, well, in the
context of goals and motivation, it

01:53:10.330 --> 01:53:13.740
can be very, very helpful to be in
the context of like-minded people.

01:53:13.740 --> 01:53:18.290
And the reason for that is we often
don't see failure up close when it comes

01:53:18.290 --> 01:53:21.259
to people pursuing their goals, but if
we are in the presence of people whose

01:53:21.259 --> 01:53:25.070
values we share, who have a similar
commitment to doing something, and we

01:53:25.080 --> 01:53:29.070
see up close that they sometimes have
those days where they fail, or we have

01:53:29.070 --> 01:53:31.960
the vulnerability to show when we've
failed, that can actually increase

01:53:31.960 --> 01:53:35.220
our resolve that the goals that we're
trying to achieve are actually possible.

01:53:36.020 --> 01:53:39.800
I think the danger of being in the
like-minded spaces is around how it

01:53:39.800 --> 01:53:44.410
limits your frame of mind, s o when it
comes to the ideas that you have, when

01:53:44.410 --> 01:53:48.950
it comes to the convictions you have
around your points of view, it can be very

01:53:48.950 --> 01:53:53.919
dangerous to only be in the echo chamber
again, because I want to give people

01:53:53.920 --> 01:53:57.370
strategies to challenge their way of
thinking without them having to socialize.

01:53:57.380 --> 01:53:57.689
For all the introverts out there,
I have a lot of compassion.

01:53:57.689 --> 01:54:00.479
I have introverted tendencies.

01:54:00.500 --> 01:54:01.080
So I get it.

01:54:01.760 --> 01:54:06.070
One helpful thought experiment you can use
when you feel like maybe you're spending a

01:54:06.070 --> 01:54:09.999
little bit too much time around people who
are just reinforcing whatever viewpoints

01:54:10.030 --> 01:54:16.690
you have, is to ask how your belief system
and your ideas and your opinions of things

01:54:16.930 --> 01:54:22.089
might have been different had you been
born during a different time period and in

01:54:22.090 --> 01:54:26.660
a different family or cultural landscape,
and what happens when it comes to our

01:54:26.660 --> 01:54:30.819
viewpoints is that they become so tethered
to our identities that we feel like if

01:54:30.830 --> 01:54:35.469
we were to jettison a certain belief or
value, we would be jettisoning ourselves,

01:54:35.840 --> 01:54:37.510
and that feels way too threatening.

01:54:37.520 --> 01:54:39.900
It's way too destabilizing
to engage in that.

01:54:40.360 --> 01:54:42.940
But the minute you imagine what it
would have been like to have been

01:54:42.940 --> 01:54:46.099
born in a different family, with a
different religious belief system, with

01:54:46.099 --> 01:54:50.470
a different value system, all of a sudden
you transport your same self, right?

01:54:50.470 --> 01:54:56.010
I'm still Maya, into this new environment,
and you start to see how non-precious

01:54:56.320 --> 01:54:57.630
some of your beliefs are, right?

01:54:57.630 --> 01:55:01.050
Maybe they don't have the sacred
quality that you thought that they did.

01:55:01.310 --> 01:55:05.400
And so you might be more open to changing
your mind, more open and receptive to

01:55:05.400 --> 01:55:09.269
challenging your own points of view if
you engage in that thought experiment.

01:55:09.830 --> 01:55:14.420
Andrew Huberman: I recall you discussing
a description of people watching a

01:55:14.420 --> 01:55:19.210
game of sport that involved bad calls.

01:55:19.320 --> 01:55:19.580
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

01:55:19.730 --> 01:55:20.820
Controversial referee calls.

01:55:20.820 --> 01:55:22.030
Andrew Huberman:
Controversial referee calls.

01:55:22.650 --> 01:55:22.820
Yeah.

01:55:22.820 --> 01:55:25.120
If you could share with us a little bit
about that result, because I find it

01:55:25.120 --> 01:55:30.549
really interesting, especially the part
where the experimenters can swap the

01:55:30.549 --> 01:55:35.549
identities of the teams in theory, and
then, well, basically what people come

01:55:35.550 --> 01:55:40.870
to realize is that our perception of
the outside world is strongly informed

01:55:41.240 --> 01:55:45.409
by the group that we see ourselves
in, and often to our own detriment.

01:55:46.180 --> 01:55:46.600
Maya Shankar: Absolutely.

01:55:46.600 --> 01:55:46.940
Yeah.

01:55:46.959 --> 01:55:48.210
So this is a study from the 1950s.

01:55:48.210 --> 01:55:52.200
And to your point, we tend to
think, okay, we're human beings.

01:55:52.200 --> 01:55:56.180
We're really enlightened, we're
making decisions, and we're engaging

01:55:56.230 --> 01:56:00.130
in judgments of things based
on data and evidence and facts.

01:56:01.130 --> 01:56:03.240
And surely my visual
system wouldn't lie to me.

01:56:03.250 --> 01:56:07.150
So whatever I perceive is going to be
true and a veridical representation

01:56:07.150 --> 01:56:09.590
of the world and, like, not true.

01:56:09.680 --> 01:56:14.610
Okay, a lot of our beliefs,
and these are strong beliefs.

01:56:14.610 --> 01:56:17.480
I mean, again, they're what we
believe to be fact about the world

01:56:17.650 --> 01:56:19.429
is informed by our group membership.

01:56:19.440 --> 01:56:25.749
So in this study, loyal fans of
two opposing football teams watched

01:56:25.850 --> 01:56:30.080
these controversial plays, so,
where the referee made a call, and

01:56:30.080 --> 01:56:32.860
they weren't quite certain if it
was like, in or out, let's say.

01:56:33.240 --> 01:56:38.900
And depending on your loyalty to the
team, to whatever sports team, whichever

01:56:38.900 --> 01:56:42.830
side you were on, you were much more
likely to favor calls that were made

01:56:42.830 --> 01:56:46.400
on your teams in your team's favor.

01:56:46.890 --> 01:56:51.559
And when you ask people coming out of a
study like this, it's not like, yes, I

01:56:51.559 --> 01:56:56.730
knew I was biased, like, I knew that I was
basing my judgment of these referee calls

01:56:56.730 --> 01:57:01.799
based on my affiliation and my love of
Team X or Team Y, you wouldn't think that.

01:57:01.799 --> 01:57:04.410
You'd think you were an arbiter
of truth in this situation.

01:57:04.410 --> 01:57:06.500
You're just recalling what
your visual system saw.

01:57:06.900 --> 01:57:12.720
And I think that shows how powerful
these social forces are, how powerful our

01:57:12.720 --> 01:57:16.750
group affiliations are, because it can
truly change the way that you see stuff.

01:57:17.879 --> 01:57:20.600
Of course, it can then transform
the way that you think about stuff.

01:57:21.400 --> 01:57:25.809
And so that, to me, is a powerful reminder
that when we are in disagreement with

01:57:25.810 --> 01:57:30.620
someone else and we just try to bombard
them with facts, I mean, you're a

01:57:30.620 --> 01:57:34.250
scientist, so if you're hearing someone
say something and you're like, oh,

01:57:34.809 --> 01:57:36.329
that's not accurate, that's not true.

01:57:36.810 --> 01:57:40.639
Your instinct probably says, but have
you heard about the 2017 study, the peer

01:57:40.640 --> 01:57:42.080
reviewed journal article from PubMed?

01:57:44.050 --> 01:57:47.600
But when you recognize that, actually
a large part of our belief system

01:57:48.610 --> 01:57:53.249
emerges from the groups that we
identify with, I think there's an

01:57:53.309 --> 01:57:54.620
inspiring lesson that comes from this.

01:57:54.690 --> 01:57:58.440
So we shouldn't be too disheartened by
the fact that this is true, but it helps

01:57:58.440 --> 01:58:02.230
round out our understanding of why it is
that people believe the things they do.

01:58:02.639 --> 01:58:06.360
And as a result, we have more
resources at hand to try to understand

01:58:06.370 --> 01:58:07.590
how we can change their minds.

01:58:08.210 --> 01:58:12.900
So, one of the guys that I interviewed
on my podcast, his name is Daryl Davis.

01:58:13.000 --> 01:58:18.330
He's a black jazz musician, and he was
confronted by a member of the Ku Klux Klan

01:58:18.380 --> 01:58:22.830
at one of his performances, and it led,
talk about a slight change of plans , I

01:58:22.830 --> 01:58:27.580
mean, he just went on a totally different
life path and ended up convincing dozens

01:58:27.580 --> 01:58:31.170
of people to leave white supremacy
groups, including the Ku Klux Klan.

01:58:31.710 --> 01:58:38.170
And when it comes to Daryl and his
approach, well, one, he recruited people's

01:58:38.170 --> 01:58:41.930
agencies, so he never implied to them,
oh, I'm trying to change your mind.

01:58:44.090 --> 01:58:45.889
He always says, like, I
didn't convince them, Maya.

01:58:46.080 --> 01:58:48.169
They convinced themselves
to change their mind.

01:58:48.170 --> 01:58:49.370
So he recruited their agency.

01:58:50.510 --> 01:58:55.769
But he also tried his absolute
hardest to not question their

01:58:55.770 --> 01:58:57.410
fundamental and underlying humanity.

01:58:57.420 --> 01:59:01.019
So he tried to understand, why
are you part of this group,

01:59:01.450 --> 01:59:03.659
this vile vitriolic group?

01:59:04.059 --> 01:59:06.960
And some people would share, well, you
know, it's a family tradition thing.

01:59:07.050 --> 01:59:08.320
My father was in the klan.

01:59:08.330 --> 01:59:09.770
My grandfather's in the klan.

01:59:09.980 --> 01:59:13.589
Look, none of this excuses
being in a hate group, okay?

01:59:13.870 --> 01:59:17.520
But at least it gave Daryl an
understanding of some of the factors

01:59:17.520 --> 01:59:20.489
that were pushing them towards
the group so that he might offer

01:59:20.490 --> 01:59:23.950
that sense of community, that
sense of belonging somewhere else,

01:59:24.250 --> 01:59:25.620
maybe outside of a hate group.

01:59:26.240 --> 01:59:30.210
But if he thought that he was actually
just fighting over facts, over whether

01:59:30.490 --> 01:59:35.019
African Americans should be treated equal
to everyone else, then he would have

01:59:35.020 --> 01:59:39.790
lost that argument, because he wasn't
even fighting with the right currency.

01:59:39.799 --> 01:59:40.710
What was relevant?

01:59:40.719 --> 01:59:46.600
It was the first episode of A Slight
Change of Plans we ever released

01:59:46.600 --> 01:59:48.150
and continues to be my favorite.

01:59:48.150 --> 01:59:53.100
Because what was so thrilling about
this interview is that the strategies

01:59:53.100 --> 01:59:55.749
Daryl used to convince people to
change their minds again of these

01:59:55.799 --> 02:00:00.410
deeply entrenched, horrific views were
totally corroborated by the science

02:00:00.410 --> 02:00:01.720
of how we change people's minds.

02:00:01.760 --> 02:00:05.759
So he used a lot of really effective
strategies, just intuitively, like,

02:00:05.760 --> 02:00:09.129
he's just a mastermind behavioral
scientist just by virtue of who he is.

02:00:10.139 --> 02:00:14.440
But he showed genuine curiosity for
why it is they believed what they

02:00:14.440 --> 02:00:16.259
did, which is, again, extremely hard.

02:00:16.259 --> 02:00:19.540
And I would not have had the equanimity
to show genuine curiosity for why

02:00:19.540 --> 02:00:20.790
someone is in the Ku Klux Klan.

02:00:21.040 --> 02:00:22.550
But he showed that curiosity.

02:00:22.810 --> 02:00:26.560
He increased his question
to statement ratio.

02:00:26.560 --> 02:00:30.870
So it's really important to ask
people a lot of questions, and then

02:00:30.870 --> 02:00:35.809
he would ask people a really important
question, which is, well, what,

02:00:35.809 --> 02:00:37.290
in theory, could change your mind?

02:00:37.490 --> 02:00:40.050
Like, what evidence would I have
to give you in order to change

02:00:40.050 --> 02:00:41.500
your mind about X, Y, or Z?

02:00:42.250 --> 02:00:47.820
And the reason that I love asking that
question is that it presupposes that

02:00:47.820 --> 02:00:51.099
someone ought to be willing to change
their mind in the face of new information.

02:00:51.420 --> 02:00:53.730
So this harkens back to the conversation
we were having earlier about the

02:00:53.730 --> 02:00:55.820
importance of having a malleable
state of mind and being willing

02:00:55.820 --> 02:00:57.179
to update in the face of new info.

02:00:57.800 --> 02:01:01.809
Now, if the person in response
says, literally nothing will change

02:01:01.810 --> 02:01:03.929
my mind, okay, well, then, you
know it's not worth your time to

02:01:03.940 --> 02:01:05.330
have the disagreement with them.

02:01:05.570 --> 02:01:08.270
But if they give you a little bit
and say, well, maybe I would change

02:01:08.270 --> 02:01:12.054
my mind on vaccines if you were to
tell me X, Y, or Z, maybe I would

02:01:12.100 --> 02:01:17.099
change my mind on immigration reform
if you were to tell me this or that.

02:01:17.340 --> 02:01:19.219
Now you have an in, right?

02:01:19.219 --> 02:01:22.190
But you do need to get them into
the state of mind where they think,

02:01:22.480 --> 02:01:25.450
yeah, I guess in theory, I could
change my mind about this thing that

02:01:25.450 --> 02:01:26.930
I feel absolutely resolute about.

02:01:27.980 --> 02:01:33.720
Andrew Huberman: I've never worked in
public policy, but I feel very strongly

02:01:33.740 --> 02:01:43.030
that where I see failures en masse of
public health policy or educational

02:01:43.030 --> 02:01:49.330
policy, almost always there seems to be a
failure of even interest in understanding

02:01:49.330 --> 02:01:51.169
what motivates the other side's position.

02:01:51.470 --> 02:01:55.883
And this actually gets me frustrated
to the point of being motivated,

02:01:56.110 --> 02:02:00.569
where it's like people are saying,
you're wrong, you're wrong.

02:02:00.840 --> 02:02:04.320
Know this, know that to the
point of it's almost maddening.

02:02:04.410 --> 02:02:12.850
And far more seldom do we see people
saying, okay, I'm in a third person

02:02:12.850 --> 02:02:15.089
myself, or I'm going to put myself
in the other person's shoes and

02:02:15.090 --> 02:02:17.390
say, why might they feel that way?

02:02:17.400 --> 02:02:21.030
Why would this person be listening
to this individual as opposed to

02:02:21.030 --> 02:02:22.940
this public health individual?

02:02:23.359 --> 02:02:28.100
And look, without taking any stance
on this, because it's a much bigger

02:02:28.100 --> 02:02:32.639
conversation than we want to have
right now, I could look at public

02:02:32.639 --> 02:02:38.270
health officials that just completely
failed to understand the other

02:02:38.270 --> 02:02:40.479
side's position and vice versa.

02:02:40.840 --> 02:02:43.440
And that to me just says
it's a communication failure.

02:02:44.179 --> 02:02:49.430
And I'll take this out of the COVID
pandemic discussion as it's normally

02:02:49.430 --> 02:02:55.269
had and say that one thing that we know
for sure is that in the 2020 to really

02:02:55.270 --> 02:03:01.570
2022, but still 2023 landscape, there
were so many mental health concerns.

02:03:01.880 --> 02:03:04.610
Everybody, regardless of where
people were on the vaccine

02:03:04.610 --> 02:03:07.710
debate, mass debate, lockdown
debate, regardless of any of that,

02:03:08.680 --> 02:03:10.160
everyone's stress level was elevated.

02:03:10.190 --> 02:03:10.860
Maya Shankar: Absolutely.

02:03:10.870 --> 02:03:16.559
Andrew Huberman: And there were very,
very few top down from, at the level

02:03:16.559 --> 02:03:19.899
of government, discussions about
how to maintain circadian rhythm

02:03:20.040 --> 02:03:23.690
and sleep health, how to maintain
health in general in that landscape.

02:03:24.360 --> 02:03:27.050
And that, for me, was
just really shocking.

02:03:27.070 --> 02:03:30.039
It was also one of the reasons why
we launched the podcast, frankly,

02:03:30.040 --> 02:03:33.280
is that I really feel that the
tools were needed by everybody and

02:03:33.280 --> 02:03:35.089
should be zero cost to everybody.

02:03:35.629 --> 02:03:41.670
But what was clear is there was so much
pointing of fingers and name calling and

02:03:41.720 --> 02:03:46.360
violence even, that no one was saying,
like, why would people feel this way?

02:03:46.360 --> 02:03:48.910
Why would people trust these
sources as opposed to these sources?

02:03:48.910 --> 02:03:53.290
And we can only conclude if
we're good scientists that the

02:03:54.580 --> 02:03:57.130
landscape was ineffective, right?

02:03:57.130 --> 02:03:57.950
It was just ineffective.

02:03:57.970 --> 02:03:58.820
And it continues.

02:03:58.820 --> 02:04:03.739
I mean, if you have the desire to take
a reduction in dopamine by going on

02:04:03.750 --> 02:04:08.490
Twitter and following this back and forth
that continues today, it's pretty ugly.

02:04:08.500 --> 02:04:11.410
Still, none of it seems
really solution oriented.

02:04:11.410 --> 02:04:12.940
There are a few people out
there who are trying to make it

02:04:12.950 --> 02:04:16.120
solution oriented, but not really.

02:04:16.340 --> 02:04:20.960
And so I don't want to go
into the dark aspects here.

02:04:20.960 --> 02:04:25.349
But it does seem like this
willingness to take a look at why

02:04:25.380 --> 02:04:31.230
others might feel the opposite of
how we feel is a very rare quality.

02:04:31.460 --> 02:04:33.139
And this gentleman, Daryl,
what was his last name?

02:04:33.140 --> 02:04:33.590
Maya Shankar: Daryl Davis.

02:04:33.850 --> 02:04:35.540
Andrew Huberman: I think I've
seen a number of things with him.

02:04:36.220 --> 02:04:40.810
He's obviously extraordinary, but
we call him that because people

02:04:40.810 --> 02:04:42.369
like him are exceedingly rare.

02:04:42.889 --> 02:04:45.760
So what can we do to cultivate
that kind of mindset?

02:04:46.320 --> 02:04:49.059
Because I'm not pointing fingers
here, I mean, I think we all have this

02:04:49.060 --> 02:04:54.160
default tendency to gather evidence
the way that we gather evidence, draw

02:04:54.160 --> 02:04:56.530
conclusions, and then stand our ground.

02:04:56.920 --> 02:04:59.250
And I think it's detrimental to everyone.

02:04:59.470 --> 02:05:02.360
Maya Shankar: So you're making me
reflect on probably the greatest

02:05:02.370 --> 02:05:06.069
gift that being a cognitive
scientist has given me in my life.

02:05:06.820 --> 02:05:09.620
Obviously, it's fed my curiosity, it's
been a delight to study things and

02:05:09.620 --> 02:05:14.800
learn things, but the greatest gift it
has given me is empathy towards people.

02:05:14.809 --> 02:05:20.110
It is the greatest driver of human
empathy to learn how our minds work.

02:05:21.099 --> 02:05:23.069
And I don't know if there's
a substitute for that.

02:05:23.420 --> 02:05:27.629
Partly that's why I started A Slight
Change of Plans . We have story episodes

02:05:27.630 --> 02:05:30.820
where you hear from people like Daryl,
but I interview scientists from all over

02:05:30.820 --> 02:05:32.540
the world about their areas of expertise.

02:05:32.820 --> 02:05:36.990
And I genuinely believe that the more
we learn about how the mind works,

02:05:37.009 --> 02:05:40.769
the more we learn from my field of
cognitive science about how we make

02:05:40.770 --> 02:05:44.310
decisions, how we develop our attitudes
and beliefs about the world, how we

02:05:44.320 --> 02:05:49.420
come to be the people that we are, the
more we can bridge these empathy gaps.

02:05:50.080 --> 02:05:51.450
And it's been profound for me.

02:05:51.450 --> 02:05:53.700
I mean, I feel so lucky to
have been steeped in this

02:05:53.700 --> 02:05:55.439
literature for decades now.

02:05:55.670 --> 02:05:58.809
But my hope is to invite people into
the conversation, because the more you

02:05:58.809 --> 02:06:03.470
learn about why people are the way they
are, the more empathy you can extend.

02:06:03.740 --> 02:06:07.700
And the more, I'm not even saying you
need to extend an olive branch, I'm

02:06:07.720 --> 02:06:11.880
not saying that you need to compromise
your own belief system, but at least

02:06:11.880 --> 02:06:15.840
you see that there might be an entry
point, a reason to have a discussion

02:06:15.840 --> 02:06:18.519
with this person who believes things
that are completely different from you.

02:06:18.570 --> 02:06:21.340
And we talked about gratitude a bit.

02:06:21.340 --> 02:06:25.410
In this conversation, I feel immense
gratitude that I have a posture of

02:06:25.410 --> 02:06:31.089
empathy as I move around in this world,
because I have strong beliefs on things.

02:06:31.090 --> 02:06:31.820
I care a lot.

02:06:31.840 --> 02:06:33.860
I care about reducing human suffering.

02:06:33.870 --> 02:06:37.820
And then I meet someone who I think is pro
a policy that promotes human suffering.

02:06:37.830 --> 02:06:41.190
And of course, the visceral human
instinct is like, to hell with

02:06:41.190 --> 02:06:43.860
you and your viewpoint, this is
horrible, this is intolerable.

02:06:44.280 --> 02:06:46.939
But because I have this
cognitive science hat on.

02:06:47.570 --> 02:06:50.480
It allows me to walk around with
a slightly different viewpoint,

02:06:50.780 --> 02:06:54.029
and I really feel that I'm a
better person as a result of that.

02:06:54.209 --> 02:06:57.990
And I've heard from listeners of A Slight
Change of Plans when they listen to these

02:06:57.990 --> 02:07:01.330
science episodes, whether it's the science
of loneliness, the science of empathy,

02:07:01.440 --> 02:07:06.580
the science of meditation, I try to bring
this empathetic spin to understanding,

02:07:06.590 --> 02:07:13.269
again, neuroscience and psychology, they
have found that they are kinder to others.

02:07:13.630 --> 02:07:16.410
And so that's probably the best
feedback that I've ever received on

02:07:16.410 --> 02:07:19.720
the show, is like, people are like,
I'm a nicer person to other people now,

02:07:19.800 --> 02:07:21.030
especially the ones I don't agree with.

02:07:21.230 --> 02:07:23.200
Andrew Huberman: And presumably
to themselves as well.

02:07:23.250 --> 02:07:27.269
I know you've brought up the
topic of empathy as a way

02:07:27.730 --> 02:07:30.080
to prevent burnout, right?

02:07:30.900 --> 02:07:32.630
And here we're not just
talking about job burnout.

02:07:32.640 --> 02:07:37.509
We're talking about the burnout
that is inherent to any long

02:07:37.550 --> 02:07:41.169
term pursuit that's challenging,
raising kids, being in a family.

02:07:41.179 --> 02:07:43.120
What is the great Ram Das quote?

02:07:43.360 --> 02:07:44.200
Think you're enlightened?

02:07:44.219 --> 02:07:49.380
Go spend a week with your parents,
no matter how enlightened you are.

02:07:51.150 --> 02:07:53.360
I remind myself that I love my parents.

02:07:53.360 --> 02:07:58.030
I love my parents, but it's just a
completely different frame shift.

02:07:58.030 --> 02:07:59.790
But also kind to oneself.

02:07:59.790 --> 02:08:04.010
I mean, I think there's starting
to be some good neuroscience at

02:08:04.010 --> 02:08:05.929
the mechanistic level of empathy.

02:08:06.770 --> 02:08:11.629
Clearly, empathy is not the
default state for most people.

02:08:11.690 --> 02:08:15.280
It's something that we need to cultivate
as a practice and that we can cultivate

02:08:15.309 --> 02:08:17.849
as a practice along the lines of empathy.

02:08:17.849 --> 02:08:21.539
But also returning to a topic that
we opened today's discussion with.

02:08:23.260 --> 02:08:27.299
We build these narratives about ourselves
starting in adolescence, maybe even

02:08:27.300 --> 02:08:30.850
earlier and through our teen years,
and we have various experiences.

02:08:31.190 --> 02:08:36.429
But I'm curious how we can continue to
build narratives about ourselves and the

02:08:36.429 --> 02:08:44.430
role of narrative, the I statements, the I
am statements, and whether or not you and

02:08:44.590 --> 02:08:47.800
we should all spend some time doing this.

02:08:47.800 --> 02:08:51.130
I mean, these days people exercise
because we know it's good for us.

02:08:51.210 --> 02:08:55.760
I hope people get sunlight because they
know it's great for them that people

02:08:55.799 --> 02:08:59.520
perhaps have a meditation practice or a
therapy practice or a journaling practice.

02:08:59.520 --> 02:09:03.980
But how is it that we can continue to
evolve our narratives about self in a way

02:09:03.980 --> 02:09:07.750
that promotes some or all of the things
that we've been talking about today?

02:09:08.580 --> 02:09:08.860
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

02:09:08.860 --> 02:09:13.389
So empathy is really interesting because
I think we have a lot of misconceptions

02:09:13.389 --> 02:09:16.469
about it, and we have misconceptions
about how empathetic we actually are.

02:09:16.799 --> 02:09:19.820
I would argue people are more empathetic
than they think, and let me tell you why.

02:09:19.820 --> 02:09:23.519
So, this comes from research by
my friend Jamil Zaki at Stanford.

02:09:25.019 --> 02:09:28.250
There're three distinct types of empathy
a lot of people don't know about.

02:09:28.250 --> 02:09:32.719
So the first kind is emotional
empathy, and this is the one that

02:09:32.719 --> 02:09:34.270
feels very intuitive to most of us.

02:09:34.289 --> 02:09:36.260
So it's this visceral reaction I have.

02:09:36.309 --> 02:09:38.610
You tell me that you've
had a really hard time.

02:09:38.870 --> 02:09:40.280
My eyes start to well up.

02:09:40.290 --> 02:09:45.510
I can truly feel your pain, and
I just feel what you feel, okay?

02:09:45.820 --> 02:09:49.519
And that typically, is what people think
of when they think of empathy, period.

02:09:50.080 --> 02:09:52.230
They overlook two other types of empathy.

02:09:53.199 --> 02:09:55.560
The second type is
called cognitive empathy.

02:09:56.090 --> 02:10:01.610
This is the ability to accurately
diagnose what it is that's causing you

02:10:01.650 --> 02:10:05.539
distress in this moment, and what it is
that I could offer up to you to try to

02:10:05.550 --> 02:10:07.700
help ameliorate some of your suffering.

02:10:08.490 --> 02:10:13.350
The third kind is called empathic
concern, or it's known as compassion

02:10:13.350 --> 02:10:18.420
as well, which is the actual desire to
help, you desire to help another person.

02:10:19.040 --> 02:10:21.939
And what's so interesting about
these three types of empathy is that

02:10:22.040 --> 02:10:23.440
they don't correlate within people.

02:10:24.009 --> 02:10:26.870
You can be really high on the
emotional empathy scale, right?

02:10:26.870 --> 02:10:29.630
You can have tears streaming down
your face as you hear about your

02:10:29.630 --> 02:10:33.759
friend's divorce, but you might be
really bad at diagnosing what it

02:10:33.760 --> 02:10:35.110
is that's causing them distress.

02:10:35.150 --> 02:10:40.000
You might be really bad at actually
offering up a solution to their problem.

02:10:40.530 --> 02:10:41.700
Or you might lack the will.

02:10:42.320 --> 02:10:45.099
Like, if you're sociopathic, you might
just not have the will to help someone.

02:10:46.009 --> 02:10:51.280
And what's so interesting is that, I
think in our society, this relates back to

02:10:51.310 --> 02:10:53.690
identity and the labels we give ourselves.

02:10:54.180 --> 02:11:00.449
I think our society puts a huge premium
on emotional empathy, and we discount

02:11:00.460 --> 02:11:03.160
people who don't have that visceral
response, and we just immediately

02:11:03.160 --> 02:11:04.350
say, oh, they're not empathetic.

02:11:04.350 --> 02:11:06.550
And this happens from the time that
we're really little, by the way, like

02:11:06.550 --> 02:11:09.740
the kid who's crying on the playground,
comforting their friend, right?

02:11:09.740 --> 02:11:11.610
They're like, wow, that
kid's got a ton of empathy.

02:11:11.750 --> 02:11:15.099
My older kid doesn't seem to
really care about people, but they

02:11:15.099 --> 02:11:17.019
might excel in cognitive empathy.

02:11:17.139 --> 02:11:20.079
They might excel when it
comes to empathic concern.

02:11:20.490 --> 02:11:23.360
So one of the things I was talking
about with Jamil on A Slight Change

02:11:23.360 --> 02:11:28.290
of Plans , you know, maybe we ought to
think about empathy languages in the

02:11:28.290 --> 02:11:29.769
same way we think about love languages.

02:11:30.290 --> 02:11:32.849
People have different ways of
expressing their empathy, and

02:11:32.849 --> 02:11:34.299
we ought to value them equally.

02:11:34.980 --> 02:11:38.900
And that's been wonderful, because I
think even in the past, I would have

02:11:38.900 --> 02:11:43.019
had a really hard situation, and I go
to one of my friends, and they just

02:11:43.020 --> 02:11:44.790
seem like, a little bit more stoic.

02:11:44.880 --> 02:11:46.240
And I'm like, do you even give a shit?

02:11:46.810 --> 02:11:48.920
Why do you not care as
much as I want you to care?

02:11:49.230 --> 02:11:52.450
It turns out they're fantastic
at wanting to help me and

02:11:52.550 --> 02:11:53.760
understanding what's wrong with me.

02:11:54.190 --> 02:11:59.689
And I love the idea of giving a little
more love to those second two buckets,

02:11:59.690 --> 02:12:03.340
because I think it'll allow us to better
recruit more empathy from others and

02:12:03.350 --> 02:12:06.389
also to see ourselves differently,
maybe for those people out there who are

02:12:06.390 --> 02:12:07.840
like, I'm not a very empathetic person.

02:12:07.850 --> 02:12:09.830
You might actually be more
empathetic than you think.

02:12:10.520 --> 02:12:12.820
The second thing I wanted
to share is about burnout.

02:12:12.820 --> 02:12:14.620
So you talked a little bit about burnout.

02:12:15.389 --> 02:12:19.660
People who rate really high on the
emotional empathy scale tend to

02:12:19.660 --> 02:12:21.190
experience burnout at higher rates.

02:12:21.200 --> 02:12:25.450
So you can imagine healthcare workers,
first responders, essentially what

02:12:25.450 --> 02:12:28.439
you're doing when you feel emotional
empathy is you're carrying the

02:12:28.440 --> 02:12:29.840
burden of the other person's pain.

02:12:29.840 --> 02:12:32.070
So you can easily imagine
how that can deplete you.

02:12:32.500 --> 02:12:36.720
And I think the instinct that we have when
we're empathetic is to say, you know what?

02:12:37.050 --> 02:12:38.460
I'm just going to shut myself off.

02:12:38.510 --> 02:12:39.960
I had that experience in 2020.

02:12:39.960 --> 02:12:42.540
I was like, there's too much
bad stuff happening around me.

02:12:42.540 --> 02:12:44.220
Like, I prefer to just not feel things.

02:12:44.230 --> 02:12:45.070
Thank you very much.

02:12:45.320 --> 02:12:48.629
And so I tried to close myself
off from natural emotional

02:12:48.630 --> 02:12:49.870
reactions I would have to things.

02:12:50.460 --> 02:12:54.259
But what Jamil's research shows is
that you don't actually have to.

02:12:55.410 --> 02:13:00.540
If you cultivate cognitive empathy
and empathic concern, those can

02:13:00.540 --> 02:13:02.330
actually be protective against burnout.

02:13:02.800 --> 02:13:05.229
So you don't have to do away
with empathy altogether.

02:13:05.230 --> 02:13:08.580
You just have to shift gears and
be more selective about the kind of

02:13:08.580 --> 02:13:09.899
empathy that you're investing in.

02:13:10.099 --> 02:13:13.139
So I love this research because, again,
it just opens your mind up to this

02:13:13.400 --> 02:13:15.686
whole world of empathy that you might
have thought of as more as, like,

02:13:15.770 --> 02:13:18.970
the singular concept and allows there
to be a little bit more grace space.

02:13:19.830 --> 02:13:22.450
Andrew Huberman: I love the idea that
there are different categories of empathy.

02:13:22.450 --> 02:13:26.950
It will also arm me with a response,
if ever, hypothetically, someone

02:13:26.950 --> 02:13:29.409
says, I don't feel like you're
really feeling what I'm feeling,

02:13:29.410 --> 02:13:30.350
and therefore you're not empathic.

02:13:31.260 --> 02:13:35.930
To my experience, where I rate
on these scales isn't important.

02:13:36.020 --> 02:13:39.910
But this notion of cognitive empathy, I
think, is really important and probably

02:13:39.910 --> 02:13:41.440
one that most people haven't heard of.

02:13:41.440 --> 02:13:46.120
I certainly haven't heard of it,
but I like to think that it really

02:13:46.120 --> 02:13:47.790
does exist and that it's at least--

02:13:47.790 --> 02:13:49.080
Maya Shankar: --And you
might have it in spades.

02:13:49.630 --> 02:13:50.349
Andrew Huberman: I don't know.

02:13:51.209 --> 02:13:52.760
You'd have to ask the people close to me.

02:13:53.520 --> 02:13:58.429
But that it is at least as important as
the emotional empathy before we conclude

02:13:58.429 --> 02:14:03.299
there is something that I unfortunately
pushed us past too quickly that I want to

02:14:03.309 --> 02:14:08.160
return to because I think it's something
that so many people care about and live

02:14:08.160 --> 02:14:11.600
with each day, which is this issue of
challenges with ongoing motivation.

02:14:11.710 --> 02:14:13.897
And forgive me for doing a
bit of an anachronism here.

02:14:13.940 --> 02:14:18.800
I'm sort of jumping back to this
because I realized that I pulled us

02:14:18.800 --> 02:14:23.349
off to another topic, but you've talked
about the middle problem before and

02:14:23.699 --> 02:14:27.020
it's too important to not return to.

02:14:27.390 --> 02:14:31.740
So tell us about the middle problem and
how we can overcome the middle problem.

02:14:31.750 --> 02:14:34.320
Maya Shankar: And before I do that,
do you mind if I give just a couple

02:14:34.360 --> 02:14:36.339
short strategies around goal setting?

02:14:36.339 --> 02:14:38.349
I just want to make sure
I round out that section.

02:14:38.360 --> 02:14:40.989
Andrew Huberman: Not only would
I not mind, I would be delighted.

02:14:40.989 --> 02:14:43.450
Maya Shankar: I just want to make
sure, again, I share the wisdom that's

02:14:43.450 --> 02:14:44.810
helped me so much in my personal life.

02:14:45.230 --> 02:14:47.579
Okay, I'll try to be fast.

02:14:48.459 --> 02:14:50.679
Andrew Huberman: Please take your time.

02:14:50.929 --> 02:14:54.060
Maya Shankar: But people have these goals
to reach, I got to get them out running.

02:14:55.230 --> 02:14:57.929
So the first is to make sure that you
are, so we've already talked about

02:14:57.950 --> 02:15:00.229
approach versus avoidant goals, right?

02:15:00.229 --> 02:15:04.380
We've talked about how who sets the goal
matters and how if it's you, it's better.

02:15:04.380 --> 02:15:06.690
If you have some ownership
over your targets.

02:15:07.360 --> 02:15:10.850
The third thing is to make sure that
you're setting goals when you're in the

02:15:10.850 --> 02:15:15.530
same psychological and physiological
state as the one you'll be in when

02:15:15.530 --> 02:15:17.139
you're actually pursuing the goal.

02:15:17.750 --> 02:15:21.010
Because we tend to have what is known
as this is, again, fishbox work.

02:15:21.030 --> 02:15:25.220
We tend to have empathy gaps between our
present day selves and our future selves.

02:15:25.550 --> 02:15:30.269
And that empathy gap can lead us to be
very compassionate towards 4:00 p.m.

02:15:30.270 --> 02:15:35.589
on Sunday watching TV Maya, and 6:00 a.m.

02:15:35.600 --> 02:15:40.290
Maya, who I hope is going to be at the
gym, like killing herself with a really

02:15:40.290 --> 02:15:42.309
high intensity interval set or whatnot.

02:15:42.780 --> 02:15:44.879
And so if it is 4:00 p.m.

02:15:45.040 --> 02:15:47.476
on Sunday, probably not the best
time for you to say, I'm going to

02:15:47.509 --> 02:15:49.380
go to the gym every day at 6:00 a.m.

02:15:49.820 --> 02:15:55.309
If you actually are at the gym at 6:00
a.m., and you are feeling viscerally the

02:15:55.310 --> 02:15:59.110
physiological pain, the psychological
pain of having gotten up really

02:15:59.110 --> 02:16:02.840
early to do the workout, then it's
reasonable for you to set that goal.

02:16:03.110 --> 02:16:05.230
But it's kind of the opposite
of, like they say, don't go

02:16:05.230 --> 02:16:06.409
to the supermarket hungry.

02:16:06.940 --> 02:16:10.880
Actually, in this situation, you want
to be in exactly the same physiological

02:16:11.059 --> 02:16:13.240
and psychological state you'll be
in when you're in goal pursuit.

02:16:13.280 --> 02:16:15.500
It'll make it much more likely
that you set reasonable goals

02:16:15.500 --> 02:16:16.510
and you actually reach them.

02:16:17.250 --> 02:16:21.000
The second thing that you might
want to think about is, so I don't

02:16:21.030 --> 02:16:24.519
know about you, Andrew, but I feel
like I'm a goal purist by nature.

02:16:24.559 --> 02:16:30.610
So when I set a goal, the minute I,
like, fall off even slightly, the goal is

02:16:30.610 --> 02:16:34.549
gone for me, and I'm like, I messed up.

02:16:34.980 --> 02:16:35.870
Let's start from the beginning.

02:16:35.870 --> 02:16:36.699
Let's start from scratch.

02:16:36.700 --> 02:16:37.690
I need a new goal.

02:16:37.990 --> 02:16:39.744
I've already messed up,
and it doesn't matter.

02:16:39.744 --> 02:16:43.789
So I feel like unless I achieve
perfection in achieving my goals,

02:16:43.790 --> 02:16:46.220
I get very frustrated and I just
fall off the wagon completely.

02:16:46.740 --> 02:16:50.360
So one thing that researchers have
shown is that it's really helpful to

02:16:50.370 --> 02:16:54.490
build in what's called an emergency
reserve into your goal setting, or

02:16:54.850 --> 02:16:56.389
slack is another way of putting it.

02:16:56.959 --> 02:16:58.000
So let's say I have a goal.

02:16:58.000 --> 02:16:59.879
I want to go to the gym
every single day this month.

02:17:00.910 --> 02:17:03.769
It's really important and helpful
to give yourself, and you're

02:17:03.770 --> 02:17:05.019
not going soft on yourself.

02:17:05.020 --> 02:17:09.160
I promise to give yourself, for
example, three get out of jail

02:17:09.160 --> 02:17:13.840
free cards, three days where, for
whatever reason, it's okay that you

02:17:13.840 --> 02:17:15.470
didn't go to the gym, you got sick.

02:17:15.570 --> 02:17:16.900
Maybe you have kids who got sick.

02:17:17.090 --> 02:17:18.310
You're just not feeling motivated.

02:17:18.310 --> 02:17:19.719
It doesn't really matter
what the reason is.

02:17:20.129 --> 02:17:20.809
You didn't go to the gym.

02:17:20.809 --> 02:17:24.610
But the important thing is that you're
still on track to achieving your goal,

02:17:24.639 --> 02:17:27.530
even if you missed those three days
because you built them into the system.

02:17:27.980 --> 02:17:32.360
The final thing I'll say about
setting the goal is to try

02:17:32.360 --> 02:17:36.249
to capitalize on a phenomenon
known as the fresh start effect.

02:17:36.259 --> 02:17:38.020
So this is work by my friend Katy Milkman.

02:17:38.030 --> 02:17:41.199
She's a professor at Wharton at
the University of Pennsylvania.

02:17:41.660 --> 02:17:46.110
So what she's found is that in our
lives, we have these big milestone

02:17:46.110 --> 02:17:50.450
moments where we break from the past
and we're entering a new future.

02:17:50.760 --> 02:17:53.510
This might be moving across the country.

02:17:53.530 --> 02:17:54.740
It could be getting a new job.

02:17:54.879 --> 02:17:56.110
It could be getting married.

02:17:56.129 --> 02:17:57.999
It could be whatever, okay?

02:17:58.190 --> 02:18:02.439
But one, it feels like a big change,
and that's a wonderful moment to try

02:18:02.440 --> 02:18:07.110
to introduce a new set of patterns
into your life, in part because

02:18:07.299 --> 02:18:10.270
again, you have a break in identity.

02:18:10.879 --> 02:18:14.909
But two, it's really easy to introduce
new habits when a lot of your

02:18:14.910 --> 02:18:17.239
environmental circumstances are different.

02:18:17.250 --> 02:18:18.980
So I take a new job.

02:18:19.150 --> 02:18:20.800
All of a sudden, I have
a new route to work.

02:18:21.049 --> 02:18:24.510
Probably a good idea to not introduce
a pastry stop every time I go to

02:18:24.510 --> 02:18:27.999
work, because I no longer am passing
by that bakery every morning.

02:18:28.440 --> 02:18:30.889
So you want to capitalize on
fresh starts of that kind.

02:18:31.160 --> 02:18:36.169
There's also more arbitrary fresh starts
that exist for all of us, and this is in

02:18:36.170 --> 02:18:37.436
the form of the first day of the year.

02:18:37.436 --> 02:18:40.850
So, of course, New Year's resolutions,
the first day of spring, even the first

02:18:40.850 --> 02:18:44.789
day of the week, can be very motivating
because we all like clean slates.

02:18:45.260 --> 02:18:46.630
We like wiping away the past.

02:18:47.040 --> 02:18:51.090
We like embarking on a new
future that's clean of failure

02:18:51.209 --> 02:18:52.570
and stumbling and whatnot.

02:18:52.580 --> 02:18:54.320
And so that can be a
really powerful motivator.

02:18:55.039 --> 02:18:58.170
Andrew Huberman: I love these
suggestions because I do think

02:18:58.170 --> 02:18:59.700
that we like a clean start.

02:18:59.870 --> 02:19:01.070
There's something to that.

02:19:01.430 --> 02:19:02.230
Who knows why?

02:19:02.250 --> 02:19:05.339
But I think it's a universal trait.

02:19:06.290 --> 02:19:10.309
And perhaps shortening the time domain
over which we think about our goals

02:19:10.680 --> 02:19:12.279
and success and failure could help.

02:19:12.279 --> 02:19:16.139
Like if you just say the clean
start is this afternoon, because

02:19:16.139 --> 02:19:18.074
this morning didn't go so well.

02:19:18.230 --> 02:19:19.889
Maya Shankar: Yeah, y ou don't have to
surrender the whole week just because

02:19:20.000 --> 02:19:21.299
you messed up on a Monday morning.

02:19:21.320 --> 02:19:21.770
Andrew Huberman: That's right.

02:19:21.770 --> 02:19:22.090
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

02:19:22.139 --> 02:19:23.799
Andrew Huberman: I'm sensing
the perfectionist in you.

02:19:23.799 --> 02:19:25.570
And I know that it's a continuum.

02:19:25.770 --> 02:19:29.529
Some people don't, I don't want to say
suffer from perfectionism, because I

02:19:29.539 --> 02:19:34.380
think it's a great attribute in certain
domains and can be challenging in others.

02:19:34.400 --> 02:19:41.619
But I love the idea of having a
little bit of grace with one's goals.

02:19:42.280 --> 02:19:48.489
And also what you said earlier of making
the carrot compelling and not so much

02:19:48.700 --> 02:19:53.600
focusing on just the stick, making the
carrot more compelling, so much there.

02:19:54.660 --> 02:19:55.910
What about the middle problem?

02:19:55.920 --> 02:20:01.950
Yeah, because I do think that people
do tend to go hard out the gate, as

02:20:01.950 --> 02:20:05.649
it were, and then people drop off.

02:20:05.740 --> 02:20:06.060
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

02:20:06.890 --> 02:20:10.600
So, yeah, all the stuff we talked about
so far has been around defining the goal.

02:20:10.600 --> 02:20:14.369
And now we need to think about how we
sustain our motivation to pursue the goal.

02:20:14.369 --> 02:20:15.739
And this can be super hard.

02:20:16.980 --> 02:20:20.369
Again, behavior change is
incredibly hard to sustain.

02:20:21.270 --> 02:20:23.309
So the middle problem.

02:20:23.330 --> 02:20:27.120
So the middle problem refers
to the fact that we don't have

02:20:27.220 --> 02:20:30.400
stable amounts of motivation
over the course of goal pursuit.

02:20:30.890 --> 02:20:33.710
We tend to have a boost in motivation
at the beginning of the pursuit.

02:20:33.800 --> 02:20:35.330
We all feel this viscerally, right?

02:20:35.330 --> 02:20:38.979
I've decided I'm going to do intermittent
fasting or I'm going to make sure I

02:20:38.980 --> 02:20:43.370
look at the sun every morning, the
first moment that I get up, or whatever

02:20:43.370 --> 02:20:46.900
the goal is on that first day, you
are so motivated to get it done right.

02:20:46.900 --> 02:20:52.150
In fact, the first few days, the first
few weeks, and then you experience a

02:20:52.150 --> 02:20:55.000
boost in motivation, a higher amount of
motivation towards the end of the goal.

02:20:55.480 --> 02:20:58.399
So we experience at the end of a goal
what's known as the goal gradient effect.

02:20:58.410 --> 02:21:02.490
So we tend to experience monotonic
increases in motivation the

02:21:02.490 --> 02:21:04.539
closer we are to the finish line.

02:21:04.820 --> 02:21:06.570
So we might even see it in
marathon runners, right?

02:21:06.570 --> 02:21:08.700
They're like, okay, I only
have this remaining part to go.

02:21:08.700 --> 02:21:11.730
I can expend all my energy now to
try to get over the finish line.

02:21:12.469 --> 02:21:16.920
There's a lull, though, in motivation
in the middle of goal pursuit, and

02:21:16.920 --> 02:21:18.900
that's something that we want to
get ahead of, we want to solve.

02:21:18.910 --> 02:21:22.330
For now, obviously, we
cannot eliminate middles.

02:21:22.640 --> 02:21:24.740
Mathematically impossible
to eliminate middles.

02:21:24.750 --> 02:21:25.490
So what do we do?

02:21:25.750 --> 02:21:28.359
Well, we do something that you already
alluded to, which is actually we

02:21:28.360 --> 02:21:30.780
shorten the time duration of our goals.

02:21:31.299 --> 02:21:33.974
So rather than setting an annual
goal, right, let's say that it's

02:21:34.049 --> 02:21:37.980
the new year, you're inspired to
try to make 2023 the best year ever.

02:21:38.490 --> 02:21:41.780
But the problem with that is when you
set an annual goal now, your middle

02:21:41.780 --> 02:21:45.429
is months long, so you're going to
experience that decrease in motivation

02:21:45.460 --> 02:21:49.580
for a healthy chunk of the year, which
is not ideal if you set a weekly goal.

02:21:49.590 --> 02:21:53.170
By contrast, all of a sudden, your
middle is a lot shorter, right?

02:21:53.879 --> 02:21:57.390
All of a sudden you're dealing with
like a few days, maybe a day or two.

02:21:57.800 --> 02:22:02.330
And so you want to be mindful
of the duration of the goal.

02:22:02.559 --> 02:22:08.112
Another thing that can help keep
motivation high is to do what my friend

02:22:08.112 --> 02:22:09.690
Katy Milkman calls temptation bundling.

02:22:09.969 --> 02:22:14.360
So this has, number one, been my
go to strategy for having done

02:22:14.420 --> 02:22:17.509
every unpleasant activity in my
life that I've had to do, okay?

02:22:18.060 --> 02:22:19.949
Folding laundry, doing the dishes.

02:22:19.970 --> 02:22:22.570
I actually really like working out
like you do, so I don't need as much

02:22:22.570 --> 02:22:25.660
motivation, but sometimes I still
need that for high intensity days.

02:22:25.660 --> 02:22:28.219
I do need the motivation to
do, like, the hard cardio.

02:22:28.219 --> 02:22:32.620
So to get on into working out in that way.

02:22:33.560 --> 02:22:34.820
So what is temptation bundling?

02:22:35.040 --> 02:22:39.119
You're pairing an unpleasant activity
like folding laundry, doing dishes,

02:22:39.120 --> 02:22:44.759
taking out the trash, with an immediately
rewarding, enjoyable activity that can

02:22:44.759 --> 02:22:48.279
be listening to your favorite podcasts,
which are, of course, the Huberman

02:22:48.279 --> 02:22:49.750
Lab, and A Slight Change of Plans

02:22:49.840 --> 02:22:55.090
.
 Obviously, it could be listening to
your favorite pop music, but the really

02:22:55.090 --> 02:22:59.329
critical piece of the temptation bundling
is that you have to forgo the indulgence

02:22:59.660 --> 02:23:03.679
of enjoying that rewarding activity
in all other spaces of your life.

02:23:04.440 --> 02:23:08.260
So, for example, for me, I
feel like a good pop song.

02:23:08.280 --> 02:23:12.510
I have, like, 25 really good listens,
and then it kind of becomes old hat.

02:23:12.620 --> 02:23:15.830
So just, like, the excitement
of the song wears off a bit.

02:23:16.040 --> 02:23:18.390
So there have been times where I'll
be, like, cooking with my husband,

02:23:18.740 --> 02:23:22.779
and he's like, hey, why don't we
play, you love Casey Musgraves,

02:23:22.779 --> 02:23:23.920
why don't we play that album?

02:23:23.949 --> 02:23:27.015
And I'm like, no, that's an
album I can only listen to when

02:23:27.170 --> 02:23:28.570
I'm, like, lifting weights.

02:23:28.620 --> 02:23:29.920
Andrew Huberman: Maintain the potency.

02:23:30.380 --> 02:23:31.740
Maya Shankar: You have to
maintain the potency, right?

02:23:31.920 --> 02:23:35.910
You don't allow yourself to get the joy
and edification of the Huberman Lab when

02:23:35.910 --> 02:23:39.040
you're not taking a walk and getting
exposure to that morning sunlight.

02:23:39.059 --> 02:23:42.279
And it's such a simple strategy
when you think about it.

02:23:42.550 --> 02:23:48.275
But I have found myself looking forward
to really annoying tasks that I have

02:23:48.410 --> 02:23:51.509
to get done because I know I'm going
to get the enjoyment of something

02:23:51.510 --> 02:23:53.020
really fun that accompanies it.

02:23:53.630 --> 02:23:54.240
Andrew Huberman: Fantastic.

02:23:54.270 --> 02:23:58.880
Is it important that the thing that
one enjoys be done simultaneously?

02:23:58.889 --> 02:24:04.030
So folding laundry while watching
the Netflix thing or listening

02:24:04.030 --> 02:24:05.200
to a particular piece of music?

02:24:05.370 --> 02:24:07.099
Maya Shankar: Yeah, you want
them to coexist, because then

02:24:07.100 --> 02:24:08.120
again, you get that immediately.

02:24:08.500 --> 02:24:10.920
Most of the time, the things
that we lament doing have really

02:24:10.920 --> 02:24:12.859
positive long term outcomes.

02:24:13.469 --> 02:24:17.180
If I'm in the habit of keeping my house
clean, there's long term benefits.

02:24:17.180 --> 02:24:19.220
If I'm in the habit of
exercising or eating healthily,

02:24:19.220 --> 02:24:20.150
there's long term benefits.

02:24:20.510 --> 02:24:23.630
But I don't often feel
the rewards in real time.

02:24:23.930 --> 02:24:27.019
So what you're trying to do is
give yourself that rush of joy and

02:24:27.020 --> 02:24:30.025
excitement that accompanies the
immediately rewarding activity so

02:24:30.130 --> 02:24:34.260
that in your mind, even just, like
neurally, the two things are coexisting.

02:24:34.980 --> 02:24:37.199
Andrew Huberman: I love it because
it has such firm grounding in the

02:24:37.300 --> 02:24:40.940
neurobiology of reward and aversion
and how to overcome aversion.

02:24:42.130 --> 02:24:45.690
There's deep neuroscience around this, but
I've never heard it presented that way.

02:24:45.690 --> 02:24:51.329
So thank you for those incredibly clear
and actionable tools for motivation,

02:24:51.330 --> 02:24:52.989
because so many people struggle with that.

02:24:53.119 --> 02:24:53.549
Maya Shankar: Yeah.

02:24:53.719 --> 02:24:54.910
Andrew Huberman: And I
hear that all the time.

02:24:54.990 --> 02:24:56.670
Maya Shankar: And I think
you talked about aversion.

02:24:56.670 --> 02:24:57.890
And actually, this is really important.

02:24:58.140 --> 02:25:02.250
So when we think about returning to our
goals, which is often the hard thing.

02:25:02.250 --> 02:25:05.560
So you do it on a Monday, and you have
that same goal on a Tuesday, and then

02:25:05.560 --> 02:25:08.070
on a Wednesday, and on Thursday, and by
Thursday, you're kind of like, oh, my

02:25:08.070 --> 02:25:10.220
God, it was so hard the first few days.

02:25:10.220 --> 02:25:13.720
Do I really want to go back and
do the same workout on a Thursday?

02:25:14.950 --> 02:25:21.090
What's really helpful here to avoid some
of that aversion is to be mindful of the

02:25:21.090 --> 02:25:23.220
way in which our minds process memories.

02:25:23.230 --> 02:25:28.539
So when we reflect back on how much we
enjoyed or didn't enjoy an experience, we

02:25:28.539 --> 02:25:30.539
don't give equal weight to every moment.

02:25:31.369 --> 02:25:32.720
Each moment doesn't get uniform weight.

02:25:33.470 --> 02:25:39.240
Instead, we tend to give more weight to
what's called the peak of the experience.

02:25:39.250 --> 02:25:42.582
So the experience that was most
emotionally intense, the part of the

02:25:42.582 --> 02:25:46.010
experience that was the most emotionally
intense, and the end of the experience.

02:25:46.020 --> 02:25:51.350
So this is work done by Nobel
laureate Daniel Kahneman and

02:25:51.360 --> 02:25:52.839
his collaborator, Amos Tversky.

02:25:52.839 --> 02:25:55.180
So the peak end rule
is what this is called.

02:25:55.180 --> 02:25:57.960
So you put a lot of weight on, again,
that really emotionally intense

02:25:57.960 --> 02:25:59.360
moment of the experience and the end.

02:26:00.200 --> 02:26:04.349
Now, researchers have studied this in the
context of lots of unpleasant activities.

02:26:04.779 --> 02:26:10.410
So in some studies, people are forced to
submerge their hands in ice cold water,

02:26:10.590 --> 02:26:13.980
or they looked at colonoscopies, for
example, and how unpleasant those are.

02:26:14.790 --> 02:26:18.850
And what they found is that
this is so interesting.

02:26:18.860 --> 02:26:23.730
Okay, I'm nerding out a little bit because
I just think that this field is so cool.

02:26:23.939 --> 02:26:26.840
Andrew Huberman: Nerding
out isn't just tolerated.

02:26:26.850 --> 02:26:28.883
It is encouraged on this podcast,

02:26:29.040 --> 02:26:30.920
Maya Shankar: I'm having a
moment with cognitive science.

02:26:31.870 --> 02:26:33.579
But this is such cool
research, because what these

02:26:33.580 --> 02:26:34.919
researchers did, it's so clever.

02:26:35.920 --> 02:26:41.040
If you elongate the unpleasant experience
by a couple of minutes, let's say, so,

02:26:41.130 --> 02:26:46.600
the hands in freezing cold ice water or
the colonoscopy, but you make those last

02:26:46.600 --> 02:26:51.730
few minutes of the unpleasant experience
slightly less unpleasant than the end of

02:26:51.820 --> 02:26:53.070
the experience would otherwise have been.

02:26:53.240 --> 02:26:56.510
Right, had you just ended the colonoscopy
procedure as planned, had you just taken

02:26:57.210 --> 02:27:01.599
the hands right out of the ice bucket by,
for example, increasing the temperature

02:27:01.600 --> 02:27:07.939
of the water by a degree, or use your
imagination, whatever the equivalent.

02:27:07.990 --> 02:27:08.852
Andrew Huberman: How can
you make a colonoscopy less?

02:27:08.852 --> 02:27:11.759
Maya Shankar: [LAUGHS] There are
mechanisms by which the pain can be less.

02:27:11.880 --> 02:27:13.520
Andrew Huberman: [LAUGHS]
Physicians everywhere know them,

02:27:13.520 --> 02:27:16.270
but we are oblivious to them.

02:27:17.109 --> 02:27:19.980
Maya Shankar: Anyway, you guys can
do the mental work of figuring out

02:27:21.910 --> 02:27:22.552
what the equivalent is on Google.

02:27:22.700 --> 02:27:27.620
What they find is that people look
back on the experience more favorably.

02:27:27.679 --> 02:27:30.000
They have a more positive
impression of the experience.

02:27:31.509 --> 02:27:34.370
Now, again, this is what's so
miraculous about this finding.

02:27:34.639 --> 02:27:39.120
The overall duration of the unpleasant
experience has been extended.

02:27:39.120 --> 02:27:42.870
There are more minutes of
overall suffering, right?

02:27:43.540 --> 02:27:46.879
But the last few minutes are less bad
than they would have been otherwise.

02:27:47.240 --> 02:27:50.530
And so people are, they view
the experience more favorably.

02:27:50.540 --> 02:27:52.900
In the case of the colonoscopies,
they were actually more likely

02:27:52.900 --> 02:27:56.100
to return for follow up visits,
for their annual checkups.

02:27:56.740 --> 02:27:58.560
And so what does this mean in daily life?

02:27:58.560 --> 02:28:03.490
Well, what it can mean is, let's say you
are literally killing yourself at the gym.

02:28:03.490 --> 02:28:05.750
You have the hardest workout
that you've ever had.

02:28:06.620 --> 02:28:10.569
Tack on a few minutes to the end of
the workout that are still unpleasant,

02:28:10.570 --> 02:28:14.380
so you're still coding them as being
part of the unpleasant working out

02:28:14.380 --> 02:28:18.900
experience, but are a little bit less
intense and less painful than the

02:28:18.900 --> 02:28:22.100
workout end would have been otherwise,
you might be more likely to return

02:28:22.219 --> 02:28:23.660
and actually do the hard workout.

02:28:24.170 --> 02:28:26.259
Andrew Huberman: Can we also
say if somebody really enjoys

02:28:26.429 --> 02:28:29.709
their training, that the opposite
would be effective as well?

02:28:29.740 --> 02:28:33.410
That perhaps if they really want to push
it hard at the end, because that's the

02:28:33.410 --> 02:28:37.290
sensation that they particularly enjoy,
that that could serve, presumably, the

02:28:37.290 --> 02:28:40.419
memory systems and the reward systems
of the brain such that they are more

02:28:40.420 --> 02:28:41.990
likely to return to the workout again.

02:28:42.000 --> 02:28:42.380
Maya Shankar: Absolutely.

02:28:42.389 --> 02:28:46.389
You raise a fantastic point, which
is when we talk about enjoyment in

02:28:46.389 --> 02:28:48.160
these contexts, it is all subjective.

02:28:48.750 --> 02:28:51.510
So I actually kind of love the
feeling like, I'm going to die,

02:28:52.000 --> 02:28:53.960
because my heart is racing.

02:28:53.960 --> 02:28:56.760
So, I mean, for whatever reason, I'm
just wired to love exercise, right.

02:28:57.080 --> 02:28:59.970
And I love a heart
strength training workout.

02:29:00.259 --> 02:29:05.910
And so for me, what enjoyment might
look like at the end is really intense.

02:29:06.139 --> 02:29:06.389
Right.

02:29:06.400 --> 02:29:07.670
That might be what brings me back.

02:29:07.670 --> 02:29:12.359
But in other domains, absolutely
not, like the colonoscopy situation.

02:29:12.360 --> 02:29:14.539
I do not want that to be
an unpleasant experience.

02:29:14.539 --> 02:29:18.060
And so there are lots of other domains
in life where if you just tack on a

02:29:18.060 --> 02:29:21.570
few of the few minutes onto something
that's really tedious or really hard

02:29:21.570 --> 02:29:24.619
or really painful, it can make you
more likely to commit to it later.

02:29:24.650 --> 02:29:25.760
But it's an excellent point.

02:29:25.760 --> 02:29:30.090
In all of these studies, you have to
consider who the person is and what

02:29:30.090 --> 02:29:31.590
their natural psychology is like.

02:29:31.590 --> 02:29:34.399
And for everyone listening, you
want to tailor these recommendations

02:29:34.559 --> 02:29:35.740
to who you are as a person.

02:29:36.280 --> 02:29:38.390
Andrew Huberman: Well, there are
certain life demands that I find

02:29:38.390 --> 02:29:41.920
incredibly aversive, so I'm going
to use this approach for those.

02:29:41.959 --> 02:29:45.610
I'm also going to use them in the
context of things I really enjoy.

02:29:45.920 --> 02:29:51.000
Because if one has the opportunity, I
believe, to further reinforce the things

02:29:51.000 --> 02:29:52.910
that bring us joy, why wouldn't we?

02:29:52.910 --> 02:29:53.470
Maya Shankar: Absolutely.

02:29:54.200 --> 02:29:55.629
Andrew Huberman:
Fantastic recommendations.

02:29:56.299 --> 02:30:00.270
Listen, I could ask you 1,000
more questions, and my hope

02:30:00.270 --> 02:30:01.210
is that you'll come back--

02:30:01.289 --> 02:30:01.359
Maya Shankar: --I'd love to--

02:30:01.359 --> 02:30:04.519
Andrew Huberman: --so that I can
ask you those 1,000+ more questions.

02:30:04.870 --> 02:30:09.890
I have to say, it is exceedingly rare
that I talk to somebody either on

02:30:09.890 --> 02:30:14.480
the podcast or elsewhere, frankly, in
